<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Iván Area
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">I. Area</span>IFCAE, Universidade de Vigo, Departamento de Matemática Aplicada II, E. E. Aeronáutica e do Espazo, Campus As Lagoas s/n, 32004 Ourense, Spain
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address, I. Area : </span><a href="mailto:area@uvigo.gal">area@uvigo.gal</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The large-degree phase of the Jacobi polynomial
<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(\cos\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(\cos\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> contains, at each order in
<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=n+(\alpha+\beta+1)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=n+(\alpha+\beta+1)/2</annotation></semantics></math>, an additive constant <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>
that the phase equation does not determine. These constants are antisymmetric in
<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha^{2},\beta^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha^{2},\beta^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and vanish for <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. We obtain three
results. First, on the diagonal <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta</annotation></semantics></math> the logarithm of the
midpoint amplitude is computed in closed form: if <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the
coefficient of <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\mathcal{N})^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\mathcal{N})^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> in that logarithm, then
<math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}L_{n}t^{n}/(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}L_{n}t^{n}/(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math> equals</p>
    
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{\sinh 2t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{\sinh 2t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">The proof is algebraic: the identity is established for half-odd-integer
<math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, where the expansion terminates, by means of two classical
recurrences for Bessel polynomials, and is extended to all <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by
a degree argument. Second, the derivative of the logarithm of the
amplitude with respect to <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at the Legendre point
<math id="abstract1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> is computed in closed form to all orders. An exact reduction of an Appell
<math id="abstract1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> function to a Gauss function shows that it is a parameter
derivative of a zero-balanced <math id="abstract1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at argument <math id="abstract1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>; the real part
of its expansion is a trigamma series, and the imaginary part is the
unique formal solution of an explicit central-difference equation. Third, we
deduce that the coefficients <math id="abstract1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of an explicit even power series
attached to the imaginary part satisfy
<math id="abstract1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="abstract1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="abstract1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="abstract1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic
valuation). This determines, for
every <math id="abstract1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact power of <math id="abstract1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in the denominator of the coefficient
of <math id="abstract1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the polynomial that represents <math id="abstract1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also give a factorisation of the
amplitude into products of pairs of Gauss functions, further valuation
formulas, and a numerical illustration for the zeros.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Jacobi polynomials; ultraspherical polynomials; Appell functions; Bessel polynomials; Borel transform; 2-adic valuation
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">2020 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>Primary 33C45; Secondary 33C05, 33C65, 41A60, 11S80
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the standard normalisation and notation for Jacobi polynomials
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>; general background on hypergeometric and
classical special functions may be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
For <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math> the Jacobi polynomial <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> is
orthogonal on <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the weight
<math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-x)^{\alpha}(1+x)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-x)^{\alpha}(1+x)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> zeros are real, simple and lie
in <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>; they are the nodes of Gauss–Jacobi quadrature. The
ultraspherical case <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta</annotation></semantics></math> contains the Legendre polynomials
(<math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math>), the Chebyshev polynomials
(<math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=\pm\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=\pm\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the Gegenbauer polynomials. Whenever
the zeros of <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> are considered as an ordered set in
<math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> (here and in Section <a href="#S11" title="11. Application: Jacobi zeros for {𝛼,𝛽}&gt;-1 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) we assume
<math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The formal and hypergeometric identities proved
below are polynomial or meromorphic in the parameters and, where
explicitly stated, hold beyond this range. Write the zeros as
<math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n,k}=\cos\theta_{n,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n,k}=\cos\theta_{n,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\theta_{n,1}&lt;\dots&lt;\theta_{n,n}&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\theta_{n,1}&lt;\dots&lt;\theta_{n,n}&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
and put</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=n+\frac{\alpha+\beta+1}{2},\qquad A=\alpha^{2},\qquad B=\beta^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=n+\frac{\alpha+\beta+1}{2},\qquad A=\alpha^{2},\qquad B=\beta^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The starting point is the elementary observation that the zeros are
almost equally spaced in the angle <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. For the Chebyshev
polynomials this is exact: the zeros of <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are
<math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos\bigl((k-\frac{1}{2})\pi/n\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos\bigl((k-\frac{1}{2})\pi/n\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>. In general one has, for the zeros
away from the endpoints,</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{n,k}=\frac{(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi+(\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{1}{4})\pi}{\mathcal{N}}+O(\mathcal{N}^{-2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{n,k}=\frac{(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi+(\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{1}{4})\pi}{\mathcal{N}}+O(\mathcal{N}^{-2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and the question addressed by the phase expansion is what the
corrections look like, to all orders in <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The source
of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is the Liouville normal form of the Jacobi
differential equation: the function
<math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u(\theta)=\sin^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}\!\frac{\theta}{2}\,\cos^{\beta+\frac{1}{2}}\!\frac{\theta}{2}\,P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(\cos\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u(\theta)=\sin^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}\!\frac{\theta}{2}\,\cos^{\beta+\frac{1}{2}}\!\frac{\theta}{2}\,P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(\cos\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{\prime\prime}(\theta)+\Bigl[\mathcal{N}^{2}+\frac{\frac{1}{4}-A}{4\sin^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}}+\frac{\frac{1}{4}-B}{4\cos^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}}\Bigr]u(\theta)=0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{\prime\prime}(\theta)+\Bigl[\mathcal{N}^{2}+\frac{\frac{1}{4}-A}{4\sin^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}}+\frac{\frac{1}{4}-B}{4\cos^{2}\frac{\theta}{2}}\Bigr]u(\theta)=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Eq. (4.24.2)]</cite>. For <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=\frac{1}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=\frac{1}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> this is the equation of
a pure sinusoid of frequency <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>; in general <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a sinusoid of
frequency <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> whose phase is slowly deformed by the potential, and
the zeros of <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the points where that phase
crosses the values <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the
equation (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) depends on the parameters only through
<math id="S1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>; this is why all the coefficients considered in this paper
are polynomials in <math id="S1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Solving the phase equation associated with (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)
recursively in powers of <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, one obtains a phase function</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(\theta)=\mathcal{N}\theta-\Bigl(\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{1}{4}\Bigr)\pi+\sum_{r\geq 1}\frac{\Psi_{r}(\theta)}{\mathcal{N}^{2r-1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(\theta)=\mathcal{N}\theta-\Bigl(\frac{\alpha}{2}+\frac{1}{4}\Bigr)\pi+\sum_{r\geq 1}\frac{\Psi_{r}(\theta)}{\mathcal{N}^{2r-1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">such that the zeros are, to all orders in <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the solutions of
<math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(\theta_{n,k})=(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(\theta_{n,k})=(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. The first term is elementary,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{1}(\theta)=\frac{(1-4B)\tan\frac{\theta}{2}-(1-4A)\cot\frac{\theta}{2}}{16}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{1}(\theta)=\frac{(1-4B)\tan\frac{\theta}{2}-(1-4A)\cot\frac{\theta}{2}}{16},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">and already very effective: for <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=20</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\frac{3}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\frac{3}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{1}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{1}{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximal error of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) on the ten
central zeros is <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.2\cdot 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2\cdot 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and adding the term
<math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{1}(\theta)/\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{1}(\theta)/\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces it to <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.9\cdot 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.9\cdot 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is
obtained from the recursion by an integration and is therefore
determined only up to an additive constant. We normalise <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to
be an odd Laurent polynomial in <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cot(\theta/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cot(\theta/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, without constant
term; the information not fixed by the differential equation is then
carried by the value at the midpoint,</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}=\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}=\Psi_{r}(\pi/2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">The numbers <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are polynomials in <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with
rational coefficients, are the only part of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)
that the phase equation does not produce. We call them the
<em id="S1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">phase constants</em>. The first three are</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}\kappa_{1}=\frac{A-B}{4},\qquad\kappa_{2}=\frac{(A-B)(2A+2B-7)}{96},\\
\kappa_{3}=\frac{(A-B)(28A^{2}+88AB+28B^{2}-380A-380B+781)}{7680}.\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>κ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>κ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>96</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>88</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>380</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>380</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>781</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>7680</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}\kappa_{1}=\frac{A-B}{4},\qquad\kappa_{2}=\frac{(A-B)(2A+2B-7)}{96},\\
\kappa_{3}=\frac{(A-B)(28A^{2}+88AB+28B^{2}-380A-380B+781)}{7680}.\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Two features of (<a href="#S1.E4" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) persist for all <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and motivate the
paper. The first is antisymmetry. Since
<math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(-x)=(-1)^{n}P_{n}^{(\beta,\alpha)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}(-x)=(-1)^{n}P_{n}^{(\beta,\alpha)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, reflection at
<math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math> interchanges <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and reverses the sign
of the phase, so</p>
<table id="S1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}(B,A)=-\kappa_{r}(A,B),\qquad\text{hence}\qquad(A-B)\mid\kappa_{r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext>hence</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}(B,A)=-\kappa_{r}(A,B),\qquad\text{hence}\qquad(A-B)\mid\kappa_{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In particular <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes identically on the diagonal <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B</annotation></semantics></math>,
which contains the ultraspherical case <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta</annotation></semantics></math>: the Legendre and
Gegenbauer cases need no phase constants. The nontrivial content
of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is therefore transverse to the diagonal. On
the diagonal itself the phase constants vanish but the series from
which they are extracted (the amplitude, introduced below) does not;
it is a nontrivial asymptotic series. Our first question is whether
this series can be computed in closed form. The second feature is
arithmetic: the denominators <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4=2^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4=2^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96=2^{5}\cdot 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96=2^{5}\cdot 3</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7680=2^{9}\cdot 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7680</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>9</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7680=2^{9}\cdot 15</annotation></semantics></math> and, for <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86016=2^{12}\cdot 21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86016</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86016=2^{12}\cdot 21</annotation></semantics></math> grow
mainly through powers of <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> it is
showed that the power of <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in the denominator of <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is at
most <math id="S1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{E_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{E_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes
the <math id="S1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic valuation. Our second question is whether this bound is
attained for every <math id="S1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Both questions are answered in this paper:
the diagonal series is the Stirling series of a quotient of Gamma
functions (Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>), and the bound <math id="S1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{E_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{E_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sharp for every
<math id="S1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>). The proof of the second statement
uses the first: the closed form on the diagonal is the background over
which the first-order variation transverse to the diagonal is
computed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Large-degree asymptotics for Jacobi polynomials and functions have a
long history, from the classical trigonometric and uniform expansions
of Hahn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and Frenzen–Wong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to
Riemann–Hilbert treatments of Jacobi-type weights
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, fast computation of Gauss–Jacobi
nodes and weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>,
nonoscillatory phase-function methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, explicit
error bounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and, closest to our starting
point, the inverse-factorial large-degree expansions of
Nemes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for Jacobi and related functions with fixed
parameters, which carry explicit computable error bounds, are built
from the same Hankel coefficients as (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) below, and terminate
at half-odd-integer parameters. These works develop the expansions,
their error bounds and the associated algorithms. The present paper
addresses a different question: the exact algebraic and arithmetic
structure of the coefficients of the phase expansion, and in particular
of the constants <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now state the main results, which are proved in the following
sections. Let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}(\nu)=\frac{(\frac{1}{2}+\nu)_{k}(\frac{1}{2}-\nu)_{k}}{(-2)^{k}k!}=\frac{\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\bigl(4\nu^{2}-(2j+1)^{2}\bigr)}{8^{k}k!}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}(\nu)=\frac{(\frac{1}{2}+\nu)_{k}(\frac{1}{2}-\nu)_{k}}{(-2)^{k}k!}=\frac{\prod_{j=0}^{k-1}\bigl(4\nu^{2}-(2j+1)^{2}\bigr)}{8^{k}k!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">be the Hankel coefficients, and put <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=2\mathcal{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=2\mathcal{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The large-degree
behaviour of the Jacobi polynomials at the midpoint <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math> is
carried by the inverse-factorial series</p>
<table id="S1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}F(Z)=\sum_{n\geq 0}\frac{G_{n}}{(Z-1)(Z-2)\cdots(Z-n)},\\
G_{n}=\sum_{l=0}^{n}a_{l}(\alpha)a_{n-l}(\beta)\,p^{\,l}q^{\,n-l},\qquad p=1+i,\quad q=1-i,\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msup><mi>q</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="4.167em">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}F(Z)=\sum_{n\geq 0}\frac{G_{n}}{(Z-1)(Z-2)\cdots(Z-n)},\\
G_{n}=\sum_{l=0}^{n}a_{l}(\alpha)a_{n-l}(\beta)\,p^{\,l}q^{\,n-l},\qquad p=1+i,\quad q=1-i,\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">which we call the <em id="S1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">amplitude</em>. Write
<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F=\sum_{n\geq 1}L_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F=\sum_{n\geq 1}L_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>; the coefficients <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomials
in <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The phase constants are the imaginary parts of the odd
coefficients,</p>
<table id="S1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}=2^{-(2r-1)}\operatorname{Im}L_{2r-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}=2^{-(2r-1)}\operatorname{Im}L_{2r-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">Formula (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is the definition of <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> used in
this paper; its agreement with the constants
<math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is discussed in
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem37" title="Proposition 7.37. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.37</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our first result concerns the diagonal <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the value of <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> there, and let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}}{(n-1)!}\,t^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}}{(n-1)!}\,t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">be the associated generating function. We write <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{k}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{k}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for the Bernoulli
polynomials and <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{k}=\mathcal{B}_{k}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{k}=\mathcal{B}_{k}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> for the Bernoulli numbers, so that
<math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{1}=-\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{1}=-\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{E}_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{E}_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Euler numbers. As usual,
<math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a)_{k}=a(a+1)\cdots(a+k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)_{k}=a(a+1)\cdots(a+k-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Pochhammer symbol,
<math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\Gamma^{\prime}/\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\Gamma^{\prime}/\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the digamma function, <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Li}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Li</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Li}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is
the polylogarithm, and <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x^{k}]f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x^{k}]f</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the coefficient of <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in a
power series <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{\sinh 2t}=\tfrac{1}{2}\bigl(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t\bigr)\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr),\qquad A=\alpha^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>csch</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{\sinh 2t}=\tfrac{1}{2}\bigl(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t\bigr)\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr),\qquad A=\alpha^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, coefficientwise,</span></p>
<table id="S1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}=\frac{4^{n}}{n(n+1)}\Bigl[\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}=\frac{4^{n}}{n(n+1)}\Bigl[\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The proof occupies Sections <a href="#S2" title="2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3" title="3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. It
does not use any asymptotic representation of the Jacobi polynomials.
The identity is first established for <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> a non-negative integer, where (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) terminates, by an
induction based on two classical recurrences for Bessel polynomials;
a degree bound in <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> then extends it to all <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On the diagonal, as an identity of formal Laurent series at <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(Z+4)\,Z(Z+2)=F(Z)\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(Z+4)\,Z(Z+2)=F(Z)\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(Z):=\Gamma(Z)F(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(Z):=\Gamma(Z)F(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies, in the same
formal sense,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(Z+4)=(Z+1)(Z+3)\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr)\mathcal{G}(Z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(Z+4)=(Z+1)(Z+3)\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr)\mathcal{G}(Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For half-odd-integer <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> the first identity is an identity of
rational functions of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and the second one of meromorphic functions,
by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}=\upsilon_{1}\cosh 2\alpha t+\upsilon_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>υ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>υ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}=\upsilon_{1}\cosh 2\alpha t+\upsilon_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with
</span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon_{0}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>υ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon_{0}(t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon_{1}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>υ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon_{1}(t)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> independent of </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;
coefficientwise, for </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
</span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A^{k}]\Upsilon_{A}=\frac{2^{2k-1}}{(2k)!}t^{2k-2}[A^{1}]\Upsilon_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A^{k}]\Upsilon_{A}=\frac{2^{2k-1}}{(2k)!}t^{2k-2}[A^{1}]\Upsilon_{A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{1/4}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{1/4}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: there is no correction in the
Chebyshev cases </span><math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=\pm\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=\pm\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the Legendre case,
</span><math id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{0}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{0}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\bigl(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The second group of results concerns the first-order variation of the
amplitude at the Legendre point <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{n}=\frac{\partial L_{n}}{\partial A}\Big|_{A=B=0}\in\mathbb{Q}(i),\qquad\Phi(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{g_{n}}{(n-1)!}\,t^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{n}=\frac{\partial L_{n}}{\partial A}\Big|_{A=B=0}\in\mathbb{Q}(i),\qquad\Phi(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{g_{n}}{(n-1)!}\,t^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), the coefficient of the monomial <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S1.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}=2^{-(2r-1)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\qquad(r\geq 1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}=2^{-(2r-1)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\qquad(r\geq 1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">We call the sequence <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, or its generating series
<math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S1.p9.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Legendre tangent</em> of the amplitude: it is
the derivative of <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F</annotation></semantics></math> in the direction of <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> at the Legendre
point. At the midpoint, the derivative of <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F</annotation></semantics></math> along the diagonal
is real and the derivative in the direction of <math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-B</annotation></semantics></math>, transverse to the
diagonal, is purely imaginary (Section <a href="#S9" title="9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). Thus
<math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the diagonal part of the tangent and
<math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> its transverse part. The real parts are
determined by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S1.E10" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\Phi(t)=\frac{t^{2}e^{t}}{\sinh 2t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\Phi(t)=\frac{t^{2}e^{t}}{\sinh 2t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">that is, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}g_{2k+1}=(1-2^{2k-1})\mathcal{B}_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}g_{2k+1}=(1-2^{2k-1})\mathcal{B}_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}g_{2k}=\frac{1}{2}(2k-1)\mathsf{E}_{2k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}g_{2k}=\frac{1}{2}(2k-1)\mathsf{E}_{2k-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\operatorname{Re}g_{n})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\operatorname{Re}g_{n})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Here and below <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ring
of <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic integers, <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> its group of units, and <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> the
number of digits <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in the binary expansion of <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(m!)=m-s_{2}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(m!)=m-s_{2}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. The imaginary parts of the <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the
substantial part. Put</p>
<table id="S1.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{t^{2}}\,\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t),\qquad w_{m}=[t^{2m}]\,W(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{t^{2}}\,\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t),\qquad w_{m}=[t^{2m}]\,W(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">so that, by Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>,
<math id="S1.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sinh(2t)\,\Phi(t)/t^{2}=e^{t}+i\,W(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sinh(2t)\,\Phi(t)/t^{2}=e^{t}+i\,W(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{r}=r+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr),\qquad E_{r}=(2r-1)+H_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{r}=r+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr),\qquad E_{r}=(2r-1)+H_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is an even
power series. For every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\,\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr),\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad\bigl((2m)!\bigr)^{2}w_{m}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">that is,</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\,\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr),\qquad\text{that is,}\qquad\bigl((2m)!\bigr)^{2}w_{m}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, for every <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\bigr)=-H_{r},\qquad\nu_{2}\bigl([A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}\bigr)=-E_{r}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\bigr)=-H_{r},\qquad\nu_{2}\bigl([A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}\bigr)=-E_{r},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> is proved in Subsection <a href="#S7.SS7" title="7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>. The
equivalence between the statement for <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and the statement for
<math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> was established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, where the
property <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((2m)!)^{2}w_{m}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((2m)!)^{2}w_{m}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> was verified in a finite range;
the argument is repeated in the proof of
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a>. The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> rests
on a closed form for the whole sequence <math id="S1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As formal series in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}\,Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+3}{4}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{i}{2}\,\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}\,Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+3}{4}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{i}{2}\,\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where each trigamma function <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for its asymptotic
(Bernoulli) expansion, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique formal
series without constant term that solves the central-difference
equation</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s+1)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s-1)=-\frac{(2s-1)}{4s(s-1)}\left[\frac{\Gamma(s/2)}{\Gamma((1+s)/2)}\right]^{2},\qquad s=\frac{1-Z}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s+1)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s-1)=-\frac{(2s-1)}{4s(s-1)}\left[\frac{\Gamma(s/2)}{\Gamma((1+s)/2)}\right]^{2},\qquad s=\frac{1-Z}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the right-hand side being replaced by its asymptotic expansion in
powers of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular</span></p>
<table id="S1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{n}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\,\widehat{q}_{n}\qquad(n\geq 1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{n}=-\tfrac{1}{2}\,\widehat{q}_{n}\qquad(n\geq 1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{q}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{q}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coefficient of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> proceeds as follows. The
amplitude (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is a Kampé de Fériet double series
(Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>), in fact an Appell <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> function evaluated on a
curve on which it reduces to a single Gauss function
(Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 7.3. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>). Consequently
<math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the expansion of a second parameter derivative of
a zero-balanced <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> at the boundary value <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+i0</annotation></semantics></math>
(Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 7.4. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>). The classical connection formula
splits this boundary value into two blocks which are quotients of Gamma
functions at the Legendre point (Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>); the
variation of one of them satisfies a first-order difference equation
which is solved by quadratures (Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.9</span></a>); and the
passage from the resulting convergent expressions to the formal series
in <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is made by an asymptotic matching along a vertical line
(Subsection <a href="#S7.SS6" title="7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>), whose estimates are given in
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> then follows from
the <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic analysis of the explicit series <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Subsection <a href="#S7.SS7" title="7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>): the exponent of the Gamma quotient
has Genocchi-number coefficients, and in the expansion of its
exponential exactly one term has minimal valuation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Besides these results the paper contains further exact structure of
the amplitude. The recurrences for Bessel polynomials used in the
proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> do not require the special relation
<math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{2}+q^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{2}+q^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> satisfied at the midpoint (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>); on
the whole curve described by the weights <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> varies,
where <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=pq</annotation></semantics></math>, the half-odd-integer amplitudes satisfy a three-term
contiguity whose numerators are given in closed form in terms of ballot
numbers (Theorems <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>), and the
derivatives of all orders normal to that curve are determined
recursively (Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 6.4. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>). For independent
half-odd-integer parameters <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> the amplitudes satisfy a
similar recurrence off the diagonal (Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>). The
amplitude is an explicit sum of products of pairs of Gauss functions,
the <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-th term being of order <math id="S1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-3r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-3r}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Corollary <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 8.2. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a>); from this expansion we derive four
valuation formulas for the numerator and the denominator of
<math id="S1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 10.3. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a> and
Proposition <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 10.4. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a>). Section <a href="#S11" title="11. Application: Jacobi zeros for {𝛼,𝛽}&gt;-1 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> illustrates the
use of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) for the numerical computation of zeros.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Two statements of the paper are not proved in full generality, and
neither is used in the proofs of Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>,
<a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>. The first is the agreement
of (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with the constants <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math>
of (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which is proved for <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>
(Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem37" title="Proposition 7.37. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.37</span></a>); for <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;8</annotation></semantics></math> the last assertion of
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> is a statement about the polynomials defined
by (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). The second is a periodicity hypothesis under
which the function whose expansion is <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated in
closed form on the real axis (Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem28" title="Proposition 7.28. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.28</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the paper, identities in <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently in
<math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> or in <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(1-Z)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(1-Z)/2</annotation></semantics></math>) are identities of formal
Laurent series unless convergence is explicitly asserted. When a
meromorphic function is introduced, its expansion for large <math id="S1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is
always understood in the stated sector. For half-odd-integer
parameters the identities are identities of rational functions or of
quotients of Gamma functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The paper is organised as follows. Sections <a href="#S2" title="2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
and <a href="#S3" title="3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and its
corollaries. Sections <a href="#S4" title="4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S5" title="5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
and <a href="#S6" title="6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> treat the half-odd-integer amplitudes away
from the midpoint. Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> contains the proofs of
Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>: the Appell reduction
(Subsection <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>), the boundary split and the quadratures
(Subsections <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> and <a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>), the asymptotic
matching (Subsection <a href="#S7.SS6" title="7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>) and the arithmetic
(Subsection <a href="#S7.SS7" title="7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>); Subsections <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>
and <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> evaluate the boundary value exactly on the real
axis and are used only in Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem28" title="Proposition 7.28. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.28</span></a>. The proofs
of Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> are contained in
Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>; apart from
the notation of the Introduction and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, they do
not depend on Sections <a href="#S4" title="4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>–<a href="#S6" title="6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
or <a href="#S8" title="8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>–<a href="#S11" title="11. Application: Jacobi zeros for {𝛼,𝛽}&gt;-1 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.
Section <a href="#S8" title="8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> gives the expansion of the amplitude into
products of Gauss functions, Section <a href="#S9" title="9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> the linear
system satisfied by the transverse variation, and
Section <a href="#S10" title="10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> the valuation formulas.
Section <a href="#S11" title="11. Application: Jacobi zeros for {𝛼,𝛽}&gt;-1 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> contains the numerical illustration, and
Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> the estimates used in
Subsection <a href="#S7.SS6" title="7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>The half-odd-integer family</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Bessel polynomials at the midpoint</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for the Bessel polynomials</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\frac{(n+k)!}{(n-k)!\,k!\,2^{k}}\,x^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n}(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\frac{(n+k)!}{(n-k)!\,k!\,2^{k}}\,x^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}\bigl(\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)=\frac{(\ell+k)!}{(\ell-k)!\,k!\,2^{k}}\quad(0\leq k\leq\ell),\qquad a_{k}\bigl(\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)=0\quad(k&gt;\ell)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}\bigl(\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)=\frac{(\ell+k)!}{(\ell-k)!\,k!\,2^{k}}\quad(0\leq k\leq\ell),\qquad a_{k}\bigl(\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)=0\quad(k&gt;\ell),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}a_{k}(\ell+\frac{1}{2})\xi^{k}=y_{\ell}(\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}a_{k}(\ell+\frac{1}{2})\xi^{k}=y_{\ell}(\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\alpha^{2}=(2\ell+1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\alpha^{2}=(2\ell+1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the product in the definition of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j&lt;k}\bigl((2\ell+1)^{2}-(2j+1)^{2}\bigr)=\prod_{j&lt;k}(2\ell-2j)(2\ell+2j+2)=4^{k}\frac{\ell!}{(\ell-k)!}\frac{(\ell+k)!}{\ell!}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j&lt;k}\bigl((2\ell+1)^{2}-(2j+1)^{2}\bigr)=\prod_{j&lt;k}(2\ell-2j)(2\ell+2j+2)=4^{k}\frac{\ell!}{(\ell-k)!}\frac{(\ell+k)!}{\ell!}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and it vanishes for <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;\ell</annotation></semantics></math>. Dividing by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8^{k}k!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8^{k}k!</annotation></semantics></math> gives
the stated value.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> for the series (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) on the diagonal at
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}^{(\ell)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}^{(\ell)}</annotation></semantics></math> for its coefficients. By
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Bessel polynomials at the midpoint ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, the generating function of these coefficients
is the <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">polynomial</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\ell}(\xi):=\sum_{n}G_{n}^{(\ell)}\xi^{n}=y_{\ell}(p\xi)\,y_{\ell}(q\xi),\qquad p=1+i,\ q=1-i," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\ell}(\xi):=\sum_{n}G_{n}^{(\ell)}\xi^{n}=y_{\ell}(p\xi)\,y_{\ell}(q\xi),\qquad p=1+i,\ q=1-i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}^{(\ell)}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}^{(\ell)}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational function of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The four relations we use among the midpoint constants are</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq=2,\qquad p+q=2,\qquad p^{2}+q^{2}=0,\qquad\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq=2,\qquad p+q=2,\qquad p^{2}+q^{2}=0,\qquad\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The third relation is the one used in the proof of
Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Two classical recurrences, and their consequence</h3>

<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with the convention <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{-1}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{-1}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{(a)}\quad x^{2}y_{n}^{\prime}(x)=(nx-1)y_{n}(x)+y_{n-1}(x),\qquad\text{(b)}\quad y_{n+1}(x)=(2n+1)x\,y_{n}(x)+y_{n-1}(x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(a)</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(b)</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{(a)}\quad x^{2}y_{n}^{\prime}(x)=(nx-1)y_{n}(x)+y_{n-1}(x),\qquad\text{(b)}\quad y_{n+1}(x)=(2n+1)x\,y_{n}(x)+y_{n-1}(x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Both identities follow by comparing coefficients in the defining sum;
they are classical <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definitionx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{\ell}(\xi)=\tfrac{1}{2}\Bigl(q\,y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{\ell}(q\xi)+p\,y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{\ell-1}(q\xi)\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{\ell}(\xi)=\tfrac{1}{2}\Bigl(q\,y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{\ell}(q\xi)+p\,y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{\ell-1}(q\xi)\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is real because the two terms are complex conjugate.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}(\xi)+\bigl(1-2\ell\xi\bigr)\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=C_{\ell}(\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}(\xi)+\bigl(1-2\ell\xi\bigr)\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=C_{\ell}(\xi)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Put <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=y_{\ell}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=y_{\ell}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=y_{\ell}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=y_{\ell}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(a) at <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=p\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=p\xi</annotation></semantics></math> reads <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\,\xi^{2}U^{\prime}=(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\,\xi^{2}U^{\prime}=(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}=\xi^{2}(U^{\prime}V+UV^{\prime})=\frac{(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}}{p}\,V+U\,\frac{(\ell q\xi-1)V+V_{-}}{q}\\
=\Bigl(2\ell\xi-\frac{1}{p}-\frac{1}{q}\Bigr)UV+\frac{U_{-}V}{p}+\frac{UV_{-}}{q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}=\xi^{2}(U^{\prime}V+UV^{\prime})=\frac{(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}}{p}\,V+U\,\frac{(\ell q\xi-1)V+V_{-}}{q}\\
=\Bigl(2\ell\xi-\frac{1}{p}-\frac{1}{q}\Bigr)UV+\frac{U_{-}V}{p}+\frac{UV_{-}}{q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">By (<a href="#S2.E15" title="In 2.1. Bessel polynomials at the midpoint ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{p}=\frac{q}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{p}=\frac{q}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{q}=\frac{p}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{q}=\frac{p}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the right-hand side is <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell\xi-1)\Pi_{\ell}+C_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell\xi-1)\Pi_{\ell}+C_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell+2}=C_{\ell}+(4\ell+2)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell+2}=C_{\ell}+(4\ell+2)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">With <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{++}=y_{\ell+2}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{++}=y_{\ell+2}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> and similarly for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(b) gives <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{++}=(2\ell+3)p\xi U_{+}+U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{++}=(2\ell+3)p\xi U_{+}+U</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{++}=(2\ell+3)q\xi V_{+}+V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{++}=(2\ell+3)q\xi V_{+}+V</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2C_{\ell+2}=qU_{+}V_{++}+pU_{++}V_{+}=(2\ell+3)\xi\,(p^{2}+q^{2})\,U_{+}V_{+}\;+\;qU_{+}V+pUV_{+}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2C_{\ell+2}=qU_{+}V_{++}+pU_{++}V_{+}=(2\ell+3)\xi\,(p^{2}+q^{2})\,U_{+}V_{+}\;+\;qU_{+}V+pUV_{+},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the first term vanishes by (<a href="#S2.E15" title="In 2.1. Bessel polynomials at the midpoint ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). Applying
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(b) once more, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{+}=(2\ell+1)p\xi U+U_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{+}=(2\ell+1)p\xi U+U_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{+}=(2\ell+1)q\xi V+V_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{+}=(2\ell+1)q\xi V+V_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="qU_{+}V+pUV_{+}=2(2\ell+1)pq\,\xi\,UV+\bigl(qU_{-}V+pUV_{-}\bigr)=4(2\ell+1)\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}+2C_{\ell}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qU_{+}V+pUV_{+}=2(2\ell+1)pq\,\xi\,UV+\bigl(qU_{-}V+pUV_{-}\bigr)=4(2\ell+1)\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}+2C_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">using <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Dividing by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> gives the claim.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell+2}^{\prime}+\bigl[1-(2\ell+4)\xi\bigr]\Pi_{\ell+2}=\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}+\bigl[1+(2\ell+2)\xi\bigr]\Pi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell+2}^{\prime}+\bigl[1-(2\ell+4)\xi\bigr]\Pi_{\ell+2}=\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}+\bigl[1+(2\ell+2)\xi\bigr]\Pi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> the left side is <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell+2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the right
side is <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\ell}+(4\ell+2)\xi\Pi_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\ell}+(4\ell+2)\xi\Pi_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>; apply Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{n}(Z)=1/\bigl((Z-1)\cdots(Z-n)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}(Z)=1/\bigl((Z-1)\cdots(Z-n)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\,e_{n}=e_{n-1}+n\,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\,e_{n}=e_{n-1}+n\,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ze_{n}=\bigl((Z-n)+n\bigr)e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ze_{n}=\bigl((Z-n)+n\bigr)e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z-n)e_{n}=e_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z-n)e_{n}=e_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell+2}(Z)\,(Z-2\ell-4)=F_{\ell}(Z)\,(Z+2\ell+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell+2}(Z)\,(Z-2\ell-4)=F_{\ell}(Z)\,(Z+2\ell+2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}=\sum_{n}G_{n}^{(\ell)}e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}=\sum_{n}G_{n}^{(\ell)}e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell)}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell)}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, for any constant <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z-c)F_{\ell}=Z-c+G_{1}^{(\ell)}+\sum_{m\geq 1}\Bigl(G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m-c)G^{(\ell)}_{m}\Bigr)e_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z-c)F_{\ell}=Z-c+G_{1}^{(\ell)}+\sum_{m\geq 1}\Bigl(G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m-c)G^{(\ell)}_{m}\Bigr)e_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell)}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell)}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, the terms in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> agree on the two
sides of the claimed identity, and comparing coefficients of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (and the
constant, which is the case <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>) shows that the identity is equivalent to</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell+2)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell-4)G^{(\ell+2)}_{m}=G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m+2\ell+2)G^{(\ell)}_{m}\qquad(m\geq 0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell+2)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell-4)G^{(\ell+2)}_{m}=G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m+2\ell+2)G^{(\ell)}_{m}\qquad(m\geq 0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Multiplying by <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and summing, and using
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m}G_{m+1}\xi^{m}=(\Pi-1)/\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m}G_{m+1}\xi^{m}=(\Pi-1)/\xi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m}mG_{m}\xi^{m}=\xi\Pi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m}mG_{m}\xi^{m}=\xi\Pi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is exactly
Corollary <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 2.6. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> after multiplication by <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}(Z)=\dfrac{Z}{Z-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}(Z)=\dfrac{Z}{Z-2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}(x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}(x)=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text"> gives <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}(x)=1+x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}(x)=1+x</annotation></semantics></math>
gives <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1}(\xi)=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)=1+2\xi+2\xi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1}(\xi)=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)=1+2\xi+2\xi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}=1+\frac{2}{Z-1}+\frac{2}{(Z-1)(Z-2)}=\frac{Z^{2}-Z}{(Z-1)(Z-2)}=\frac{Z}{Z-2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}=1+\frac{2}{Z-1}+\frac{2}{(Z-1)(Z-2)}=\frac{Z^{2}-Z}{(Z-1)(Z-2)}=\frac{Z}{Z-2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=Z/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=Z/4</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z)=\mathcal{M}_{\ell}(Z):=\frac{\Gamma\bigl(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2}\bigr)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2}\bigr)}{\Gamma(\zeta)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1}{2}\bigr)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z)=\mathcal{M}_{\ell}(Z):=\frac{\Gamma\bigl(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2}\bigr)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2}\bigr)}{\Gamma(\zeta)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1}{2}\bigr)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the functional equation of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathcal{M}_{\ell+2}}{\mathcal{M}_{\ell}}=\frac{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2}-1)}{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2})}\cdot\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{\ell+3}{2})}{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2})}=\frac{\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2}}{\zeta-\frac{\ell+2}{2}}=\frac{Z+2\ell+2}{Z-2\ell-4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathcal{M}_{\ell+2}}{\mathcal{M}_{\ell}}=\frac{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2}-1)}{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{\ell}{2})}\cdot\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{\ell+3}{2})}{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2})}=\frac{\zeta+\frac{\ell+1}{2}}{\zeta-\frac{\ell+2}{2}}=\frac{Z+2\ell+2}{Z-2\ell-4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>. For the base cases, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\frac{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{1}{2})}{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})}\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+1)}{\Gamma(\zeta)}=\frac{\zeta}{\zeta-\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{Z}{Z-2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\frac{\Gamma(\zeta-\frac{1}{2})}{\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})}\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+1)}{\Gamma(\zeta)}=\frac{\zeta}{\zeta-\frac{1}{2}}=\frac{Z}{Z-2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">matching Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>. Induction in steps of two along each parity class completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>From half-integers to all <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For arbitrary weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, each coefficient <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F</annotation></semantics></math> is a
polynomial in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> of total degree at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg_{A}L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>deg</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg_{A}L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover the right-hand side of
(<a href="#S1.E9" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is a polynomial in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}(\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}(\nu)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is a polynomial in <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}(\alpha)a_{n-l}(\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}(\alpha)a_{n-l}(\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a polynomial in <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math>
of total degree at most <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Since</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n}=\sum_{m\geq n}\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}Z^{-m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}=\sum_{m\geq n}\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}Z^{-m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the Stirling numbers of the
second kind, the coefficients</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}=\sum_{n\leq m}G_{n}\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}=\sum_{n\leq m}G_{n}\genfrac{}{}{0.0pt}{}{m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">of <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=1+\sum_{m}c_{m}Z^{-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=1+\sum_{m}c_{m}Z^{-m}</annotation></semantics></math> have total degree at most <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an
isobaric polynomial of weight <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, whence the first two
assertions. On the other
side <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\frac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\frac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\frac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\frac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
is even in <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence a polynomial in
<math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Put</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(\zeta;\alpha)=\frac{\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)}{\Gamma(\zeta)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1}{2}\bigr)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(\zeta;\alpha)=\frac{\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)}{\Gamma(\zeta)\,\Gamma\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1}{2}\bigr)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\mathcal{M}(\zeta;\alpha)\sim\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{\chi_{n}(\alpha)}{n(n+1)}\,\zeta^{-n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∼</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\mathcal{M}(\zeta;\alpha)\sim\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{\chi_{n}(\alpha)}{n(n+1)}\,\zeta^{-n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi_{n}(\alpha)=\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_{n}(\alpha)=\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)+\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a> is the value of
the left-hand quotient at <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The classical gamma-quotient expansion, obtained by subtracting
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Eq. 5.11.8]</cite> at <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=b</annotation></semantics></math> (see also
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Sec. 24.2]</cite> for the Bernoulli polynomials),</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+a)}{\Gamma(\zeta+b)}-(a-b)\log\zeta\sim\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n(n+1)}\bigl(\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(a)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(b)\bigr)\zeta^{-n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∼</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{\Gamma(\zeta+a)}{\Gamma(\zeta+b)}-(a-b)\log\zeta\sim\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n(n+1)}\bigl(\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(a)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(b)\bigr)\zeta^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">applies with <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\frac{1\mp 2\alpha}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∓</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\frac{1\mp 2\alpha}{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\{0,\frac{1}{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\{0,\frac{1}{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; the logarithmic
terms cancel because <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}=\frac{1}{2}=0+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}=\frac{1}{2}=0+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{n+1}\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(a)=\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1-a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{n+1}\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(a)=\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1-a)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{1\mp 2\alpha}{4}=\frac{3\pm 2\alpha}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∓</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{1\mp 2\alpha}{4}=\frac{3\pm 2\alpha}{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-0=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-0=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{1}{2}=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{1}{2}=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
gives the stated form. For the second assertion, at
<math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}=-\frac{\ell}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}=-\frac{\ell}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}=\frac{\ell+1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}=\frac{\ell+1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> both sides of (<a href="#S1.E9" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) are
polynomials in <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>,
<math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\mathcal{M}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\mathcal{M}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; both sides
being rational functions of <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> there, their expansions at <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>
coincide, and by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> the coefficients of
<math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\mathcal{M}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\mathcal{M}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> are the right-hand side of (<a href="#S1.E9" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) evaluated at
<math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the two polynomials agree at the infinitely many
distinct points <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=0,1,2,\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=0,1,2,\dots</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore
coincide. This proves (<a href="#S1.E9" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), sum (<a href="#S1.E9" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) with the generating function
<math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}\mathcal{B}_{k}(x)u^{k}/k!=ue^{xu}/(e^{u}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}\mathcal{B}_{k}(x)u^{k}/k!=ue^{xu}/(e^{u}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=4t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=4t</annotation></semantics></math>. The constant and linear
corrections cancel because
<math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3+2\alpha}{4}+\frac{3-2\alpha}{4}-1-\frac{1}{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3+2\alpha}{4}+\frac{3-2\alpha}{4}-1-\frac{1}{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and one obtains</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{(3+2\alpha)t}+e^{(3-2\alpha)t}-e^{4t}-e^{2t}}{e^{4t}-1}=\frac{2e^{3t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{2e^{2t}\sinh 2t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{A}(t)=\frac{e^{(3+2\alpha)t}+e^{(3-2\alpha)t}-e^{4t}-e^{2t}}{e^{4t}-1}=\frac{2e^{3t}\bigl(\cosh 2\alpha t-\cosh t\bigr)}{2e^{2t}\sinh 2t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, the formal
Laurent series <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F</annotation></semantics></math> on the diagonal is the Stirling series of
<math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4})\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4})/\bigl(\Gamma(\zeta)\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4})\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4})/\bigl(\Gamma(\zeta)\Gamma(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=Z/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=Z/4</annotation></semantics></math>, whose ratio
at <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\mapsto\zeta+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\mapsto\zeta+1</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)/\bigl(\zeta(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})\bigr)=\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr)/\bigl(Z(Z+2)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1-2\alpha}{4}\bigr)\bigl(\zeta+\frac{1+2\alpha}{4}\bigr)/\bigl(\zeta(\zeta+\frac{1}{2})\bigr)=\bigl((Z+1)^{2}-4A\bigr)/\bigl(Z(Z+2)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the ratio identity is first obtained coefficientwise in <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Exponentiating is legitimate because both formal logarithms have zero
constant term, and gives the first displayed contiguity identity. Multiplying
by the exact quotient
<math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(Z+4)/\Gamma(Z)=Z(Z+1)(Z+2)(Z+3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(Z+4)/\Gamma(Z)=Z(Z+1)(Z+2)(Z+3)</annotation></semantics></math> gives the second identity.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, the contiguity of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> determines
(<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\sum_{n\geq 1}c_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\sum_{n\geq 1}c_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> put
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{B}f(t)=\sum_{n}c_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{B}f(t)=\sum_{n}c_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math>; then
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{B}[f(Z+c)]=e^{-ct}\mathfrak{B}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔅</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{B}[f(Z+c)]=e^{-ct}\mathfrak{B}f</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{B}\bigl[\log\frac{Z+a}{Z+b}\bigr]=(e^{-bt}-e^{-at})/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔅</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{B}\bigl[\log\frac{Z+a}{Z+b}\bigr]=(e^{-bt}-e^{-at})/t</annotation></semantics></math>, both term by
term. Applying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{B}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F(Z+4)-\log F(Z)=\log\frac{Z+1-2\alpha}{Z}+\log\frac{Z+1+2\alpha}{Z+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>Z</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F(Z+4)-\log F(Z)=\log\frac{Z+1-2\alpha}{Z}+\log\frac{Z+1+2\alpha}{Z+2}</annotation></semantics></math>
gives
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e^{-4t}-1)\Upsilon_{A}(t)/t=\bigl[1+e^{-2t}-2e^{-t}\cosh 2\alpha t\bigr]/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e^{-4t}-1)\Upsilon_{A}(t)/t=\bigl[1+e^{-2t}-2e^{-t}\cosh 2\alpha t\bigr]/t</annotation></semantics></math>, and
multiplying numerator and denominator by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2t}</annotation></semantics></math> yields (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollaries <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) is <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cosh 2\alpha t=\sum_{k}(4A)^{k}t^{2k}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cosh 2\alpha t=\sum_{k}(4A)^{k}t^{2k}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math> read off (<a href="#S1.E8" title="In Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>);
(ii) is <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cosh 2\alpha t=\cosh t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cosh 2\alpha t=\cosh t</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\pm\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\pm\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; (iii) is
<math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t)(1-\cosh t)=\frac{1}{2}(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>csch</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t)(1-\cosh t)=\frac{1}{2}(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For
Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>: exchanging <math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, complex
conjugation, exchanges the roles of <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), so <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{L_{n}(A,B)}=L_{n}(B,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{L_{n}(A,B)}=L_{n}(B,A)</annotation></semantics></math> for real <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Re}g_{n}=\frac{1}{2}(\partial_{A}+\partial_{B})L_{n}|_{A=B=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}g_{n}=\frac{1}{2}(\partial_{A}+\partial_{B})L_{n}|_{A=B=0}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. half the
derivative along the diagonal, so
<math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\operatorname{Re}\Phi=\partial_{A}\Upsilon_{A}|_{A=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\operatorname{Re}\Phi=\partial_{A}\Upsilon_{A}|_{A=0}</annotation></semantics></math>; and
<math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A}\cosh 2\alpha t|_{A=0}=2t^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A}\cosh 2\alpha t|_{A=0}=2t^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\Phi=t^{2}e^{t}/\sinh 2t=\frac{t^{2}}{2}(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>csch</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\Phi=t^{2}e^{t}/\sinh 2t=\frac{t^{2}}{2}(\operatorname{csch}t+\operatorname{sech}t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Expanding
<math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sech}t=\sum_{j}\mathsf{E}_{2j}t^{2j}/(2j)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sech}t=\sum_{j}\mathsf{E}_{2j}t^{2j}/(2j)!</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{csch}t=\frac{1}{t}+\sum_{k\geq 1}2(1-2^{2k-1})\mathcal{B}_{2k}t^{2k-1}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>csch</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{csch}t=\frac{1}{t}+\sum_{k\geq 1}2(1-2^{2k-1})\mathcal{B}_{2k}t^{2k-1}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math>
gives the coefficient formulas. Finally Euler numbers are odd integers and, by von
Staudt–Clausen, <math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-1=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-1=1</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math>; since
<math id="S3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2^{2k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2^{2k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, <math id="S3.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\operatorname{Re}g_{n})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\operatorname{Re}g_{n})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S3.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The ladder off the midpoint</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> used the midpoint identity
<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{2}+q^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{2}+q^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. For arbitrary weights, the two recurrences of
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> give instead the following system, which couples
the two parities of <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix arbitrary nonzero <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> and set, for <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>),</p>
<table id="S4.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=y_{\ell}(p\xi),\quad V=y_{\ell}(q\xi),\quad U_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi),\quad V_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(q\xi),\qquad\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=UV," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=y_{\ell}(p\xi),\quad V=y_{\ell}(q\xi),\quad U_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi),\quad V_{-}=y_{\ell-1}(q\xi),\qquad\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=UV,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell}=\frac{U_{-}V}{p}+\frac{UV_{-}}{q},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell}=\frac{UV_{-}}{p}+\frac{U_{-}V}{q},\qquad\sigma=\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q},\qquad\rho=\frac{p}{q}+\frac{q}{p}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell}=\frac{U_{-}V}{p}+\frac{UV_{-}}{q},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell}=\frac{UV_{-}}{p}+\frac{U_{-}V}{q},\qquad\sigma=\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q},\qquad\rho=\frac{p}{q}+\frac{q}{p}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">At the midpoint <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell}=C_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell}=C_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and arbitrary <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(i)}\qquad\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}+(\sigma-2\ell\xi)\,\Pi_{\ell}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>(i)</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(i)}\qquad\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}+(\sigma-2\ell\xi)\,\Pi_{\ell}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(ii)}\qquad\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1}=\widetilde{D}_{\ell}+\rho\,(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(ii)</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(ii)}\qquad\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1}=\widetilde{D}_{\ell}+\rho\,(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(iii)}\qquad\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}+\rho\,(2\ell+3)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>(iii)</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(iii)}\qquad\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell}+\rho\,(2\ell+3)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(i) Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(a) at <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=p\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=p\xi</annotation></semantics></math> reads
<math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\,\xi^{2}U^{\prime}=(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\,\xi^{2}U^{\prime}=(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly at <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=q\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=q\xi</annotation></semantics></math>; hence
<math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}=\bigl[(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}\bigr]V/p+U\bigl[(\ell q\xi-1)V+V_{-}\bigr]/q=(2\ell\xi-\sigma)\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell}^{\prime}=\bigl[(\ell p\xi-1)U+U_{-}\bigr]V/p+U\bigl[(\ell q\xi-1)V+V_{-}\bigr]/q=(2\ell\xi-\sigma)\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(ii) With <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{+}=y_{\ell+1}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(b) gives <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{+}=(2\ell+1)p\xi U+U_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{+}=(2\ell+1)p\xi U+U_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{+}=(2\ell+1)q\xi V+V_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{+}=(2\ell+1)q\xi V+V_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1}=\frac{UV_{+}}{p}+\frac{U_{+}V}{q}=(2\ell+1)\xi\Bigl(\frac{q}{p}+\frac{p}{q}\Bigr)UV+\frac{UV_{-}}{p}+\frac{U_{-}V}{q}=\rho(2\ell+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{D}_{\ell}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1}=\frac{UV_{+}}{p}+\frac{U_{+}V}{q}=(2\ell+1)\xi\Bigl(\frac{q}{p}+\frac{p}{q}\Bigr)UV+\frac{UV_{-}}{p}+\frac{U_{-}V}{q}=\rho(2\ell+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{D}_{\ell},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and likewise
<math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1}=2(2\ell+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1}=2(2\ell+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell}+\widetilde{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(iii) Compose the two identities of (ii).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At the midpoint, (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) is Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 2.5. ‣ 2.2. Two classical recurrences, and their consequence ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>; away from
it, the correction is the single term <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(2\ell+3)\xi\Pi_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(2\ell+3)\xi\Pi_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which
couples the two parities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>The physical curve</h2>

<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a general angle <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\theta&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\theta&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math> the series (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is taken with
the weights <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=2/(1-e^{i\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=2/(1-e^{i\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2/(1+e^{i\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2/(1+e^{i\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>. They satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=pq=\frac{4}{1-e^{2i\theta}}=:\varpi,\qquad\text{hence}\qquad\sigma=1,\qquad\rho=\varpi-2" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">hence</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=pq=\frac{4}{1-e^{2i\theta}}=:\varpi,\qquad\text{hence}\qquad\sigma=1,\qquad\rho=\varpi-2</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph">identically in</em><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, not only at the midpoint <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi=2</annotation></semantics></math>).
Equivalently, in the variables <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=p/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=p/2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=q/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=q/2</annotation></semantics></math>, the weights lie on the
curve <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+y=2xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+y=2xy</annotation></semantics></math>, which we call the <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">physical curve</em>; <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> are
the <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Horn variables</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q=2\bigl[(1+e^{i\theta})+(1-e^{i\theta})\bigr]/(1-e^{2i\theta})=4/(1-e^{2i\theta})=pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q=2\bigl[(1+e^{i\theta})+(1-e^{i\theta})\bigr]/(1-e^{2i\theta})=4/(1-e^{2i\theta})=pq</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because the translation to the basis <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> requires
precisely <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the analysis of Section <a href="#S2" title="2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for
half-odd-integer parameters applies on the whole curve. For
<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{\ell}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\ell}(\xi)=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{\ell}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> again
generates the coefficients of <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z)=\sum_{n}G^{(\ell)}_{n}e_{n}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z)=\sum_{n}G^{(\ell)}_{n}e_{n}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On the physical curve, for every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, as rational functions in
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\varpi)(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϖ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\varpi)(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell+2}(Z)\,(Z-2\ell-4)=F_{\ell}(Z)\,(Z+2\ell+2)+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)\,F_{\ell+1}(Z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell+2}(Z)\,(Z-2\ell-4)=F_{\ell}(Z)\,(Z+2\ell+2)+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)\,F_{\ell+1}(Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>(i) with <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, comparing coefficients of
<math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell)G^{(\ell)}_{m}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell)G^{(\ell)}_{m}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>(iii) with <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\varpi-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\varpi-2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)G^{(\ell)}_{m}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)G^{(\ell+1)}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}+2(2\ell+1)G^{(\ell)}_{m}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)G^{(\ell+1)}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Eliminating <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell+2)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell-4)G^{(\ell+2)}_{m}=G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m+2\ell+2)G^{(\ell)}_{m}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)G^{(\ell+1)}_{m}\qquad(m\geq 0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell+2)}_{m+1}+(m-2\ell-4)G^{(\ell+2)}_{m}=G^{(\ell)}_{m+1}+(m+2\ell+2)G^{(\ell)}_{m}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)G^{(\ell+1)}_{m}\qquad(m\geq 0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the translation argument of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> (the case <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math>
supplies the constants, and the coefficients of <math id="S5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> agree because
<math id="S5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}^{(\ell)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}^{(\ell)}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) yields (<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z)=P_{\ell}(Z;\varpi)/\bigl((Z-2)(Z-4)\cdots(Z-2\ell)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ϖ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z)=P_{\ell}(Z;\varpi)/\bigl((Z-2)(Z-4)\cdots(Z-2\ell)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}\in\mathbb{Z}[\varpi][Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ϖ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}\in\mathbb{Z}[\varpi][Z]</annotation></semantics></math> monic of degree <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}=1,\qquad P_{1}=Z+\varpi-2,\qquad P_{\ell+2}(Z)=\bigl(Z^{2}-(2\ell+2)^{2}\bigr)P_{\ell}(Z)+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)\,P_{\ell+1}(Z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}=1,\qquad P_{1}=Z+\varpi-2,\qquad P_{\ell+2}(Z)=\bigl(Z^{2}-(2\ell+2)^{2}\bigr)P_{\ell}(Z)+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)\,P_{\ell+1}(Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi=2</annotation></semantics></math> this collapses to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell+2}=(Z^{2}-(2\ell+2)^{2})P_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell+2}=(Z^{2}-(2\ell+2)^{2})P_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose
solutions are the numerators of the Gamma quotient of
Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 2.10. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>:
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2m}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(Z^{2}-(4j-2)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2m}=\prod_{j=1}^{m}(Z^{2}-(4j-2)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2m+1}=Z\prod_{j=1}^{m}(Z^{2}-16j^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2m+1}=Z\prod_{j=1}^{m}(Z^{2}-16j^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that uniformly
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\nu+2}=(Z^{2}-(2\nu+2)^{2})S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\nu+2}=(Z^{2}-(2\nu+2)^{2})S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1}=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)=1+\varpi\xi+\varpi\xi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1}=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)=1+\varpi\xi+\varpi\xi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text"> gives
<math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}=1+\varpi e_{1}+\varpi e_{2}=(Z^{2}+(\varpi-3)Z+2-\varpi)/((Z-1)(Z-2))=(Z+\varpi-2)/(Z-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϖ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}=1+\varpi e_{1}+\varpi e_{2}=(Z^{2}+(\varpi-3)Z+2-\varpi)/((Z-1)(Z-2))=(Z+\varpi-2)/(Z-2)</annotation></semantics></math>,
the numerator vanishing at <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Solving (<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) for
<math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell+2}</annotation></semantics></math> and clearing denominators gives
<math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell+2}=(Z+2\ell+2)(Z-2\ell-2)P_{\ell}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)P_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell+2}=(Z+2\ell+2)(Z-2\ell-2)P_{\ell}+(\varpi-2)(2\ell+3)P_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by
induction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The recurrence (<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) has a closed solution. Write <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi_{0}=\varpi-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi_{0}=\varpi-2</annotation></semantics></math> and
let</p>
<table id="S5.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)=\frac{k}{2j+k}\binom{2j+k}{j}\qquad(\mathfrak{b}_{0}(k)=1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)=\frac{k}{2j+k}\binom{2j+k}{j}\qquad(\mathfrak{b}_{0}(k)=1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">be the ballot (Catalan-triangle) numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=\ell-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=\ell-k</annotation></semantics></math> in the summand indexed by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell}(Z;2+\varpi_{0})=\sum_{k=0}^{\ell}(2k-1)!!\binom{\ell+k}{2k}\varpi_{0}^{k}\sum_{j\geq 0}(-1)^{j}\,\frac{d!}{(d-2j)!}\;\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)\,S_{d-2j}(Z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell}(Z;2+\varpi_{0})=\sum_{k=0}^{\ell}(2k-1)!!\binom{\ell+k}{2k}\varpi_{0}^{k}\sum_{j\geq 0}(-1)^{j}\,\frac{d!}{(d-2j)!}\;\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)\,S_{d-2j}(Z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the inner sum running over <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq d/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq d/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof consists in verifying that the closed coefficients
(<a href="#S5.E21" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) satisfy the same recurrence and the same initial data
as the curve numerators: the interior identity is checked first, then
the boundary patterns excluded by the interior divisions, then the base
cases; induction closes the argument.
The polynomials <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> have degree <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, so the expansion of any polynomial in
the basis <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\nu}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\nu}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is unique. Write
<math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,j)=(2k-1)!!\binom{\ell+k}{2k}(-1)^{j}\frac{d!}{(d-2j)!}\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,j)=(2k-1)!!\binom{\ell+k}{2k}(-1)^{j}\frac{d!}{(d-2j)!}\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>
for the coefficient of <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\ell-k-2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\ell-k-2j}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E21" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>), with
<math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,j)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,j)=0</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq k\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq k\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq 2j\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq 2j\leq d</annotation></semantics></math>. Since
<math id="S5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{2}S_{\nu}=S_{\nu+2}+(2\nu+2)^{2}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{2}S_{\nu}=S_{\nu+2}+(2\nu+2)^{2}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>, substituting (<a href="#S5.E21" title="In Theorem 5.4. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) into
(<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) and matching the coefficient of <math id="S5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi_{0}^{k}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=(\ell+2)-k-2J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=(\ell+2)-k-2J</annotation></semantics></math> reduces the claim, by uniqueness, to the four-term
identity</span></p>
<table id="S5.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+2,k,J)=c(\ell,k,J)-4(k+2J-2)(2\ell+4-k-2J)\,c(\ell,k,J-1)+(2\ell+3)\,c(\ell+1,k-1,J)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+2,k,J)=c(\ell,k,J)-4(k+2J-2)(2\ell+4-k-2J)\,c(\ell,k,J-1)+(2\ell+3)\,c(\ell+1,k-1,J).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">We verify (<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) for all indices, including the boundary
patterns. First suppose <math id="S5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The coefficient admits the factorial form</span></p>
<table id="S5.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,j)=(-1)^{j}\frac{(\ell+k)!\,(2j+k-1)!}{2^{k}(k-1)!\,j!\,(j+k)!\,(\ell-k-2j)!}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,j)=(-1)^{j}\frac{(\ell+k)!\,(2j+k-1)!}{2^{k}(k-1)!\,j!\,(j+k)!\,(\ell-k-2j)!},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">whenever <math id="S5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-k-2j\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-k-2j\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; outside this range it is zero by convention.
This follows immediately from
<math id="S5.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k-1)!!=(2k)!/(2^{k}k!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k-1)!!=(2k)!/(2^{k}k!)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)=k(2j+k-1)!/(j!(j+k)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{b}_{j}(k)=k(2j+k-1)!/(j!(j+k)!)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the interior range <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\ell-k-2J\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\ell-k-2J\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, division by the
nonzero coefficient <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,J)</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell+2,k,J)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)}{(n+1)(n+2)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell+2,k,J)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)}{(n+1)(n+2)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{2(k-1)(J+k)}{(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{2(k-1)(J+k)}{(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{-\,J(J+k)}{(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell,k,J)}=\frac{-\,J(J+k)}{(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">Substitution in (<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>), followed by clearing denominators,
reduces the recurrence to</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)(2J+k-1)={}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)(2J+k-1)={}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(n+1)(n+2)(2J+k-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+4J(J+k)(2\ell+4-k-2J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+4J(J+k)(2\ell+4-k-2J)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+2(2\ell+3)(k-1)(J+k)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+2(2\ell+3)(k-1)(J+k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">and after <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=n+k+2J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=n+k+2J</annotation></semantics></math> both sides expand to the same cubic polynomial.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to treat the boundary cases excluded by that division. If <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,0,0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,0,0)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,0,j)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,0,j)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
(<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) is immediate: for <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=0</annotation></semantics></math> it is <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=1</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=1</annotation></semantics></math> the only
potential term <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplied by
<math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+2J-2=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+2J-2=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> every term is zero.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, the middle term of
(<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) vanishes and (<a href="#S5.E23" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell+2,k,0)}{c(\ell,k,0)}=\frac{(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)}{(\ell-k+1)(\ell-k+2)},\qquad\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,0)}{c(\ell,k,0)}=\frac{2k}{(\ell-k+1)(\ell-k+2)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell+2,k,0)}{c(\ell,k,0)}=\frac{(\ell+k+1)(\ell+k+2)}{(\ell-k+1)(\ell-k+2)},\qquad\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,0)}{c(\ell,k,0)}=\frac{2k}{(\ell-k+1)(\ell-k+2)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">(For <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> one uses <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+1,0,0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+1,0,0)=1</annotation></semantics></math> directly.) Their substitution gives
<math id="S5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+2,k,0)=c(\ell,k,0)+(2\ell+3)c(\ell+1,k-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+2,k,0)=c(\ell,k,0)+(2\ell+3)c(\ell+1,k-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly. The only
remaining <math id="S5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=0</annotation></semantics></math> targets have <math id="S5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\ell+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\ell+2</annotation></semantics></math>; then
<math id="S5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and the middle term is again zero, while direct evaluation
of the double-factorial formula gives</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+2,k,0)=(2\ell+3)c(\ell+1,k-1,0)=(2\ell+3)!!." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+2,k,0)=(2\ell+3)c(\ell+1,k-1,0)=(2\ell+3)!!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally take <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. When the target coefficient
<math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+2,k,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+2,k,J)</annotation></semantics></math> exists but <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell,k,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell,k,J)</annotation></semantics></math> does not, necessarily
<math id="S5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=-2</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=-1</annotation></semantics></math>; these are the only remaining possibilities because the
target basis index is <math id="S5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=-2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=-\frac{J}{2(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)(2J+2k-1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=-\frac{J}{2(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)(2J+2k-1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=\frac{k-1}{(2J+k-1)(2J+2k-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=\frac{k-1}{(2J+k-1)(2J+2k-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since here <math id="S5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=k+2J-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=k+2J-2</annotation></semantics></math>, insertion in the right-hand side of
(<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) gives exactly <math id="S5.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> after division by
<math id="S5.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+2,k,J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+2,k,J)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=-1</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding ratios are</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=-\frac{J}{2(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)(2J+2k+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell,k,J-1)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=-\frac{J}{2(2J+k-2)(2J+k-1)(2J+2k+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=\frac{k-1}{(2J+k-1)(2J+2k+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{c(\ell+1,k-1,J)}{c(\ell+2,k,J)}=\frac{k-1}{(2J+k-1)(2J+2k+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">and with <math id="S5.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=k+2J-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=k+2J-1</annotation></semantics></math> the same substitution again gives <math id="S5.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. These formulas
also cover <math id="S5.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, because then
<math id="S5.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\ell+1,0,J)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\ell+1,0,J)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and the factor <math id="S5.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes. Thus
(<a href="#S5.E22" title="In Proof. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) holds for every admissible coefficient.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">With the base cases <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}=1=c(0,0,0)S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}=1=c(0,0,0)S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{1}=Z+\varpi_{0}=S_{1}+\varpi_{0}S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϖ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{1}=Z+\varpi_{0}=S_{1}+\varpi_{0}S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, induction on <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> through (<a href="#S5.E20" title="In Corollary 5.3. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) completes
the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The curve <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math> passes through the midpoint in the plane of the
Horn variables <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. On it, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, the
degree bound of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (valid at any fixed <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>) and
interpolation at <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> determine the diagonal amplitude
for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. The derivatives normal to the curve are not
determined in this way: for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient relations take
the form <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma G_{m+1}+(m-c)G_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma G_{m+1}+(m-c)G_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and their translation to the basis
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> involves, in addition, the operation
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{m}G_{m}e_{m}\mapsto\sum_{m}mG_{m}e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{m}G_{m}e_{m}\mapsto\sum_{m}mG_{m}e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is not expressible through
rational operations and shifts in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. This operation is studied in
Section <a href="#S6" title="6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 5.5. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, extending the curve contiguity away from
<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math> requires the multiplication of the coefficient <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> by its
index <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. We show that this operation is a first-order differential
operator in the normal variable, and deduce a recurrence which
determines every derivative normal to the curve of the
half-odd-integer diagonal amplitudes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For arbitrary <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+q\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+q\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, put
<math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{p}{q}+\frac{q}{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{p}{q}+\frac{q}{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let</p>
<table id="S6.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}(Z;\sigma,\rho)=\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m}^{(\ell)}e_{m}(Z),\qquad e_{m}(Z)=\frac{1}{(Z-1)\cdots(Z-m)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}(Z;\sigma,\rho)=\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m}^{(\ell)}e_{m}(Z),\qquad e_{m}(Z)=\frac{1}{(Z-1)\cdots(Z-m)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The two invariants recover the symmetric functions of <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S6.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pq=\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad p+q=\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pq=\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma^{2}},\qquad p+q=\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Consequently <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric in <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math> and is a rational function of
<math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z,\sigma,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z,\sigma,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> for every integer <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definitionx">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On an inverse-factorial series define the <em id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">index operator</em></p>
<table id="S6.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\Bigl(\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m}e_{m}\Bigr)=\sum_{m\geq 0}mG_{m}e_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\Bigl(\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m}e_{m}\Bigr)=\sum_{m\geq 0}mG_{m}e_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If every <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{m}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{m}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous of total degree <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, then
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}F=(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y})F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}F=(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y})F</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}F=\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m+1}e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}F=\sum_{m\geq 0}G_{m+1}e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S6.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}F=Z(F-1)-\mathcal{I}F," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}F=Z(F-1)-\mathcal{I}F,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q</annotation></semantics></math>, hence a function of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then at fixed <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{I}=-\sigma\partial_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=-\sigma\partial_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Homogeneity gives the first assertion. Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> gives
<math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZF=Z+\sum_{m\geq 1}G_{m}e_{m-1}+\sum_{m\geq 1}mG_{m}e_{m}=Z+\mathcal{R}F+\mathcal{I}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF=Z+\sum_{m\geq 1}G_{m}e_{m-1}+\sum_{m\geq 1}mG_{m}e_{m}=Z+\mathcal{R}F+\mathcal{I}F</annotation></semantics></math>, which is (<a href="#S6.E26" title="In Lemma 6.2. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). Under a common
scaling <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)\mapsto(rp,rq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)\mapsto(rp,rq)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and
<math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\mapsto r^{-1}\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\mapsto r^{-1}\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>; hence <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}=-\sigma\partial_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>σ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}=-\sigma\partial_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>
at fixed <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Define the first-order normal operator</p>
<table id="S6.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(\sigma)}F:=\sigma Z(F-1)-\sigma(1-\sigma)\partial_{\sigma}F-cF=\sum_{m\geq 0}\bigl(\sigma G_{m+1}+(m-c)G_{m}\bigr)e_{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(\sigma)}F:=\sigma Z(F-1)-\sigma(1-\sigma)\partial_{\sigma}F-cF=\sum_{m\geq 0}\bigl(\sigma G_{m+1}+(m-c)G_{m}\bigr)e_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus the operation of Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Remark 5.5. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> is a first-order
derivative in the coordinate <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and arbitrary <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{2\ell+4}^{(\sigma)}F_{\ell+2}=\mathcal{Q}_{-2\ell-2}^{(\sigma)}F_{\ell}+\rho(2\ell+3)F_{\ell+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{2\ell+4}^{(\sigma)}F_{\ell+2}=\mathcal{Q}_{-2\ell-2}^{(\sigma)}F_{\ell}+\rho(2\ell+3)F_{\ell+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math> this reduces to the curve contiguity
(<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">From Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>(i), comparison of the coefficient of
<math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell)}+(m-2\ell)G_{m}^{(\ell)}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell)}+(m-2\ell)G_{m}^{(\ell)}=[\xi^{m+1}]\widetilde{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Use Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>(iii) to eliminate <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>. One obtains, for every <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell+2)}+(m-2\ell-4)G_{m}^{(\ell+2)}={}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell+2)}+(m-2\ell-4)G_{m}^{(\ell+2)}={}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell)}+(m+2\ell+2)G_{m}^{(\ell)}+\rho(2\ell+3)G_{m}^{(\ell+1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma G_{m+1}^{(\ell)}+(m+2\ell+2)G_{m}^{(\ell)}+\rho(2\ell+3)G_{m}^{(\ell+1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Multiplication by <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and summation give
(<a href="#S6.E28" title="In Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>). At <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S6.E27" title="In 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) becomes
<math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(1)}F=(Z-c)F-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(1)}F=(Z-c)F-Z</annotation></semantics></math>; the two copies of <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-Z</annotation></semantics></math> cancel and
(<a href="#S5.E19" title="In Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient of <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\sigma}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\sigma}F</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S6.E27" title="In 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) vanishes. Nevertheless all derivatives in <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
at <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math> are determined recursively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Put
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}^{[k]}(Z;\rho)=\partial_{\sigma}^{k}F_{\ell}(Z;\sigma,\rho)\big|_{\sigma=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}^{[k]}(Z;\rho)=\partial_{\sigma}^{k}F_{\ell}(Z;\sigma,\rho)\big|_{\sigma=1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}^{[-1]}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}^{[-1]}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(Z+k-2\ell-4)F_{\ell+2}^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F_{\ell+2}^{[k-1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(Z+k-2\ell-4)F_{\ell+2}^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F_{\ell+2}^{[k-1]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad=(Z+k+2\ell+2)F_{\ell}^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F_{\ell}^{[k-1]}+\rho(2\ell+3)F_{\ell+1}^{[k]}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="2.278em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad=(Z+k+2\ell+2)F_{\ell}^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F_{\ell}^{[k-1]}+\rho(2\ell+3)F_{\ell+1}^{[k]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, at the midpoint <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the two parity ladders decouple
and every <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}^{[k]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}^{[k]}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained recursively from the two base families
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, Leibniz’ rule applied to (<a href="#S6.E27" title="In 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) gives</span></p>
<table id="S6.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\sigma}^{k}\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(\sigma)}F\big|_{\sigma=1}=(Z+k-c)F^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F^{[k-1]}-\delta_{k,1}Z:" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>σ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\sigma}^{k}\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(\sigma)}F\big|_{\sigma=1}=(Z+k-c)F^{[k]}+k(Z+k-1)F^{[k-1]}-\delta_{k,1}Z:</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">only the first two derivatives of <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(1-\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(1-\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> are nonzero, and
their contributions combine with the derivative of <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma Z(F-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma Z(F-1)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly
as displayed. For <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> the formula is
<math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(1)}F=(Z-c)F^{[0]}-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{c}^{(1)}F=(Z-c)F^{[0]}-Z</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> the terms <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-Z</annotation></semantics></math>,
which do not depend on <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, cancel between the two
sides of (<a href="#S6.E28" title="In Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>). Differentiating (<a href="#S6.E28" title="In Theorem 6.3. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>) <math id="S6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times
and using (<a href="#S6.E30" title="In Proof. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) proves (<a href="#S6.Ex54" title="In Theorem 6.4. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The base data are explicit: from <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1}=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1}=(1+p\xi)(1+q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> and (<a href="#S6.E25" title="In 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S6.E31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(31)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}=1,\qquad F_{1}=1+\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma}\,e_{1}+\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma^{2}}\,e_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>σ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}=1,\qquad F_{1}=1+\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma}\,e_{1}+\frac{\rho+2}{\sigma^{2}}\,e_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p">hence <math id="S6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}^{[k]}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}^{[k]}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S6.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(32)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}^{[k]}=(-1)^{k}k!\,(\rho+2)\bigl(e_{1}+(k+1)e_{2}\bigr)\qquad(k\geq 1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}^{[k]}=(-1)^{k}k!\,(\rho+2)\bigl(e_{1}+(k+1)e_{2}\bigr)\qquad(k\geq 1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p8.3" class="ltx_p">Equations (<a href="#S6.Ex54" title="In Theorem 6.4. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>)–(<a href="#S6.E32" title="In 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a>) therefore determine every
<math id="S6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell}^{[k]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell}^{[k]}</annotation></semantics></math> recursively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each fixed order in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and each fixed <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the interpolation
argument of Section <a href="#S3" title="3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> extends (<a href="#S6.Ex54" title="In Theorem 6.4. ‣ 6. The index operator and the derivatives normal to the curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>),
written with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell+1=2\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell+1=2\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell},F_{\ell+1},F_{\ell+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell},F_{\ell+1},F_{\ell+2}</annotation></semantics></math>
replaced by the diagonal amplitudes at <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\alpha+1,\alpha+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\alpha+1,\alpha+2</annotation></semantics></math>, from
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to arbitrary <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, each coefficient being a
polynomial in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the diagonal
amplitude is formally determined, to any prescribed order in
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma-1</annotation></semantics></math>, in a neighbourhood of the physical curve.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section contains the proofs of Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>
and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>. We begin with two preliminary results for parameters
off the diagonal: a recurrence for independent half-odd-integer
parameters, and the identification of the amplitude (<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) with a
double hypergeometric series.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Take <em id="S7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent</em> half-odd-integer parameters
<math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=m+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=m+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at the midpoint <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1+i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1-i</annotation></semantics></math>:
both Hankel series terminate and <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\ell,m}(\xi)=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\ell,m}(\xi)=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>
generates the coefficients of the rational function
<math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\sum_{n}G^{(\ell,m)}_{n}e_{n}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\sum_{n}G^{(\ell,m)}_{n}e_{n}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Set
<math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)/p+y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m-1}(q\xi)/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}=y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)/p+y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m-1}(q\xi)/q</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m}=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m-1}(q\xi)/p+y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m}=y_{\ell}(p\xi)y_{m-1}(q\xi)/p+y_{\ell-1}(p\xi)y_{m}(q\xi)/q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell,m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell,m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, at the midpoint,</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(33)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell,m}^{\prime}+\bigl(1-(\ell+m)\xi\bigr)\Pi_{\ell,m}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\xi^{2}\Pi_{\ell,m}^{\prime}+\bigl(1-(\ell+m)\xi\bigr)\Pi_{\ell,m}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(34)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m}+2i(\ell-m)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell,m},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}+2(\ell+m+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell,m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m}+2i(\ell-m)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell,m},\qquad\widetilde{D}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}+2(\ell+m+1)\,\xi\,\Pi_{\ell,m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(35)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{\ell+2,m+2}(Z)\,\bigl(Z-(\ell+m)-4\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{\ell+2,m+2}(Z)\,\bigl(Z-(\ell+m)-4\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad=F_{\ell,m}(Z)\,\bigl(Z+(\ell+m)+2\bigr)+2i(\ell-m)\,F_{\ell+1,m+1}(Z)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="2.278em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad=F_{\ell,m}(Z)\,\bigl(Z+(\ell+m)+2\bigr)+2i(\ell-m)\,F_{\ell+1,m+1}(Z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">(<a href="#S7.E33" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) is the computation of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. The ladder off the midpoint ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>(i) with the two
orders kept distinct, using <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For (<a href="#S7.E34" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>), with <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=y_{\ell}(p\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=y_{\ell}(p\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=y_{m}(q\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=y_{m}(q\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\frac{UV_{+}}{p}+\frac{U_{+}V}{q}=\xi\Bigl[(2m+1)\frac{q}{p}+(2\ell+1)\frac{p}{q}\Bigr]\Pi_{\ell,m}+\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell+1,m+1}=\frac{UV_{+}}{p}+\frac{U_{+}V}{q}=\xi\Bigl[(2m+1)\frac{q}{p}+(2\ell+1)\frac{p}{q}\Bigr]\Pi_{\ell,m}+\widetilde{D}_{\ell,m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and at the midpoint <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q/p=-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q/p=-i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p/q=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p/q=i</annotation></semantics></math>, so the bracket is <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i(\ell-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i(\ell-m)</annotation></semantics></math>; the
second identity has bracket <math id="S7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2\ell+1)+(2m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2\ell+1)+(2m+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing gives
<math id="S7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2,m+2}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}+2(\ell+m+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell,m}+2i(\ell-m)\xi\Pi_{\ell+1,m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}_{\ell+2,m+2}=\widetilde{C}_{\ell,m}+2(\ell+m+1)\xi\Pi_{\ell,m}+2i(\ell-m)\xi\Pi_{\ell+1,m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the translation of
Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2. ‣ 5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, with <math id="S7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell+m</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, yields
(<a href="#S7.E35" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>); the constants match because
<math id="S7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell,m)}_{1}=\frac{\ell(\ell+1)}{2}p+\frac{m(m+1)}{2}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell,m)}_{1}=\frac{\ell(\ell+1)}{2}p+\frac{m(m+1)}{2}q</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S7.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(\ell+2,m+2)}_{1}-G^{(\ell,m)}_{1}=\bigl[(\ell+m)+2\bigr]+\bigl[(\ell+m)+4\bigr]+2i(\ell-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(\ell+2,m+2)}_{1}-G^{(\ell,m)}_{1}=\bigl[(\ell+m)+2\bigr]+\bigl[(\ell+m)+4\bigr]+2i(\ell-m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the diagonal <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=m</annotation></semantics></math> the inhomogeneity vanishes and
(<a href="#S7.E35" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) is Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 2.8. ‣ 2.3. From the generating function to the inverse-factorial series ‣ 2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>. Off the diagonal the
transverse direction enters as the explicit term <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i(\ell-m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i(\ell-m)</annotation></semantics></math>:
antisymmetric, purely imaginary, of constant coefficient along the diagonal
step. Together with the two boundary strips <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the recurrence determines
every <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}</annotation></semantics></math> by induction on <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(\ell,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(\ell,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, using the midpoint
conjugation symmetry</p>
<table id="S7.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\overline{F_{m,\ell}(\overline{Z})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\overline{F_{m,\ell}(\overline{Z})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.2" class="ltx_p">(and hence <math id="S7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\overline{F_{m,\ell}(Z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}(Z)=\overline{F_{m,\ell}(Z)}</annotation></semantics></math> for real <math id="S7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>) whenever
the smaller of the two indices is <math id="S7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. More explicitly,
if <math id="S7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, equation (<a href="#S7.E35" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) expresses <math id="S7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms
of <math id="S7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell-2,m-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell-2,m-2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell-1,m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell-1,m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> after shifting the two indices down;
repeating reaches <math id="S7.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S7.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math> one first interchanges the
indices by the displayed conjugation symmetry and applies the same argument.
The strip <math id="S7.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0</annotation></semantics></math> is the terminating Gauss family of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>; the
strip <math id="S7.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> is equally explicit from <math id="S7.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}(q\xi)=1+q\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}(q\xi)=1+q\xi</annotation></semantics></math> and the finite defining
sum. Thus the two-step recurrence, together with these two strips, determines
every <math id="S7.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,0}(Z)=\Fhyp\bigl(-\ell,\ell+1;1-Z;x_{0}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>{}_2F_1</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,0}(Z)=\Fhyp\bigl(-\ell,\ell+1;1-Z;x_{0}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=\frac{1+i}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=\frac{1+i}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. More generally, for all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, as formal
series,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(36)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\sum_{\mu,\nu\geq 0}\frac{(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}-\beta)_{\nu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\beta)_{\nu}}{(1-Z)_{\mu+\nu}\;\mu!\,\nu!}\;x_{0}^{\,\mu}y_{0}^{\,\nu}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>ν</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\sum_{\mu,\nu\geq 0}\frac{(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}-\beta)_{\nu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\beta)_{\nu}}{(1-Z)_{\mu+\nu}\;\mu!\,\nu!}\;x_{0}^{\,\mu}y_{0}^{\,\nu},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a Kampé de Fériet function <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F^{0:2;2}_{1:0;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{0:2;2}_{1:0;0}</annotation></semantics></math> at arguments with
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}+y_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}+y_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}y_{0}=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}y_{0}=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n}(Z)=(-1)^{n}/(1-Z)_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}(Z)=(-1)^{n}/(1-Z)_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text"> and
<math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\mu}(\alpha)(2x_{0})^{\mu}(-1)^{\mu}=(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{\mu}x_{0}^{\mu}/\mu!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>μ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\mu}(\alpha)(2x_{0})^{\mu}(-1)^{\mu}=(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{\mu}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{\mu}x_{0}^{\mu}/\mu!</annotation></semantics></math>, term by term in
(<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). At <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\ell+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, only <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0</annotation></semantics></math>
survives and the <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-series terminates.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>The Appell <math id="S7.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> specialization on the physical curve</h3>

<section id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Branch convention.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All multivalued functions in this and
the following subsections are continued from a common germ at the
origin of the path described below: the Appell/Kampé de Fériet series at
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> small, the reduced Gauss function through the argument path
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4x(\lambda)(1-x(\lambda))=2\lambda+2i\lambda(1-\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4x(\lambda)(1-x(\lambda))=2\lambda+2i\lambda(1-\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, which stays in the <em id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">upper</em> half-plane
and reaches <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+i0</annotation></semantics></math>; every boundary value written
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(\cdots;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(\cdots;2)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1-z)\to-i\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1-z)\to-i\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1-i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1-i0</annotation></semantics></math>
is the one obtained along this path, and the lower determinations
(denoted <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) are their Schwarz reflections. Replacing the
upper boundary value by the lower one changes the functions; no identity
below mixes the two determinations except where both appear explicitly,
as in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. The two-component relation and vertical decay ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Kampé de Fériet notation in Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> hides an additional
one-variable structure. We make it explicit because it removes one of the
two Horn variables before any asymptotic or <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic argument is made.
For independent variables <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> write</p>
<table id="S7.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,y):=F_{3}\!\left(\begin{matrix}\frac{1}{2}-\alpha,\frac{1}{2}-\beta;\frac{1}{2}+\alpha,\frac{1}{2}+\beta\\[2.84526pt]
1-Z\end{matrix}\middle|x,y\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,y):=F_{3}\!\left(\begin{matrix}\frac{1}{2}-\alpha,\frac{1}{2}-\beta;\frac{1}{2}+\alpha,\frac{1}{2}+\beta\\[2.84526pt]
1-Z\end{matrix}\middle|x,y\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the convention is that the first and third upper parameters belong to
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second and fourth to <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus (<a href="#S7.E36" title="In Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) is precisely
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x_{0},y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x_{0},y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Put <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1-Z</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}=\frac{1}{2}+\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}=\frac{1}{2}+\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}=\frac{1}{2}+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}=\frac{1}{2}+\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. On</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\frac{x}{2x-1},\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad x+y=2xy," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">equivalently</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\frac{x}{2x-1},\qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad x+y=2xy,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one has, for generic parameters and then by meromorphic continuation,</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}\!\left(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,\frac{x}{2x-1}\right)={}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.648em;" width="0.648em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}\!\left(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,\frac{x}{2x-1}\right)={}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-x)^{c-1}(1-2x)^{b_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-x)^{c-1}(1-2x)^{b_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(37)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}+c}{2},\frac{b_{1}+b_{2}+c-1}{2};c;4x(1-x)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\times{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left(\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}+c}{2},\frac{b_{1}+b_{2}+c-1}{2};c;4x(1-x)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The branches are those obtained by analytic continuation from a neighbourhood
of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> along the chosen path.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The four upper parameters of our <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-b_{1},1-b_{2};b_{1},b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-b_{1},1-b_{2};b_{1},b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fourth pair in Theorem 3.1 of
Vidūnas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> is therefore exactly the pair displayed in
(<a href="#S7.E37" title="In Theorem 7.3. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>). Immediately after that theorem, Vidūnas states
that in the first and fourth pairs the two functions are directly identical
in a neighbourhood of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> (not merely solutions of the same second-order
ODE). Hence (<a href="#S7.E37" title="In Theorem 7.3. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">37</span></a>) holds on the common germ at <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The relation <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x/(2x-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x/(2x-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is the physical-curve identity already used in
Section <a href="#S5" title="5. The physical curve ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Therefore the two sides represent the same analytic
germ near <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Analytic continuation of this common germ along any path
avoiding the singular locus gives the stated identity. Notice that this is
an identity for fixed generic <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> (and then meromorphically in the
parameters); no passage to the large-<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> limit is used in establishing it.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At the midpoint,</p>
<table id="S7.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=\frac{1+i}{2},\qquad y_{0}=\frac{1-i}{2},\qquad y_{0}=\frac{x_{0}}{2x_{0}-1},\qquad 4x_{0}(1-x_{0})=2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=\frac{1+i}{2},\qquad y_{0}=\frac{1-i}{2},\qquad y_{0}=\frac{x_{0}}{2x_{0}-1},\qquad 4x_{0}(1-x_{0})=2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For the straight path <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x(\lambda)=\lambda x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x(\lambda)=\lambda x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, one has</p>
<table id="S7.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y(\lambda)=\frac{x(\lambda)}{2x(\lambda)-1},\qquad 4x(\lambda)(1-x(\lambda))=2\lambda+2i\lambda(1-\lambda)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y(\lambda)=\frac{x(\lambda)}{2x(\lambda)-1},\qquad 4x(\lambda)(1-x(\lambda))=2\lambda+2i\lambda(1-\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Thus the transformed Gauss argument stays strictly in the upper half-plane
for <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and tends to <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+i0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\uparrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\uparrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition,
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x(\lambda)|=\lambda/\sqrt{2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x(\lambda)|=\lambda/\sqrt{2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S7.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y(\lambda)|^{2}=\frac{\lambda^{2}}{2(2\lambda^{2}-2\lambda+1)}\leq\frac{1}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y(\lambda)|^{2}=\frac{\lambda^{2}}{2(2\lambda^{2}-2\lambda+1)}\leq\frac{1}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">so the defining Appell double series itself stays inside its bidisc of
absolute convergence along the whole path. The path has <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, meets
neither <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the additional finite Appell singular relation
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy=x+y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy=x+y</annotation></semantics></math>, together with <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x+y=2xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x+y=2xy</annotation></semantics></math>, would force <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence continuation
may start at any sufficiently small <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> inside the common germ and reaches
the midpoint without meeting the finite singular locus. The only
nontrivial continuation issue in the reduced expression is therefore the
Gauss boundary value at its standard cut. If
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;c;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;c;2)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes this upper boundary value, then
analytic continuation of the common germ gives</p>
<table id="S7.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(38)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=y_{0}^{-Z}(-i)^{\frac{1}{2}+\beta}{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}\!\left(\frac{1-Z+\beta-\alpha}{2},\frac{1-Z+\alpha+\beta}{2};1-Z;2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=y_{0}^{-Z}(-i)^{\frac{1}{2}+\beta}{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}\!\left(\frac{1-Z+\beta-\alpha}{2},\frac{1-Z+\alpha+\beta}{2};1-Z;2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.5" class="ltx_p">This branch convention is essential: the two boundary values on the cut
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> need not agree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Set <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(1-Z)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(1-Z)/2</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}\!\left(s-\frac{\alpha}{2},s+\frac{\alpha}{2};2s;2\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}\!\left(s-\frac{\alpha}{2},s+\frac{\alpha}{2};2s;2\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the inverse-power expansion at <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> inherited coefficientwise
from the defining inverse-factorial series for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S7.E39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(39)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{2}\left.\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial\alpha^{2}}\log\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)\right|_{\alpha=0}=\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}{\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mo>∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{2}\left.\frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial\alpha^{2}}\log\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)\right|_{\alpha=0}=\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}{\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The last expression is independent of a choice of logarithm. In particular
the two numerator parameters in (<a href="#S7.E39" title="In Corollary 7.4. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>) sum to the
denominator parameter: the series <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from a
parameter variation of a single <em id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">zero-balanced</em> Gauss function.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Take <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S7.E38" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>). Its prefactor is independent of
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the left-hand side is even in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{A}|_{A=0}=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{\alpha}^{2}|_{\alpha=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A}|_{A=0}=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{\alpha}^{2}|_{\alpha=0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The function
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself even in <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\alpha}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\alpha}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>;
therefore the second logarithmic derivative is
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)/\mathcal{H}(0;Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)/\mathcal{H}(0;Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=1+O(Z^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=1+O(Z^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> coefficientwise, so its formal logarithm at
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely defined. Since (<a href="#S7.E38" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) is an exact
identity for generic fixed <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, the formal inverse-power expansion used in
this paper may be taken on either side; the coefficients inherited from the
left-hand inverse-factorial series are therefore exactly those in
(<a href="#S7.E39" title="In Corollary 7.4. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>). No assertion about convergence of the resulting
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> series is needed.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">There is also a differential form of the same reduction. With
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the defining coefficients
of <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> give</p>
<table id="S7.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(40)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A)}\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A)}\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}-Z)-x\bigl((\Theta_{x}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-A\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}-Z)-x\bigl((\Theta_{x}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-A\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(41)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(B)}\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(B)}\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(B)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(B)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}-Z)-y\bigl((\Theta_{y}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-B\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}(\Theta_{x}+\Theta_{y}-Z)-y\bigl((\Theta_{y}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-B\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Thus the parameters enter the Appell system <em id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">affinely</em>. Put</p>
<table id="S7.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}=\frac{A+B}{2},\qquad A_{-}=\frac{A-B}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}=\frac{A+B}{2},\qquad A_{-}=\frac{A-B}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}=\mathcal{F}|_{A_{-}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}=\mathcal{F}|_{A_{-}=0}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}=\partial_{A_{-}}\mathcal{F}|_{A_{-}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}=\partial_{A_{-}}\mathcal{F}|_{A_{-}=0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Differentiating
(<a href="#S7.E40" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E41" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) yields the forced
system</p>
<table id="S7.E42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(42)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A_{+})}\mathcal{F}_{1}=-x\mathcal{F}_{0},\qquad\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(A_{+})}\mathcal{F}_{1}=+y\mathcal{F}_{0},\qquad\mathcal{F}_{1}(y,x)=-\mathcal{F}_{1}(x,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{x}^{(A_{+})}\mathcal{F}_{1}=-x\mathcal{F}_{0},\qquad\mathcal{L}_{y}^{(A_{+})}\mathcal{F}_{1}=+y\mathcal{F}_{0},\qquad\mathcal{F}_{1}(y,x)=-\mathcal{F}_{1}(x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.4" class="ltx_p">At the midpoint <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}=\overline{x_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}=\overline{x_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, coefficientwise conjugation and
exchange of the two variables give
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{F}(A_{+},A_{-};x_{0},y_{0})}=\mathcal{F}(A_{+},-A_{-};x_{0},y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{F}(A_{+},A_{-};x_{0},y_{0})}=\mathcal{F}(A_{+},-A_{-};x_{0},y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> for
real <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and real <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, at <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{+}}\mathcal{F}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{+}}\mathcal{F}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are real, whereas <math id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is purely
imaginary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a polynomial in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math>; its values on the grid
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}=(\ell+\frac{1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{m}=(m+\frac{1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{m}=(m+\frac{1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined by the
rational functions <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\ell,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\ell,m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the node differences
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\ell}-A_{m}=(\ell-m)(\ell+m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\ell}-A_{m}=(\ell-m)(\ell+m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> are integers. The recurrence
(<a href="#S7.E35" title="In Theorem 7.1. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) therefore expresses <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
Lagrange interpolation functional with integer node differences.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>The boundary split and the transverse quadrature</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 7.4. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, the series <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> is
obtained from the one-parameter family of boundary values
<math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. We now split this boundary value into two
classical constituents and compute its second variation in <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by
quadratures. Throughout, <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(1-Z)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(1-Z)/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=s-\frac{\alpha}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=s-\frac{\alpha}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=s+\frac{\alpha}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=s+\frac{\alpha}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is real, so <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; the
degenerate lattice <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s\in\mathbb{Z}_{\leq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s\in\mathbb{Z}_{\leq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is excluded.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S7.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(0;Z)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(s,s;2s;2)=e^{i\pi s/2}\,\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})}{\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>π</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(0;Z)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(s,s;2s;2)=e^{i\pi s/2}\,\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})}{\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)/\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)/\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The quadratic transformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Sec. 3.1]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;2b;z)=(1-z)^{-a/2}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\Bigl(\frac{a}{2},\,b-\frac{a}{2};\,b+\frac{1}{2};\,\frac{z^{2}}{4(z-1)}\Bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo rspace="0.337em">;</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;2b;z)=(1-z)^{-a/2}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\Bigl(\frac{a}{2},\,b-\frac{a}{2};\,b+\frac{1}{2};\,\frac{z^{2}}{4(z-1)}\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">at <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b=s</annotation></semantics></math> has transformed argument
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{2}/(4(z-1))\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{2}/(4(z-1))\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\to 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\to 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the right-hand Gauss series is
continuous up to argument <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> because there
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}(c-a-b)=\frac{1}{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}(c-a-b)=\frac{1}{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; Gauss’s summation gives
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(\frac{s}{2},\frac{s}{2};s+\frac{1}{2};1)=\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1}{2})/\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(\frac{s}{2},\frac{s}{2};s+\frac{1}{2};1)=\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1}{2})/\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Approaching from <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}z&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}z&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1-z)\to-i\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1-z)\to-i\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S7.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(1-z)^{-s/2}\to e^{i\pi s/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-z)^{-s/2}\to e^{i\pi s/2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}s&lt;\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}s&lt;\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s\notin\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s\notin\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(43)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)=\frac{\Gamma(2s)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\bigl[\mathsf{S}(\alpha;s)+i\pi\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)=\frac{\Gamma(2s)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\bigl[\mathsf{S}(\alpha;s)+i\pi\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)={}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1),\qquad\mathsf{S}(\alpha;s)=\sum_{n\geq 0}(-1)^{n}\frac{(a)_{n}(b)_{n}}{(n!)^{2}}\bigl[2\psi(n+1)-\psi(a+n)-\psi(b+n)\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)={}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1),\qquad\mathsf{S}(\alpha;s)=\sum_{n\geq 0}(-1)^{n}\frac{(a)_{n}(b)_{n}}{(n!)^{2}}\bigl[2\psi(n+1)-\psi(a+n)-\psi(b+n)\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both series converging absolutely. In particular, for real <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> the
imaginary part of the boundary value is
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\Gamma(2s)\,{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)/(\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\Gamma(2s)\,{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)/(\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b))</annotation></semantics></math>.
At the Legendre point <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> both blocks have closed forms:</span></p>
<table id="S7.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(44)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}(0;s)=\frac{\Gamma(1+\frac{s}{2})}{\Gamma(1+s)\,\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})}\quad\text{\emph{(Kummer)}},\qquad\mathsf{S}(0;s)=\cos\frac{\pi s}{2}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})\,\Gamma(s)^{2}}{\Gamma(2s)\,\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext><em class="ltx_emph">(Kummer)</em></mtext><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mi>π</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}(0;s)=\frac{\Gamma(1+\frac{s}{2})}{\Gamma(1+s)\,\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})}\quad\text{\emph{(Kummer)}},\qquad\mathsf{S}(0;s)=\cos\frac{\pi s}{2}\cdot\frac{\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma(s+\frac{1}{2})\,\Gamma(s)^{2}}{\Gamma(2s)\,\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{n}(a,b)=2\psi(n+1)-\psi(a+n)-\psi(b+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{n}(a,b)=2\psi(n+1)-\psi(a+n)-\psi(b+n)</annotation></semantics></math>. The zero-balanced
connection formula
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Sec. 2.3]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Eq. 15.8.10]</cite>)
gives, for <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|1-z|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|1-z|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\arg(1-z)|&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\arg(1-z)|&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;a+b;z)=\frac{\Gamma(a+b)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\sum_{n\geq 0}\frac{(a)_{n}(b)_{n}}{(n!)^{2}}\bigl[R_{n}(a,b)-\log(1-z)\bigr](1-z)^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;a+b;z)=\frac{\Gamma(a+b)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\sum_{n\geq 0}\frac{(a)_{n}(b)_{n}}{(n!)^{2}}\bigl[R_{n}(a,b)-\log(1-z)\bigr](1-z)^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">On the upper approach <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\to 2+i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\to 2+i0</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-z\to-1-i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-z\to-1-i0</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S7.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1-z)\to-i\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1-z)\to-i\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-z)^{n}\to(-1)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-z)^{n}\to(-1)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>; the two limiting series are
absolutely convergent for <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}(a+b)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}(a+b)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> since their terms are
<math id="S7.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{\operatorname{Re}(a+b)-2}\log n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{\operatorname{Re}(a+b)-2}\log n)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the boundary value is attained
by Abel continuity along the path. The digamma-free part is the Taylor
series of <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)_{n}=n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)_{n}=n!</annotation></semantics></math>. The first evaluation
in (<a href="#S7.E44" title="In Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>) is Kummer’s theorem
<math id="S7.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1+a-b;-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1+a-b;-1)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=b=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=b=s</annotation></semantics></math>; the second follows from
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 7.6. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> by taking real parts in (<a href="#S7.E43" title="In Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">43</span></a>).
The <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> kernel <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\psi(1)-\psi(a)-\psi(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\psi(1)-\psi(a)-\psi(b)</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the constant
<math id="S7.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(a,b)=-2\gamma-\psi(a)-\psi(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(a,b)=-2\gamma-\psi(a)-\psi(b)</annotation></semantics></math> governing the zero-balanced asymptotics
studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.8</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and generic <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(45)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(s-1)(4s^{2}-\alpha^{2})\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s+1)+2\alpha^{2}(2s-1)\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)+s\bigl(4(s-1)^{2}-\alpha^{2}\bigr)\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s-1)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(s-1)(4s^{2}-\alpha^{2})\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s+1)+2\alpha^{2}(2s-1)\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)+s\bigl(4(s-1)^{2}-\alpha^{2}\bigr)\,\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s-1)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> this degenerates to the two-term relation
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4s\,\mathsf{K}(0;s+1)+(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}(0;s-1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4s\,\mathsf{K}(0;s+1)+(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}(0;s-1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, which is also satisfied by
the closed form of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}(0;s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}(0;s)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S7.E44" title="In Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume first that <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}s&lt;-\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}s&lt;-\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the three
series converge absolutely. With <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}=(-1)^{n}(a)_{n}(b)_{n}/(n!)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}=(-1)^{n}(a)_{n}(b)_{n}/(n!)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the shifted
series have termwise ratios
<math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}^{+}=t_{n}(a+n)(b+n)/(ab)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}^{+}=t_{n}(a+n)(b+n)/(ab)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}^{-}=t_{n}(a-1)(b-1)/((a-1+n)(b-1+n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}^{-}=t_{n}(a-1)(b-1)/((a-1+n)(b-1+n))</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the rational function</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r(n)=\frac{-n^{2}\,\mathsf{P}(n)}{(a-1+n)(b-1+n)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r(n)=\frac{-n^{2}\,\mathsf{P}(n)}{(a-1+n)(b-1+n)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{P}(n)=8(s-1)n^{2}+8(3s-2)(s-1)n+4(5s-2)(s-1)^{2}+(2-s)\alpha^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{P}(n)=8(s-1)n^{2}+8(3s-2)(s-1)n+4(5s-2)(s-1)^{2}+(2-s)\alpha^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">satisfies the identity</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{+}\,\frac{(a+n)(b+n)}{ab}+c_{0}+c_{-}\,\frac{(a-1)(b-1)}{(a-1+n)(b-1+n)}=-\frac{(a+n)(b+n)}{(n+1)^{2}}\,r(n+1)-r(n)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{+}\,\frac{(a+n)(b+n)}{ab}+c_{0}+c_{-}\,\frac{(a-1)(b-1)}{(a-1+n)(b-1+n)}=-\frac{(a+n)(b+n)}{(n+1)^{2}}\,r(n+1)-r(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{+},c_{0},c_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{+},c_{0},c_{-}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the three coefficients of (<a href="#S7.E45" title="In Lemma 7.8. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>), in
this order (an identity of rational functions of <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,s,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,s,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>).
Multiplying by <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and summing over <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the right side telescopes:
<math id="S7.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{n}r(n)=O(n^{2\operatorname{Re}s})\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{n}r(n)=O(n^{2\operatorname{Re}s})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves
(<a href="#S7.E45" title="In Lemma 7.8. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) for <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}s&lt;-\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}s&lt;-\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the general
case follows by analytic continuation in <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, since
<math id="S7.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;1;-1)</annotation></semantics></math> is an entire function of <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>
degeneration follows since the middle coefficient carries <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;
that the closed form of <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}(0;s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}(0;s)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies it is the elementary computation
<math id="S7.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{K}(0;s+1)/\mathsf{K}(0;s-1)=-(s-1)/(4s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>𝖪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}(0;s+1)/\mathsf{K}(0;s-1)=-(s-1)/(4s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.9</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{2}(s):=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)\big|_{\alpha=0}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{n\geq 1}(-1)^{n}\Bigl(\frac{(s)_{n}}{n!}\Bigr)^{\!2}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{(s+j)^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>𝖪</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{2}(s):=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathsf{K}(\alpha;s)\big|_{\alpha=0}=-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{n\geq 1}(-1)^{n}\Bigl(\frac{(s)_{n}}{n!}\Bigr)^{\!2}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{(s+j)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a series with rational terms. Then:</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies the inhomogeneous two-term recurrence</span></p>
<table id="S7.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(46)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8s^{2}(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s+1)+2s(s-1)^{2}\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s-1)=4(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)-2(2s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)+s\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8s^{2}(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s+1)+2s(s-1)^{2}\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s-1)=4(s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)-2(2s-1)\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)+s\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with </span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{0}=\mathsf{K}(0;\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{0}=\mathsf{K}(0;\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Equivalently, the normalised quotient
</span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\mathsf{K}_{2}/\mathsf{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\mathsf{K}_{2}/\mathsf{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> obeys the exact difference equation</span></p>
<table id="S7.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(47)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s+1)-Q(s-1)=\mathsf{f}(s),\qquad\mathsf{f}(s)=-\frac{2s-1}{2s^{2}(s-1)^{2}}-\frac{(2s-1)\,\mathsf{r}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s+1)-Q(s-1)=\mathsf{f}(s),\qquad\mathsf{f}(s)=-\frac{2s-1}{2s^{2}(s-1)^{2}}-\frac{(2s-1)\,\mathsf{r}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
</span><math id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)/\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)=2\,\Gamma(1+\frac{s}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1-s}{2})\big/\bigl(\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1+s}{2})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)/\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)=2\,\Gamma(1+\frac{s}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1-s}{2})\big/\bigl(\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})\Gamma(\frac{1+s}{2})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix1.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On each lattice </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{0}-2k\}_{k\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{0}-2k\}_{k\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is bounded
and </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}(\varsigma)=O(\varsigma^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ς</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}(\varsigma)=O(\varsigma^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, so
</span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}(s-1-2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}(s-1-2k)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> converges absolutely and</span></p>
<table id="S7.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(48)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{2}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)\Bigl[c_{*}(s_{0})+\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}(s-1-2k)\Bigr]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{2}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)\Bigl[c_{*}(s_{0})+\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}(s-1-2k)\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with a constant </span><math id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}(s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}(s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> depending only on the lattice. This constant
is in fact zero (Theorem </span><a href="#A1.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem A.12. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a><span id="S7.I1.ix2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)_{n}(b)_{n}=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}\bigl((s+j)^{2}-\alpha^{2}/4\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)_{n}(b)_{n}=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}\bigl((s+j)^{2}-\alpha^{2}/4\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,
one has</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\bigl[(a)_{n}(b)_{n}\bigr]_{\alpha=0}=-\frac{1}{2}(s)_{n}^{2}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{(s+j)^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\bigl[(a)_{n}(b)_{n}\bigr]_{\alpha=0}=-\frac{1}{2}(s)_{n}^{2}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{(s+j)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">which gives the series for <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. (i) Differentiate (<a href="#S7.E45" title="In Lemma 7.8. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">45</span></a>) twice at <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>: the
first <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives vanish by evenness, and the <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-parts of
the three coefficients contribute
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-8(s-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-8(s-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(2s-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(2s-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2s</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving
(<a href="#S7.E46" title="In item (i) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>) after division by <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Dividing (<a href="#S7.E46" title="In item (i) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>) by
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8s^{2}(s-1)\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8s^{2}(s-1)\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and eliminating <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1)</annotation></semantics></math> through
the two-term relation of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 7.8. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a> yields (<a href="#S7.E47" title="In item (i) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>).
(ii) By reflection and Stirling,
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma(\frac{1-s}{2})/\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})\sim(|s|/2)^{-1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\frac{1-s}{2})/\Gamma(1-\frac{s}{2})\sim(|s|/2)^{-1/2}</annotation></semantics></math> while the
quotient of the two left-hand Gammas is
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|s|^{1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|s|^{1/2})</annotation></semantics></math> times a trigonometric factor that is periodic on the
lattice, so <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded there; summing (<a href="#S7.E47" title="In item (i) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) in
steps of two telescopes to (<a href="#S7.E48" title="In item (ii) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>), the general solution of
the homogeneous equation on a fixed lattice being
<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{0}\times\mathrm{const}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>const</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{0}\times\mathrm{const}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.10</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix real <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(1-Z)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(1-Z)/2</annotation></semantics></math> generic, and let</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(s)=\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}{\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(s)=\frac{1}{2}\,\frac{\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}{\mathcal{H}(0;Z)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the function on the right-hand side of (<a href="#S7.E39" title="In Corollary 7.4. ‣ Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">39</span></a>). Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(49)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=\frac{\pi\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s)}{2\,\mathsf{S}(0;s)}-\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\Bigl(\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}+\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=\frac{\pi\,\mathsf{K}_{2}(s)}{2\,\mathsf{S}(0;s)}-\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\Bigl(\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}+\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every term on the right except <math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is in closed form (<math id="S7.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}(0;s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}(0;s)</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#S7.E44" title="In Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">44</span></a>)).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}=\mathsf{G}\,(\mathsf{S}+i\pi\mathsf{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖦</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖲</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖪</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}=\mathsf{G}\,(\mathsf{S}+i\pi\mathsf{K})</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{G}=\Gamma(2s)/(\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{G}=\Gamma(2s)/(\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b))</annotation></semantics></math>, by
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 7.7. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>. All three factors are even in <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, so
first <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives vanish at <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\,\mathcal{A}=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathcal{H}|_{0}=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathsf{G}|_{0}+\bigl(\mathsf{S}_{2}+i\pi\mathsf{K}_{2}\bigr)/\bigl(\mathsf{S}_{0}+i\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝖦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\,\mathcal{A}=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathcal{H}|_{0}=\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathsf{G}|_{0}+\bigl(\mathsf{S}_{2}+i\pi\mathsf{K}_{2}\bigr)/\bigl(\mathsf{S}_{0}+i\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2},\mathsf{K}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2},\mathsf{K}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the second
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives and <math id="S7.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{0},\mathsf{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{0},\mathsf{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the values. Since
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathsf{G}|_{0}=-\frac{1}{2}\psi^{\prime}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝖦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\alpha}^{2}\log\mathsf{G}|_{0}=-\frac{1}{2}\psi^{\prime}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is real, taking real
and imaginary parts and using
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}/\mathsf{S}_{0}=\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{H}(0)/\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{H}(0)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}/\mathsf{S}_{0}=\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{H}(0)/\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{H}(0)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 7.6. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>) to eliminate
<math id="S7.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> between the two resulting equations gives
(<a href="#S7.E49" title="In Corollary 7.10. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now separate the quadrature (<a href="#S7.E48" title="In item (ii) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) into a rational
part, which is summed in closed form, and a part which is the product
of a single oscillatory factor and a function with an asymptotic
expansion in powers of <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.11</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The forcing in (<a href="#S7.E47" title="In item (i) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">47</span></a>) splits as</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}=\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}+\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma},\qquad\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s)=\frac{1}{2s^{2}}-\frac{1}{2(s-1)^{2}},\qquad\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(s)=-\frac{(2s-1)\,\mathsf{r}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}=\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}+\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma},\qquad\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s)=\frac{1}{2s^{2}}-\frac{1}{2(s-1)^{2}},\qquad\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(s)=-\frac{(2s-1)\,\mathsf{r}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover the Gamma quotient obeys the multiplicative step-two identity</span></p>
<table id="S7.E50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(50)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}(s)\,\mathsf{r}(s-1)=-\frac{4s}{s-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}(s)\,\mathsf{r}(s-1)=-\frac{4s}{s-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The partial-fraction identity
<math id="S7.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2s^{2}}-\frac{1}{2(s-1)^{2}}=-\frac{2s-1}{2s^{2}(s-1)^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2s^{2}}-\frac{1}{2(s-1)^{2}}=-\frac{2s-1}{2s^{2}(s-1)^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is immediate.
Since <math id="S7.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)/\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}(s)=\mathsf{K}_{0}(s)/\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the two-term relation
<math id="S7.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4s\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)+(s-1)\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4s\,\mathsf{K}_{0}(s+1)+(s-1)\mathsf{K}_{0}(s-1)=0</annotation></semantics></math> of
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 7.8. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a> gives (<a href="#S7.E50" title="In Lemma 7.11. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.12</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a field of characteristic zero and let
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(s)\in s^{-2}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(s)\in s^{-2}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a unique
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s)\in s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s)\in s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s+1)-Q(s-1)=f(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s+1)-Q(s-1)=f(s)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the primitive with
zero constant term in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(51)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\frac{1}{2\sinh\partial_{s}}f=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{s}^{-1}f+\sum_{j\geq 1}\frac{(1-2^{2j-1})\mathcal{B}_{2j}}{(2j)!}\,\partial_{s}^{2j-1}f." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\frac{1}{2\sinh\partial_{s}}f=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{s}^{-1}f+\sum_{j\geq 1}\frac{(1-2^{2j-1})\mathcal{B}_{2j}}{(2j)!}\,\partial_{s}^{2j-1}f.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular (<a href="#S7.E51" title="In Proposition 7.12. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>) applies to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}</annotation></semantics></math>; to
apply it to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> one must first separate the
lattice-periodic factor of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.15</span></a> below).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">On formal Laurent series, the central difference is
<math id="S7.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e^{\partial_{s}}-e^{-\partial_{s}}=2\sinh\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\partial_{s}}-e^{-\partial_{s}}=2\sinh\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{x}{\sinh x}=\sum_{j\geq 0}\frac{2(1-2^{2j-1})\mathcal{B}_{2j}}{(2j)!}x^{2j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{x}{\sinh x}=\sum_{j\geq 0}\frac{2(1-2^{2j-1})\mathcal{B}_{2j}}{(2j)!}x^{2j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">its inverse on series with no constant term is (<a href="#S7.E51" title="In Proposition 7.12. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>).
The leading operator is <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is bijective from
<math id="S7.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-2}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-2}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math>; this also proves
existence and uniqueness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 7.13</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The unique formal solution of</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\rm rat}(s+1)-Q_{\rm rat}(s-1)=\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s),\qquad Q_{\rm rat}(s)\in s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\rm rat}(s+1)-Q_{\rm rat}(s-1)=\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s),\qquad Q_{\rm rat}(s)\in s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is also the distinguished analytic solution which tends to zero along a
leftward step-two lattice. It has the closed form</span></p>
<table id="S7.E52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(52)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\rm rat}(s)=\frac{1}{8}\left[\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right)-\psi^{\prime}\!\left(1-\frac{s}{2}\right)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\rm rat}(s)=\frac{1}{8}\left[\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right)-\psi^{\prime}\!\left(1-\frac{s}{2}\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently,
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\rm rat}(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s-1-2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\rm rat}(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(s-1-2k)</annotation></semantics></math>,
the series converging whenever the lattice avoids the poles.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">From <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(\varsigma)=\frac{1}{2}(\varsigma^{-2}-(\varsigma-1)^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ς</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ς</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ς</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}(\varsigma)=\frac{1}{2}(\varsigma^{-2}-(\varsigma-1)^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k\geq 0}(a+2k)^{-2}=\frac{1}{4}\psi^{\prime}(a/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\geq 0}(a+2k)^{-2}=\frac{1}{4}\psi^{\prime}(a/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, the lattice sum is
<math id="S7.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{4}[\psi^{\prime}(\frac{1-s}{2})-\psi^{\prime}(1-\frac{s}{2})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{4}[\psi^{\prime}(\frac{1-s}{2})-\psi^{\prime}(1-\frac{s}{2})]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
(<a href="#S7.E52" title="In Corollary 7.13. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>). Alternatively, the trigamma recurrence
<math id="S7.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}(z+1)=\psi^{\prime}(z)-z^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}(z+1)=\psi^{\prime}(z)-z^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> shows directly that the central difference of
(<a href="#S7.E52" title="In Corollary 7.13. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) is <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}_{\rm rat}</annotation></semantics></math>. Its asymptotic expansion has no
constant term, so uniqueness follows from
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 7.12. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.12</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.14</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On functions, the kernel of the central-difference operator contains
all <math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-periodic functions; the constant <math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}(s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}(s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S7.E48" title="In item (ii) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) reflects this ambiguity. On formal series in
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}\mathbb{K}[[s^{-1}]]</annotation></semantics></math> the kernel is zero.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.15</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Put</span></p>
<table id="S7.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(53)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s):=s\left[\frac{\Gamma(s/2)}{\Gamma((1+s)/2)}\right]^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s):=s\left[\frac{\Gamma(s/2)}{\Gamma((1+s)/2)}\right]^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(54)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}(s)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}(s)=\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a canonical Poincaré expansion</span></p>
<table id="S7.E55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(55)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s)\sim 2\exp\left(2\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\bigl[\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(0)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(\tfrac{1}{2})\bigr]}{n(n+1)}\left(\frac{2}{s}\right)^{\!n}\right)\in 2+s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s)\sim 2\exp\left(2\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\bigl[\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(0)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(\tfrac{1}{2})\bigr]}{n(n+1)}\left(\frac{2}{s}\right)^{\!n}\right)\in 2+s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus every non-formal dependence of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝗋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> on the step-two lattice is
carried by the single factor <math id="S7.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan(\pi s/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan(\pi s/2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The reflection formulas</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{1+s}{2}\Bigr)=\frac{\pi}{\cos(\pi s/2)},\qquad\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)\Gamma\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)=\frac{\pi}{\sin(\pi s/2)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{1+s}{2}\Bigr)=\frac{\pi}{\cos(\pi s/2)},\qquad\Gamma\Bigl(\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)\Gamma\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)=\frac{\pi}{\sin(\pi s/2)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">together with <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(1+s/2)=(s/2)\Gamma(s/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(1+s/2)=(s/2)\Gamma(s/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, give
(<a href="#S7.E54" title="In Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>). Formula (<a href="#S7.E55" title="In Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) is the standard
formal logarithmic expansion of the Gamma quotient at <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=s/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=s/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
<em id="S7.SS3.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">squared</em> (whence the factor <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in the exponent), followed by
exponentiation; the leading term is <math id="S7.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\cdot(s/2)^{-1}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\cdot(s/2)^{-1}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.16</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the formal forcing</span></p>
<table id="S7.E56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(56)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s):=-\frac{(2s-1)\,\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)}\in s^{-2}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s):=-\frac{(2s-1)\,\widehat{\mathsf{r}}(s)}{4s^{2}(s-1)}\in s^{-2}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and let</span></p>
<table id="S7.E57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(57)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}:=\frac{1}{2\sinh\partial_{s}}\,\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}\in s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}:=\frac{1}{2\sinh\partial_{s}}\,\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}\in s^{-1}\mathbb{Q}[[s^{-1}]].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a fixed step-two lattice avoiding the poles, the Gamma part of the
convergent quadrature has the exact factorisation</span></p>
<table id="S7.E58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(58)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E58.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)=-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2}\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>cot</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)=-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2}\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the upper half-plane the second sum is asymptotic to
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> up to an exponentially small error
(Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a>(c)); this expansion holds in sectors off the
real axis, not on real step-two lattices.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.15</span></a>,
<math id="S7.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(\varsigma)=\tan(\pi\varsigma/2)\,\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(\varsigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖿</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ς</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ς</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ς</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{f}_{\Gamma}(\varsigma)=\tan(\pi\varsigma/2)\,\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(\varsigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varsigma=s-1-2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ς</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varsigma=s-1-2k</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan\frac{\pi(s-1-2k)}{2}=-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan\frac{\pi(s-1-2k)}{2}=-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">independently of <math id="S7.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves (<a href="#S7.E58" title="In Proposition 7.16. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">58</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.17</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=\mathsf{K}_{2}/\mathsf{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=\mathsf{K}_{2}/\mathsf{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S7.E59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(59)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On a fixed step-two lattice the quadrature (<a href="#S7.E48" title="In item (ii) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) reads</span></p>
<table id="S7.E60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(60)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(s)=c_{*}(s_{0})+Q_{\rm rat}(s)-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2}\,T(s)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(s)=c_{*}(s_{0})+Q_{\rm rat}(s)-\cot\frac{\pi s}{2}\,T(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\rm rat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\rm rat}</annotation></semantics></math> given by (<a href="#S7.E52" title="In Corollary 7.13. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>), and the identity
(<a href="#S7.E49" title="In Corollary 7.10. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) is equivalent to</span></p>
<table id="S7.E61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(61)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=-\frac{1}{2}\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\mathcal{E}(s)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=-\frac{1}{2}\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\mathcal{E}(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem17.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S7.E62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(62)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(s)=\frac{c_{*}(s_{0})}{2}+\frac{Q_{\rm rat}(s)}{2}-\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}-\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(s)=\frac{c_{*}(s_{0})}{2}+\frac{Q_{\rm rat}(s)}{2}-\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}-\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since
<math id="S7.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}/\mathsf{S}_{0}=\tan(\pi s/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\mathsf{K}_{0}/\mathsf{S}_{0}=\tan(\pi s/2)</annotation></semantics></math>,
formula (<a href="#S7.E49" title="In Corollary 7.10. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) reads</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{2}\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,Q(s)-\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\left(\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}+\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{2}\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,Q(s)-\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\left(\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}+\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{4}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">The decomposition (<a href="#S7.E60" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">60</span></a>) is (<a href="#S7.E48" title="In item (ii) ‣ Theorem 7.9. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) combined with
Corollary <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary 7.13. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.13</span></a> and (<a href="#S7.E58" title="In Proposition 7.16. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">58</span></a>). Inserting
it, the product of tangent and cotangent is <math id="S7.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and
(<a href="#S7.E61" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">61</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E62" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">62</span></a>) follow.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.18</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>,
the series <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the asymptotic
expansion of</p>
<table id="S7.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{t\,e^{2st}}{\sinh 2t}\,dt=\frac{1}{8}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right),\qquad\operatorname{Re}s&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{\infty}\frac{t\,e^{2st}}{\sinh 2t}\,dt=\frac{1}{8}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right),\qquad\operatorname{Re}s&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.2" class="ltx_p">On the real axis the function <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> does not
coincide with this function: at <math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> the value
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}=-\pi^{2}/48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}=-\pi^{2}/48</annotation></semantics></math> is finite
(Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a> below), whereas
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> has a double pole there. We denote the
difference by</p>
<table id="S7.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(s):=\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}(s)-\frac{1}{8}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(s):=\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}(s)-\frac{1}{8}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\left(\frac{1-s}{2}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem18.p1.3" class="ltx_p">It is evaluated on the real axis in
Subsections <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a> and <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> and, under
a periodicity hypothesis, in closed form in
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem28" title="Proposition 7.28. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.28</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4. </span>Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the boundary analysis it is convenient to pass to the symmetric
normalisation <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\alpha/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\alpha/2</annotation></semantics></math>: put</p>
<table id="S7.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}(\eta;z):={}_{2}F_{1}(s-\eta,s+\eta;2s;z),\qquad\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)=Y_{s}(\alpha/2;2^{+})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}(\eta;z):={}_{2}F_{1}(s-\eta,s+\eta;2s;z),\qquad\mathcal{H}(\alpha;Z)=Y_{s}(\alpha/2;2^{+}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S7.Ex85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{8}\left.\partial_{\eta}^{2}\log Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\right|_{\eta=0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\frac{1}{8}\left.\partial_{\eta}^{2}\log Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\right|_{\eta=0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and write, at the upper boundary value <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2^{+}=2+i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2^{+}=2+i0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S7.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex86"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{s}^{[0]}=Y_{s}(0;2^{+}),\qquad\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}=\partial_{z}Y_{s}(0;2^{+})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{s}^{[0]}=Y_{s}(0;2^{+}),\qquad\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}=\partial_{z}Y_{s}(0;2^{+}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex87"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{\eta}^{2}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\big|_{\eta=0},\qquad\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{z}\partial_{\eta}^{2}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\big|_{\eta=0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{\eta}^{2}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\big|_{\eta=0},\qquad\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{z}\partial_{\eta}^{2}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\big|_{\eta=0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S7.Ex88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s}=\frac{Y_{s}^{[2]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}},\qquad\mathfrak{p}_{s}=\frac{\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}},\qquad J_{s}=\frac{\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}}-\frac{Y_{s}^{[2]}\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}}{(Y_{s}^{[0]})^{2}}=\partial_{z}R_{s}(2^{+})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s}=\frac{Y_{s}^{[2]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}},\qquad\mathfrak{p}_{s}=\frac{\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}},\qquad J_{s}=\frac{\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}}-\frac{Y_{s}^{[2]}\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}}{(Y_{s}^{[0]})^{2}}=\partial_{z}R_{s}(2^{+}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=R_{s}/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=R_{s}/8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(s)</annotation></semantics></math> of Remark <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem18" title="Remark 7.18. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a> is
<math id="S7.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{8}\operatorname{Re}R_{s}-\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}(\frac{1-s}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{8}\operatorname{Re}R_{s}-\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}(\frac{1-s}{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. All identities
below hold first away from parameter poles and then by meromorphic
continuation; the branch is unchanged throughout because every function is
continued to the same upper boundary value.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.19</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Write <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\uparrow}(a,b)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;a+b;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\uparrow}(a,b)={}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;a+b;2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(63)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\,{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a+1,b;a+b;2)+(b-a)\,F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+b\,{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a-1,b;a+b;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\,{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a+1,b;a+b;2)+(b-a)\,F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+b\,{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a-1,b;a+b;2)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and symmetrically in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently the argument derivative is
eliminated:</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(64)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\,\partial_{z}{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;a+b;z)\big|_{z=2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\,\partial_{z}{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b;a+b;z)\big|_{z=2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.E64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-b\bigl(F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a-1,b;a+b;2)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-b\bigl(F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a-1,b;a+b;2)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex89"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-a\bigl(F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b-1;a+b;2)\bigr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-a\bigl(F^{\uparrow}(a,b)+{}_{2}F_{1}^{\uparrow}(a,b-1;a+b;2)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Gauss’s contiguous relation
<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(z-1)F(a+1,b;c;z)+(2a-c-az+bz)F(a,b;c;z)+(c-a)F(a-1,b;c;z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(z-1)F(a+1,b;c;z)+(2a-c-az+bz)F(a,b;c;z)+(c-a)F(a-1,b;c;z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> at <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=a+b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=a+b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2</annotation></semantics></math> gives
(<a href="#S7.E63" title="In Lemma 7.19. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>). The derivative contiguities
<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="zF^{\prime}=a(F(a+1,b;c;z)-F)=b(F(a,b+1;c;z)-F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">zF^{\prime}=a(F(a+1,b;c;z)-F)=b(F(a,b+1;c;z)-F)</annotation></semantics></math> are standard
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Sec. 15.5]</cite>; substituting (<a href="#S7.E63" title="In Lemma 7.19. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">63</span></a>) and its
<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>-exchange gives (<a href="#S7.E64" title="In Lemma 7.19. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">64</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The lower-parameter contiguity
<math id="S7.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\vartheta+c)F(a,b;c+1;z)=cF(a,b;c;z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\vartheta+c)F(a,b;c+1;z)=cF(a,b;c;z)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\vartheta=z\partial_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta=z\partial_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, combined
with <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{z}F(a,b;c;z)=\frac{ab}{c}F(a+1,b+1;c+1;z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{z}F(a,b;c;z)=\frac{ab}{c}F(a+1,b+1;c+1;z)</annotation></semantics></math>, gives the
first <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>-shift of the zero-balanced family,</p>
<table id="S7.E65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(65)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\vartheta+2s+1)\,Y_{s+1}(\eta;z)=\frac{2s(2s+1)}{s^{2}-\eta^{2}}\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;z)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\vartheta+2s+1)\,Y_{s+1}(\eta;z)=\frac{2s(2s+1)}{s^{2}-\eta^{2}}\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;z).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.20</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With <math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d_{s}(\eta)=s^{2}-\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d_{s}(\eta)=s^{2}-\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(66)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\,(\vartheta+2s)(\vartheta+2s+1)\,Y_{s+1}(\eta;z)=2s(2s+1)\bigl((\vartheta+s)^{2}-\eta^{2}\bigr)Y_{s}(\eta;z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\,(\vartheta+2s)(\vartheta+2s+1)\,Y_{s+1}(\eta;z)=2s(2s+1)\bigl((\vartheta+s)^{2}-\eta^{2}\bigr)Y_{s}(\eta;z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">an identity of power series at <math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence of upper boundary values. At
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2</annotation></semantics></math> it is functionally independent of (<a href="#S7.E65" title="In 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">65</span></a>):
after use of the two hypergeometric equations, the determinant of the
coefficients of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.Thmtheorem20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4d^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4d^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The quotient of the <math id="S7.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th Taylor coefficients of <math id="S7.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex90" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(n+s)^{2}-\eta^{2}}{s^{2}-\eta^{2}}\cdot\frac{2s(2s+1)}{(n+2s)(n+2s+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(n+s)^{2}-\eta^{2}}{s^{2}-\eta^{2}}\cdot\frac{2s(2s+1)}{(n+2s)(n+2s+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is (<a href="#S7.E66" title="In Proposition 7.20. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">66</span></a>) coefficientwise, since
<math id="S7.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta z^{n}=nz^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta z^{n}=nz^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.21</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At the upper boundary value, the pair
(<a href="#S7.E65" title="In 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">65</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E66" title="In Proposition 7.20. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">66</span></a>)
reduces to the two boundary rows</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.E67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(67)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\,\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)+(2s+1)Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)=\frac{2s(2s+1)}{d}\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\,\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)+(2s+1)Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)=\frac{2s(2s+1)}{d}\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex91"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\,\bigl\{4(s-1)\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)+2(s^{2}-s-1+\eta^{2})Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)\bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\,\bigl\{4(s-1)\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)+2(s^{2}-s-1+\eta^{2})Y_{s+1}(\eta;2)\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.E68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(68)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E68.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad=2s(2s+1)\bigl\{-2\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2)-d\,Y_{s}(\eta;2)\bigr\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad=2s(2s+1)\bigl\{-2\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2)-d\,Y_{s}(\eta;2)\bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">an invertible two-by-two system. Eliminating <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the
scalar transfer</span></p>
<table id="S7.E69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(69)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}(\eta;2^{+})=\frac{s(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\bigl[2s\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})+d\,Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}(\eta;2^{+})=\frac{s(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\bigl[2s\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})+d\,Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, twice differentiated in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> at zero,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(70)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}^{[2]}=\frac{2s+1}{s^{3}}\bigl(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[2]}-2Y_{s}^{[0]}\bigr)+\frac{4(2s+1)}{s^{5}}\bigl(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[0]}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}^{[2]}=\frac{2s+1}{s^{3}}\bigl(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[2]}-2Y_{s}^{[0]}\bigr)+\frac{4(2s+1)}{s^{5}}\bigl(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[0]}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s}(\eta)=\bigl(Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+}),\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\bigr)^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s}(\eta)=\bigl(Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+}),\,\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})\bigr)^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(71)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s+1}(\eta)=M_{s}(\eta)\,U_{s}(\eta),\qquad M_{s}(\eta)=\begin{pmatrix}\dfrac{s(2s+1)}{d}&amp;\dfrac{2s^{2}(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\\[8.53581pt]
-\dfrac{s(2s+1)^{2}}{2d}&amp;\dfrac{s(2s+1)\{d-s(2s+1)\}}{d^{2}}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s+1}(\eta)=M_{s}(\eta)\,U_{s}(\eta),\qquad M_{s}(\eta)=\begin{pmatrix}\dfrac{s(2s+1)}{d}&amp;\dfrac{2s^{2}(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\\[8.53581pt]
-\dfrac{s(2s+1)^{2}}{2d}&amp;\dfrac{s(2s+1)\{d-s(2s+1)\}}{d^{2}}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, since <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is even in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the second derivatives with
respect to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S7.E72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(72)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s+1}^{[2]}=M_{s}(0)\,U_{s}^{[2]}+M_{s}^{\prime\prime}(0)\,U_{s}(0),\qquad U_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{\eta}^{2}U_{s}(\eta)\big|_{\eta=0}=(Y_{s}^{[2]},\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]})^{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s+1}^{[2]}=M_{s}(0)\,U_{s}^{[2]}+M_{s}^{\prime\prime}(0)\,U_{s}(0),\qquad U_{s}^{[2]}=\partial_{\eta}^{2}U_{s}(\eta)\big|_{\eta=0}=(Y_{s}^{[2]},\dot{Y}_{s}^{[2]})^{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The hypergeometric equation for <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluated at
<math id="S7.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2</annotation></semantics></math>, converts the second-order operators in
(<a href="#S7.E66" title="In Proposition 7.20. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">66</span></a>) into the displayed first-order
boundary rows; (<a href="#S7.E67" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">67</span></a>) is
(<a href="#S7.E65" title="In 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">65</span></a>) at <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The determinant of the system is
proportional to <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; elimination gives
(<a href="#S7.E69" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>), and the first row returns
<math id="S7.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{z}Y_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. (<a href="#S7.E71" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>). Formula
(<a href="#S7.E70" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">70</span></a>) is
<math id="S7.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\eta}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∂</mo><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\eta}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of (<a href="#S7.E69" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>) at <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math> using
<math id="S7.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=s^{2}-\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=s^{2}-\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and evenness in <math id="S7.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>; (<a href="#S7.E72" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) is the same statement
for the pair.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.22</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the upper boundary value,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(73)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{p}_{s}=-\frac{s}{2}+\frac{i\,s^{2}(s+\frac{1}{2})}{2(2s+1)}\left[\frac{\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})}{\Gamma(\frac{s+2}{2})}\right]^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{p}_{s}=-\frac{s}{2}+\frac{i\,s^{2}(s+\frac{1}{2})}{2(2s+1)}\left[\frac{\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})}{\Gamma(\frac{s+2}{2})}\right]^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently the coefficients in (<a href="#S7.E72" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) are all explicit, and</span></p>
<table id="S7.E74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(74)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s}=\frac{1}{s}+\frac{2s\mathfrak{p}_{s}+s^{2}}{2s}\left(R_{s+1}-R_{s}-\frac{4}{s^{2}}\right):" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s}=\frac{1}{s}+\frac{2s\mathfrak{p}_{s}+s^{2}}{2s}\left(R_{s+1}-R_{s}-\frac{4}{s^{2}}\right):</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thus <math id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed through the first difference <math id="S7.Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s+1}-R_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s+1}-R_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">At <math id="S7.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S7.E69" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">69</span></a>) reads
<math id="S7.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}^{[0]}=\frac{2s+1}{s^{3}}(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[0]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}^{[0]}=\frac{2s+1}{s^{3}}(2s\,\dot{Y}_{s}^{[0]}+s^{2}Y_{s}^{[0]})</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S7.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{p}_{s}=\frac{s^{2}}{2(2s+1)}\frac{Y_{s+1}^{[0]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}}-\frac{s}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔭</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{p}_{s}=\frac{s^{2}}{2(2s+1)}\frac{Y_{s+1}^{[0]}}{Y_{s}^{[0]}}-\frac{s}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 7.6. ‣ 7.2. The boundary split and the transverse quadrature ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>,
<math id="S7.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s+1}^{[0]}/Y_{s}^{[0]}=e^{i\pi/2}\,(s+\tfrac{1}{2})\,[\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})/\Gamma(\frac{s+2}{2})]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s+1}^{[0]}/Y_{s}^{[0]}=e^{i\pi/2}\,(s+\tfrac{1}{2})\,[\Gamma(\frac{s+1}{2})/\Gamma(\frac{s+2}{2})]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives
(<a href="#S7.E73" title="In Proposition 7.22. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">73</span></a>). Formula (<a href="#S7.E74" title="In Proposition 7.22. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>) follows by
taking two <math id="S7.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives at <math id="S7.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math> of the logarithm of</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{Y_{s+1}(\eta;2^{+})}{Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}=\frac{s(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\,\Bigl\{2s\,\frac{\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}{Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}+d\Bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{Y_{s+1}(\eta;2^{+})}{Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}=\frac{s(2s+1)}{d^{2}}\,\Bigl\{2s\,\frac{\partial_{z}Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}{Y_{s}(\eta;2^{+})}+d\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 7.23</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The transfer (<a href="#S7.E71" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>) is invertible away from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}=\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}=\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
it is two-dimensional: formula (<a href="#S7.E74" title="In Proposition 7.22. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">74</span></a>) exchanges the
derivative <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for the adjacent value <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> but does not eliminate
it. The recurrence therefore determines <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{s},J_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{s},J_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> on a class
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}+\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}+\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> once the pair <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{s_{*}},J_{s_{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{s_{*}},J_{s_{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> is known at one point
of the class. Terminating points <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=-N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=-N</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be used for this
purpose, because <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s=-2N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s=-2N</annotation></semantics></math> is then a pole of the denominator parameter
away from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math>. The next subsection supplies these initial values,
which we call <em id="S7.Thmtheorem23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anchors</em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5. </span>Exact anchors: polylogarithms at <math id="S7.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
Euler moments for every class</h3>

<div id="S7.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.24</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y_{1}(0;z)={}_{2}F_{1}(1,1;2;z)=-\log(1-z)/z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mn>2</mn><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}(0;z)={}_{2}F_{1}(1,1;2;z)=-\log(1-z)/z</annotation></semantics></math>, so for the upper
boundary convention</span></p>
<table id="S7.E75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(75)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}^{[0]}=\frac{i\pi}{2},\qquad\dot{Y}_{1}^{[0]}=-\frac{1}{2}-\frac{i\pi}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}^{[0]}=\frac{i\pi}{2},\qquad\dot{Y}_{1}^{[0]}=-\frac{1}{2}-\frac{i\pi}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore</span></p>
<table id="S7.E76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(76)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{6}-\frac{7i}{\pi}\,\zeta(3),\qquad J_{1}=2\log 2-\frac{7}{\pi^{2}}\,\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi}{3}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{6}-\frac{7i}{\pi}\,\zeta(3),\qquad J_{1}=2\log 2-\frac{7}{\pi^{2}}\,\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi}{3},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem24.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equivalently</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}^{[2]}=\frac{7}{2}\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi^{3}}{12},\qquad\dot{Y}_{1}^{[2]}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{4}-\frac{7}{4}\zeta(3)+i\Bigl(\pi\log 2+\frac{\pi^{3}}{24}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>12</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>24</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}^{[2]}=\frac{7}{2}\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi^{3}}{12},\qquad\dot{Y}_{1}^{[2]}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{4}-\frac{7}{4}\zeta(3)+i\Bigl(\pi\log 2+\frac{\pi^{3}}{24}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Formulae (<a href="#S7.E75" title="In Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">75</span></a>) are immediate from <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1-z)\to-i\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1-z)\to-i\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Define
<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(u)=\int_{0}^{u}\log^{2}(1-t)\,t^{-1}\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(u)=\int_{0}^{u}\log^{2}(1-t)\,t^{-1}\,dt</annotation></semantics></math>; an antiderivative calculation
gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex94" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(u)=\log^{2}(1-u)\log u+2\log(1-u)\operatorname{Li}_{2}(1-u)-2\operatorname{Li}_{3}(1-u)+2\zeta(3)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(u)=\log^{2}(1-u)\log u+2\log(1-u)\operatorname{Li}_{2}(1-u)-2\operatorname{Li}_{3}(1-u)+2\zeta(3),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">whence, at the upper boundary,
<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(2^{+})=\frac{7}{2}\zeta(3)-\pi^{2}\log 2+\frac{i\pi^{3}}{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(2^{+})=\frac{7}{2}\zeta(3)-\pi^{2}\log 2+\frac{i\pi^{3}}{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. The transverse
variational equation at <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> and reduction of order over the explicit
solution <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{1}(0;z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{1}(0;z)</annotation></semantics></math> yield</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex95" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{1}=-2\int_{0}^{2^{+}}\frac{I(u)}{(1-u)\log^{2}(1-u)}\,du,\qquad J_{1}=-\frac{2}{\pi^{2}}\,I(2^{+})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=-2\int_{0}^{2^{+}}\frac{I(u)}{(1-u)\log^{2}(1-u)}\,du,\qquad J_{1}=-\frac{2}{\pi^{2}}\,I(2^{+}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">which proves the <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> formula. For <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, substitute <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\log(1-u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\log(1-u)</annotation></semantics></math>;
since <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dI/dx=-x^{2}e^{x}/(1-e^{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dI/dx=-x^{2}e^{x}/(1-e^{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, integration by parts gives</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}=2\left[-\frac{I}{x}+x\log(1-e^{x})+\operatorname{Li}_{2}(e^{x})\right]_{x=0}^{x=-i\pi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>I</mi><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}=2\left[-\frac{I}{x}+x\log(1-e^{x})+\operatorname{Li}_{2}(e^{x})\right]_{x=0}^{x=-i\pi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text">and the endpoint values <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Li}_{2}(1)=\pi^{2}/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Li}_{2}(1)=\pi^{2}/6</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Li}_{2}(-1)=-\pi^{2}/12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Li}_{2}(-1)=-\pi^{2}/12</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Li}_{3}(-1)=-\frac{3}{4}\zeta(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Li}_{3}(-1)=-\frac{3}{4}\zeta(3)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(-1-i0)=-i\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(-1-i0)=-i\pi</annotation></semantics></math> give (<a href="#S7.E76" title="In Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">76</span></a>); the <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log 2</annotation></semantics></math> terms cancel
exactly.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a> with the recurrence
(<a href="#S7.E72" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) determines, by finite algebra, the pair
<math id="S7.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{s},J_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{s},J_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> at every positive integer <math id="S7.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>; the first
propagated value is</p>
<table id="S7.E77" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(77)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{2}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{2}+4-\frac{7i}{\pi}\,\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi}{2}\Bigl(4\log 2-\frac{14}{\pi^{2}}\,\zeta(3)-2\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>14</mn><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{2}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{2}+4-\frac{7i}{\pi}\,\zeta(3)-\frac{i\pi}{2}\Bigl(4\log 2-\frac{14}{\pi^{2}}\,\zeta(3)-2\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.25</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\operatorname{Re}s_{*}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\operatorname{Re}s_{*}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s_{*}}^{[0]}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s_{*}}^{[0]}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> put</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex97"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}(t)=t^{s_{*}-1}(1-t)^{s_{*}-1}\bigl(1-(2+i\varepsilon)t\bigr)^{-s_{*}},\qquad\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}(t)=\frac{t}{1-(2+i\varepsilon)t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝗐</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}(t)=t^{s_{*}-1}(1-t)^{s_{*}-1}\bigl(1-(2+i\varepsilon)t\bigr)^{-s_{*}},\qquad\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}(t)=\frac{t}{1-(2+i\varepsilon)t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S7.Ex98"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}(t)=\log t-\log(1-t)+\log\bigl(1-(2+i\varepsilon)t\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}(t)=\log t-\log(1-t)+\log\bigl(1-(2+i\varepsilon)t\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the normalised complex expectation
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}=\int_{0}^{1}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}f\,dt\big/\int_{0}^{1}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝗐</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.055em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝗐</mi><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}=\int_{0}^{1}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}f\,dt\big/\int_{0}^{1}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\,dt</annotation></semantics></math>; all integrals converge absolutely
for every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Write
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle f\rangle_{s_{*}}=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\langle f\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle f\rangle_{s_{*}}=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\langle f\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> whenever the limit exists. Then
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> exists as an absolutely convergent integral
at <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math> (boundary values <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2t-i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2t-i0</annotation></semantics></math>), the <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>-moments exist
as <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> limits (equivalently, as absolutely
convergent integrals over any contour from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> that leaves the
segment near <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> into the upper half-plane), and</span></p>
<table id="S7.E78" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(78)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s_{*}}=-2\psi^{\prime}(s_{*})+\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}},\qquad J_{s_{*}}=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\Bigl[s_{*}\bigl\{\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}^{2}\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}-\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\langle\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\bigr\}-2\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝖳</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s_{*}}=-2\psi^{\prime}(s_{*})+\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}},\qquad J_{s_{*}}=\lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow 0}\Bigl[s_{*}\bigl\{\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}^{2}\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}-\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\langle\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\bigr\}-2\langle\mathsf{L}_{\varepsilon}\mathsf{T}_{\varepsilon}\rangle_{s_{*},\varepsilon}\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently every <em id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">real</em> residual class <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}+\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}+\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> avoiding
the parameter poles and the zeros of the boundary solution carries a
canonical anchor: choose the representative <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}\in s_{0}+\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}\in s_{0}+\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;s_{*}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;s_{*}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the integer class being covered by
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a> at <math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}=1</annotation></semantics></math>; the invertible transfer
(<a href="#S7.E71" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E72" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>) and its inverse then determine
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem25.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(R_{s},J_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(R_{s},J_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> uniquely on the whole class, independently of the
representative used.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Convergence and existence of the limits.</em><span id="S7.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text"> For <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
the factor <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(2+i\varepsilon)t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(2+i\varepsilon)t</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded away from zero on <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
so all moments converge absolutely; at <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math> the integrand of
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msup><mi>𝖫</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathsf{L}^{2}\rangle_{s_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\bigl(|1-2t|^{-\operatorname{Re}s_{*}}\log^{2}|1-2t|\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\bigl(|1-2t|^{-\operatorname{Re}s_{*}}\log^{2}|1-2t|\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> near
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, absolutely integrable for
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}s_{*}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}s_{*}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, while the <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>-moments carry an extra
factor <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-2t-i0)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-2t-i0)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and are <em id="S7.SS5.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> absolutely convergent at
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math>: they must be taken as the stated limits. The limits
exist by contour deformation: the singularity of the integrand sits at
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\varepsilon}=1/(2+i\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\varepsilon}=1/(2+i\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, which lies <em id="S7.SS5.p3.1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">below</em> the real
segment and tends to <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>; the
integrand is analytic in <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> in the upper half-disc around
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> may be replaced, for every
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, by the integral over the contour
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> which follows the segment but crosses a semicircle of
fixed radius <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}\in(0,\tfrac{1}{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}\in(0,\tfrac{1}{4})</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S7.SS5.p3.1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">above</em> <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. On
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> the integrands converge uniformly as
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> (the denominator is bounded away from zero),
so each moment tends to the corresponding absolutely convergent
<math id="S7.SS5.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>-integral, which is independent of <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; this
realises the boundary prescription <math id="S7.SS5.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-2t-i0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-2t-i0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The formulae.</em><span id="S7.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Euler’s integral representation gives, for <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
near zero and <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> interpreted as above,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s_{*}}(\eta;2^{+})=\frac{\Gamma(2s_{*})}{\Gamma(s_{*}+\eta)\Gamma(s_{*}-\eta)}\int_{\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*}}(t)\,e^{\eta\mathsf{L}(t)}\,dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝗐</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s_{*}}(\eta;2^{+})=\frac{\Gamma(2s_{*})}{\Gamma(s_{*}+\eta)\Gamma(s_{*}-\eta)}\int_{\mathcal{C}_{r_{0}}}\mathsf{w}_{s_{*}}(t)\,e^{\eta\mathsf{L}(t)}\,dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The family is even in <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle_{s_{*}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝖫</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathsf{L}\rangle_{s_{*}}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Two
<math id="S7.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>-derivatives of the logarithm give the <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> formula, the
<math id="S7.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2\psi^{\prime}(s_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2\psi^{\prime}(s_{*})</annotation></semantics></math> coming from the Gamma prefactor. For fixed <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>,
logarithmic <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-differentiation of the integral has score <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{*}\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{*}\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> at
<math id="S7.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=0</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{z}\mathsf{L}=-\mathsf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>𝖫</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{z}\mathsf{L}=-\mathsf{T}</annotation></semantics></math>; differentiating the normalised second
moment gives the <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{s_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{s_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> formula, first at <math id="S7.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and then
in the limit, which is (<a href="#S7.E78" title="In Theorem 7.25. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">78</span></a>). The transfer matrix is
invertible away from its explicit divisors, so the anchor propagates
uniquely in both directions; if two admissible representatives are
chosen, Euler’s formula identifies both with the same analytic boundary
solution, and uniqueness of the propagation proves independence of the
choice.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorems <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem25" title="Theorem 7.25. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.25</span></a>, together with the
transfer (<a href="#S7.E71" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">71</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E72" title="In Theorem 7.21. ‣ 7.4. Derivative-free contiguities and the boundary transfer ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">72</span></a>), give <math id="S7.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence
<math id="S7.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=R_{s}/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=R_{s}/8</annotation></semantics></math> and the difference <math id="S7.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(s)</annotation></semantics></math> of
Remark <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem18" title="Remark 7.18. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>, as explicit convergent expressions on
every real class.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.6. </span>The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a></h3>

<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection proves Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>. The real part is
obtained first, directly from Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. The imaginary
part is obtained by an asymptotic matching in the lower half-plane; the
estimates on which it relies are stated in Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a> and
proved in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.26</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As formal series in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(79)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}\,Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+3}{4}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{2}\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}\,Z^{-n}=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+3}{4}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{Z+1}{2}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where each <math id="S7.Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for its Bernoulli asymptotic expansion.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">From <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}(x)=\int_{0}^{\infty}ue^{-xu}(1-e^{-u})^{-1}\,du" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}(x)=\int_{0}^{\infty}ue^{-xu}(1-e^{-u})^{-1}\,du</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=4t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=4t</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4t</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2t</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, the right side of (<a href="#S7.E79" title="In Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>) is the
Watson expansion of <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{\infty}te^{-Zt}\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{\infty}te^{-Zt}\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)\,dt</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex100" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)=\frac{e^{-t}-e^{-3t}}{1-e^{-4t}}+\frac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-2t}}=\frac{1}{2\cosh t}+\frac{1}{2\sinh t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cosh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)=\frac{e^{-t}-e^{-3t}}{1-e^{-4t}}+\frac{e^{-t}}{1-e^{-2t}}=\frac{1}{2\cosh t}+\frac{1}{2\sinh t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Watson’s correspondence <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{n}e^{-Zt}dt=n!\,Z^{-n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{n}e^{-Zt}dt=n!\,Z^{-n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
coefficientwise exact, so the <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th coefficient of the right side of
(<a href="#S7.E79" title="In Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>) is the <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-st Taylor coefficient of
<math id="S7.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>,
<math id="S7.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}\,t^{n-1}/(n-1)!=\operatorname{Re}\Phi(t)/t=te^{t}/\sinh 2t=t(\operatorname{sech}t+\operatorname{csch}t)/2=t\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>csch</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Re}g_{n}\,t^{n-1}/(n-1)!=\operatorname{Re}\Phi(t)/t=te^{t}/\sinh 2t=t(\operatorname{sech}t+\operatorname{csch}t)/2=t\varphi_{\mathrm{R}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>,
which proves (<a href="#S7.E79" title="In Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By the duplication formula <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}(2x)=\frac{1}{4}\bigl[\psi^{\prime}(x)+\psi^{\prime}(x+\frac{1}{2})\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}(2x)=\frac{1}{4}\bigl[\psi^{\prime}(x)+\psi^{\prime}(x+\frac{1}{2})\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>,
the right-hand side of (<a href="#S7.E79" title="In Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">79</span></a>) is also equal to
<math id="S7.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{Z+1}{4}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>; with <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=1-2s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=1-2s</annotation></semantics></math> this is the expression
<math id="S7.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{8}\psi^{\prime}\bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> of Remark <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem18" title="Remark 7.18. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.27</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S7.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix </span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\theta_{0}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\theta_{0}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. For every </span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> there is </span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such
that, uniformly on the bilateral cone
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Re}Z|\leq\theta_{0}\,|\operatorname{Im}Z|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Re}Z|\leq\theta_{0}\,|\operatorname{Im}Z|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Im}Z|\geq y_{\min}(\theta_{0},N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Im}Z|\geq y_{\min}(\theta_{0},N)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (</span><em id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph">both</em><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> signs of
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}Z</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, in particular on every vertical strip)</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl|\mathcal{A}-\sum_{n=1}^{N}g_{n}Z^{-n}\Bigr|\leq C_{N}\,|Z|^{-N-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl|\mathcal{A}-\sum_{n=1}^{N}g_{n}Z^{-n}\Bigr|\leq C_{N}\,|Z|^{-N-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On every closed subsector of
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\arg(\pm is)|&lt;\pi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\arg(\pm is)|&lt;\pi/2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> the closed blocks </span><math id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖪</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{S}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖲</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{S}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{r}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\rm rat}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\rm rat}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the trigamma
functions are asymptotic to their Bernoulli–Stirling series, with
uniform remainders after each truncation.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(c)</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every </span><math id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and every </span><math id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, uniformly on
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}s\geq\delta|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}s\geq\delta|s|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl|T(s)-\sum_{j=1}^{N}\omega_{j}\,s^{-j}\Bigr|\leq C_{\delta,N}\,|s|^{-N-1}+C_{\delta}\,\frac{e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}s}}{\operatorname{Im}s},\qquad T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl|T(s)-\sum_{j=1}^{N}\omega_{j}\,s^{-j}\Bigr|\leq C_{\delta,N}\,|s|^{-N-1}+C_{\delta}\,\frac{e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}s}}{\operatorname{Im}s},\qquad T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}\omega_{j}s^{-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}\omega_{j}s^{-j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the formal series
</span><math id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}=(2\sinh\partial_{s})^{-1}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>sinh</mi><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}=(2\sinh\partial_{s})^{-1}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of
Proposition </span><a href="#S7.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 7.12. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.12</span></a><span id="S7.I2.ix3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The mirror statements hold in the lower half-plane by Schwarz
reflection, with the same (rational) coefficients.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>: (a) is
Proposition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition A.3. ‣ A.1. Coefficient bounds and the bilateral sector expansion ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>, proved from the coefficient bound
<math id="S7.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|G_{n}|\leq C(n+1)!\,2^{-n/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|G_{n}|\leq C(n+1)!\,2^{-n/2}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma A.1. ‣ A.1. Coefficient bounds and the bilateral sector expansion ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), the elementary
product inequality <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j+1)\leq\sqrt{2}\,|Z-j|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j+1)\leq\sqrt{2}\,|Z-j|</annotation></semantics></math> valid on the cone
(Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma A.2. ‣ A.1. Coefficient bounds and the bilateral sector expansion ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>), and the exact function-level identity
(<a href="#S7.E38" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">38</span></a>) between the convergent inverse-factorial series
(<a href="#S1.E6" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and the boundary family <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>; (b) is
Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma A.5. ‣ A.2. Uniform asymptotics of the closed blocks ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>; (c) is
Proposition <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition A.8. ‣ A.3. The Gamma forcing and its lattice sum ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>, proved from a two-regime uniform
expansion of <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> on horizontal lines
(Stirling on <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\arg\varsigma|\leq\tfrac{3}{4}\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ς</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\arg\varsigma|\leq\tfrac{3}{4}\pi</annotation></semantics></math>; reflection, a Beta-integral
bound and <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cot(\pi\varsigma/2)=-i+O(e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}\varsigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cot</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ς</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>ς</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cot(\pi\varsigma/2)=-i+O(e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}\varsigma})</annotation></semantics></math> on
the left regime), the <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq|s|</annotation></semantics></math> / <math id="S7.SS6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;|s|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;|s|</annotation></semantics></math> splitting of the lattice
sum, the uniform Hurwitz–Bernoulli expansions of the moment sums
<math id="S7.SS6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}(s-1-2k)^{-j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}(s-1-2k)^{-j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the formal uniqueness of
Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem12" title="Proposition 7.12. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.12</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition gives a closed form on the real axis for the
difference <math id="S7.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of Remark <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem18" title="Remark 7.18. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>, under a
periodicity hypothesis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.28</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the analytic extension of the difference <math id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of
Remark <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem18" title="Remark 7.18. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.18</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}(s):=\frac{\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}(s)+\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}(s)}{2}-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}(1-s)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}(s):=\frac{\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}(s)+\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}(s)}{2}-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}(1-s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow\!,\downarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.380em;" width="0.380em"><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow\!,\downarrow}</annotation></semantics></math> the two boundary components
of Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. The two-component relation and vertical decay ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, so that
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}=\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}-{}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}=\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}-{}</annotation></semantics></math>(trigamma part) on the real axis.
Assume</span></p>
<ol id="S7.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S7.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(P)</span> 
<div id="S7.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S7.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.I3.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-periodic, with poles confined to the
odd class, of order at most two.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, on the real axis,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(80)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}(s)=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}(1-s)-\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\mathcal{A}(s)=\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(\frac{1-s}{2}\Bigr)-\frac{1}{16}\,\psi^{\prime}\!\Bigl(1-\frac{s}{2}\Bigr)+\frac{1}{4}\,\psi^{\prime}(1-s)-\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with convergent trigamma functions; that is,
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}=-\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently</span></p>
<table id="S7.E81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(81)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(s)=\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}-\frac{\pi^{2}}{4}\,\csc^{2}(\pi s)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>csc</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(s)=\frac{\pi^{2}}{8}\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}-\frac{\pi^{2}}{4}\,\csc^{2}(\pi s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem28.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the identity (<a href="#S7.E61" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">61</span></a>) becomes explicit:</span></p>
<table id="S7.E82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(82)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}(s)=-\frac{1}{2}\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\mathcal{E}(s),\qquad T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\mathcal{A}(s)=-\frac{1}{2}\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\,\mathcal{E}(s),\qquad T(s)=\sum_{k\geq 0}\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s-1-2k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The function <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> decays at both vertical ends of the strip
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}s\in[0,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}s\in[0,2]</annotation></semantics></math>: by the two-component
relation (<a href="#A1.E106" title="In Corollary A.13. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">106</span></a>) and Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a>(a), in
the upper half-plane
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}=-\tfrac{i}{2}X+O(e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}=-\tfrac{i}{2}X+O(e^{-\pi\operatorname{Im}s})</annotation></semantics></math>
while <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotic to the series <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}+\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}+\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow})/2</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotic to
the <em id="S7.SS6.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">real</em> trigamma series of Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.26</span></a>, which
the convergent trigamma part reproduces (Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a>(b));
the lower end is the mirror statement. Under hypothesis (P) the
classification lemma (Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma A.11. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.11</span></a>, with <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>)
gives <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}=c\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}=c\,\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. The
principal part at <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> determines <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>: the trigamma part of <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> has the
double pole
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\bigl[\tfrac{1}{16}\cdot 4+\tfrac{1}{4}\bigr](s-1)^{-2}=-\tfrac{1}{2}(s-1)^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\bigl[\tfrac{1}{16}\cdot 4+\tfrac{1}{4}\bigr](s-1)^{-2}=-\tfrac{1}{2}(s-1)^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
while <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow\!,\downarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.380em;" width="0.380em"><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></mpadded><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">↓</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow\!,\downarrow}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite at <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math>
(Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a>), and
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}\sim 4/(\pi^{2}(s-1)^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>sec</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sec^{2}\frac{\pi s}{2}\sim 4/(\pi^{2}(s-1)^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>; hence <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c=-\pi^{2}/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=-\pi^{2}/8</annotation></semantics></math>. The
finite part at <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=1</annotation></semantics></math> is consistent with the value given by the
polylogarithmic evaluation:
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m20" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Re}R_{1}/8=-\pi^{2}/48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}R_{1}/8=-\pi^{2}/48</annotation></semantics></math>
(Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 7.24. ‣ 7.5. Exact anchors: polylogarithms at 𝑠=1, Euler moments for every class ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.24</span></a>). Formula (<a href="#S7.E81" title="In Proposition 7.28. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">81</span></a>) follows from
(<a href="#S7.E62" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">62</span></a>) with <math id="S7.SS6.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{*}(s_{0})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{*}(s_{0})=0</annotation></semantics></math>
(Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem A.12. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>) and the reflection
<math id="S7.SS6.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}(s)=\pi^{2}\csc^{2}(\pi s)-\psi^{\prime}(1-s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msup><mi>csc</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}(s)=\pi^{2}\csc^{2}(\pi s)-\psi^{\prime}(1-s)</annotation></semantics></math>; and (<a href="#S7.E82" title="In Proposition 7.28. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">82</span></a>) is
(<a href="#S7.E61" title="In Proposition 7.17. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">61</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We describe the structure of the argument; the details are given in
Appendix <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6. Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a>. The two boundary components obey the exact
two-component relation (Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma A.9. ‣ A.4. The two-component relation and vertical decay ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.9</span></a>,
Corollary <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem13" title="Corollary A.13. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.13</span></a>)</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex104" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bigl(\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}-i\Bigr)\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}+\Bigl(\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}+i\Bigr)\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}=-\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\Bigl(Q_{\rm rat}(s)-\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{2}\Bigr)=:X(s)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>rat</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigl(\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}-i\Bigr)\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}+\Bigl(\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}+i\Bigr)\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}=-\,T(s)+\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}\Bigl(Q_{\rm rat}(s)-\frac{\psi^{\prime}(s)}{2}\Bigr)=:X(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS6.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">which uses the vanishing of the lattice constant
(Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem A.12. ‣ A.5. The classification lemma and the vanishing of the lattice constant ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.12</span></a>). Along the vertical line
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=-\tfrac{1}{2}+iY" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=-\tfrac{1}{2}+iY</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\to-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\to-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, one has
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}+i=O(e^{-\pi|Y|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}+i=O(e^{-\pi|Y|})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the term in
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\downarrow}</annotation></semantics></math> is exponentially small, <math id="S7.SS6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}-i\to-2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}-i\to-2i</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotic to <math id="S7.SS6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the
<em id="S7.SS6.p8.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bilateral</em> expansion of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a>(a); hence
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}=\tfrac{i}{2}X+O(e^{-\pi|Y|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{\uparrow}=\tfrac{i}{2}X+O(e^{-\pi|Y|})</annotation></semantics></math>. Expanding <math id="S7.SS6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem27" title="Lemma 7.27. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.27</span></a>(b),(c), with
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}=-i+O(e^{-\pi|Y|})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan\frac{\pi s}{2}=-i+O(e^{-\pi|Y|})</annotation></semantics></math>, and using the formal
reflection parity <math id="S7.SS6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\psi}(1-s)=-S_{\psi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\psi}(1-s)=-S_{\psi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Stirling series of
<math id="S7.SS6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma A.14. ‣ A.6. Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A The sectorial estimates ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.14</span></a>) to put the real part into
trigamma-kernel form, one obtains exactly
(<a href="#S1.E13" title="In Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>): the real part is
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 7.26. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.26</span></a>, and the imaginary part is
(<a href="#S1.E14" title="In Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.29</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem29.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Write the series (<a href="#S7.E56" title="In Proposition 7.16. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>) in powers of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\sum_{n\geq 2}f_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\sum_{n\geq 2}f_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)=\sum_{n\geq 2}f_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)=\sum_{n\geq 2}f_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math> be its Borel transform. Then
the Borel transform of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)/(2\sinh 2t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)/(2\sinh 2t)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex105" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)=-\frac{\varphi(t)}{4t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)=-\frac{\varphi(t)}{4t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\mp 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∓</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\mp 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the equation
<math id="S7.SS6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s+1)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s-1)=\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s+1)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(s-1)=\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>
reads <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z-2)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z+2)=\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z-2)-\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}(Z+2)=\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A shift <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\mapsto Z+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\mapsto Z+c</annotation></semantics></math> multiplies Borel transforms by <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-ct}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-ct}</annotation></semantics></math>
(Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Remark 3.3. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), so the Borel transform <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{q}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{q}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S7.SS6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies
<math id="S7.SS6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e^{2t}-e^{-2t})\,\widehat{q}(t)=\varphi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e^{2t}-e^{-2t})\,\widehat{q}(t)=\varphi(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. By
(<a href="#S1.E14" title="In Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>),
<math id="S7.SS6.p9.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t=-\tfrac{1}{2}\widehat{q}(t)=-\varphi(t)/(4\sinh 2t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>sinh</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t=-\tfrac{1}{2}\widehat{q}(t)=-\varphi(t)/(4\sinh 2t)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the formula for <math id="S7.SS6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> follows from (<a href="#S1.E12" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.7. </span>The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a></h3>

<div id="S7.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 7.29. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.29</span></a>,
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> is a statement about the <math id="S7.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic valuations of
the coefficients of the explicit series <math id="S7.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Throughout, <math id="S7.SS7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{G}_{2j}=2(1-2^{2j})\mathcal{B}_{2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{G}_{2j}=2(1-2^{2j})\mathcal{B}_{2j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Genocchi
numbers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.30</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The formal exponent of Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.15</span></a>, written in the variable
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, is</span></p>
<table id="S7.E83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(83)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\frac{\widehat{\mathsf{r}}}{2}=\mathsf{b}(Z)=\sum_{j\geq 1}b_{j}\,(Z-1)^{1-2j},\qquad b_{j}=\frac{2^{2j-1}\mathfrak{G}_{2j}}{(2j)(2j-1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\frac{\widehat{\mathsf{r}}}{2}=\mathsf{b}(Z)=\sum_{j\geq 1}b_{j}\,(Z-1)^{1-2j},\qquad b_{j}=\frac{2^{2j-1}\mathfrak{G}_{2j}}{(2j)(2j-1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and coefficientwise <math id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Watson expansion of
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-(Z-1)t}\,t^{-1}\tanh t\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∫</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mi>t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>tanh</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-(Z-1)t}\,t^{-1}\tanh t\,dt</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover</span></p>
<table id="S7.E84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(84)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(b_{j})=2j-2-\nu_{2}(j)\ \geq\ 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(b_{j})=2j-2-\nu_{2}(j)\ \geq\ 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with equality only for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.Thmtheorem30.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}=\mathfrak{G}_{2}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}=\mathfrak{G}_{2}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">In (<a href="#S7.E55" title="In Lemma 7.15. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) the bracket is
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(0)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(\tfrac{1}{2})=(2-2^{-n})\mathcal{B}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(0)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(\tfrac{1}{2})=(2-2^{-n})\mathcal{B}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{m}(\tfrac{1}{2})=(2^{1-m}-1)\mathcal{B}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{m}(\tfrac{1}{2})=(2^{1-m}-1)\mathcal{B}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>; it vanishes for even <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> (odd-index
Bernoulli numbers vanish), and for <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2j-1</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient of
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{1-2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{1-2j}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(2^{2j+1}-2)\mathcal{B}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))=-\mathfrak{G}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2^{2j+1}-2)\mathcal{B}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))=-\mathfrak{G}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Substituting <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s^{-1}=-2/(Z-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{-1}=-2/(Z-1)</annotation></semantics></math> gives (<a href="#S7.E83" title="In Lemma 7.30. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">83</span></a>). For the kernel form,
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh t=\sum_{j\geq 1}2^{2j}(2^{2j}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2j}\,t^{2j-1}/(2j)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh t=\sum_{j\geq 1}2^{2j}(2^{2j}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2j}\,t^{2j-1}/(2j)!</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-(2j-2)!\,[t^{2j-1}]\tanh t=2^{2j-1}\mathfrak{G}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))=b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(2j-2)!\,[t^{2j-1}]\tanh t=2^{2j-1}\mathfrak{G}_{2j}/((2j)(2j-1))=b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and Watson’s correspondence
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z-1)^{1-2j}\leftrightarrow t^{2j-2}/(2j-2)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z-1)^{1-2j}\leftrightarrow t^{2j-2}/(2j-2)!</annotation></semantics></math> is coefficientwise exact.
For (<a href="#S7.E84" title="In Lemma 7.30. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">84</span></a>): by von Staudt–Clausen <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2j})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2j})=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{G}_{2j}=2(1-2^{2j})\mathcal{B}_{2j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔊</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{G}_{2j}=2(1-2^{2j})\mathcal{B}_{2j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an odd integer, and
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(b_{j})=(2j-1)-\nu_{2}\bigl((2j)(2j-1)\bigr)=2j-2-\nu_{2}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(b_{j})=(2j-1)-\nu_{2}\bigl((2j)(2j-1)\bigr)=2j-2-\nu_{2}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>; finally
<math id="S7.SS7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(j)\leq\log_{2}j\leq 2j-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(j)\leq\log_{2}j\leq 2j-2</annotation></semantics></math>, with equality throughout only at <math id="S7.SS7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.31</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As formal series in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E85" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(85)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{b}(Z)+\sum_{j\geq 1}b_{j}(Z+1)^{1-2j}=\log\frac{Z-1}{Z+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{b}(Z)+\sum_{j\geq 1}b_{j}(Z+1)^{1-2j}=\log\frac{Z-1}{Z+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and consequently the forcing of (<a href="#S7.E56" title="In Proposition 7.16. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>), written in
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E86" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(86)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\mathsf{F}(Z):=-\,\frac{4Z\,e^{\mathsf{b}(Z)}}{(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>𝖻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\mathsf{F}(Z):=-\,\frac{4Z\,e^{\mathsf{b}(Z)}}{(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an <em id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">even</em> series: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖥</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S7.Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem30" title="Lemma 7.30. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.30</span></a> the left side of (<a href="#S7.E85" title="In Lemma 7.31. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">85</span></a>) is the
Watson expansion of</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex106" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex106.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-Zt}\,(e^{t}+e^{-t})\,\frac{\tanh t}{t}\,dt=-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-Zt}\,\frac{2\sinh t}{t}\,dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-Zt}\,(e^{t}+e^{-t})\,\frac{\tanh t}{t}\,dt=-\int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-Zt}\,\frac{2\sinh t}{t}\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">whose coefficient series is
<math id="S7.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-2\sum_{k\geq 0}Z^{-2k-1}/(2k+1)=\log\frac{Z-1}{Z+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2\sum_{k\geq 0}Z^{-2k-1}/(2k+1)=\log\frac{Z-1}{Z+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Formula
(<a href="#S7.E86" title="In Lemma 7.31. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">86</span></a>) is the substitution <math id="S7.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=(1-Z)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=(1-Z)/2</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S7.E56" title="In Proposition 7.16. ‣ 7.3. Formal quadratures and the isolation of the oscillation ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">56</span></a>): <math id="S7.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2s-1=-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2s-1=-Z</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4s^{2}(s-1)=-(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4s^{2}(s-1)=-(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)/2</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S7.SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{r}}=2e^{\mathsf{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝗋</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>𝖻</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{r}}=2e^{\mathsf{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex107" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathsf{F}(-Z)}{\mathsf{F}(Z)}=e^{-\bigl(\mathsf{b}(Z)+\sum_{j}b_{j}(Z+1)^{1-2j}\bigr)}\cdot\frac{Z-1}{Z+1}=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝖥</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathsf{F}(-Z)}{\mathsf{F}(Z)}=e^{-\bigl(\mathsf{b}(Z)+\sum_{j}b_{j}(Z+1)^{1-2j}\bigr)}\cdot\frac{Z-1}{Z+1}=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">by (<a href="#S7.E85" title="In Lemma 7.31. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">85</span></a>), which is evenness.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.32</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Write <math id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\mathsf{b}(Z)}=\sum_{N\geq 0}u_{N}(Z-1)^{-N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>𝖻</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\mathsf{b}(Z)}=\sum_{N\geq 0}u_{N}(Z-1)^{-N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex108" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex108.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(u_{N})=-\nu_{2}(N!)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(u_{N})=-\nu_{2}(N!),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N!\,u_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N!\,u_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S7.Thmtheorem32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic unit.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding the exponential,
<math id="S7.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{N}=\sum_{\lambda}\prod_{j}b_{j}^{m_{j}}/m_{j}!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>λ</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{N}=\sum_{\lambda}\prod_{j}b_{j}^{m_{j}}/m_{j}!</annotation></semantics></math>, the sum over partitions
<math id="S7.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> into odd parts <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2j-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2j-1</annotation></semantics></math> with multiplicities <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. For
any <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, since every <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j}m_{j}!\mid\ell!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j}m_{j}!\mid\ell!</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\sum_{j}m_{j}\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\sum_{j}m_{j}\leq N</annotation></semantics></math> (multinomial
integrality),</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex109" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex109.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\Bigl(\prod_{j}b_{j}^{m_{j}}\big/\!\prod_{j}m_{j}!\Bigr)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}\Bigl(\prod_{j}m_{j}!\Bigr)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(\ell!)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(N!)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.026em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.026em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\Bigl(\prod_{j}b_{j}^{m_{j}}\big/\!\prod_{j}m_{j}!\Bigr)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}\Bigl(\prod_{j}m_{j}!\Bigr)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(\ell!)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(N!).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The all-ones partition contributes <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}^{N}/N!=(-1)^{N}/N!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}^{N}/N!=(-1)^{N}/N!</annotation></semantics></math>, of valuation
exactly <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\nu_{2}(N!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nu_{2}(N!)</annotation></semantics></math>. Every other partition has some part
<math id="S7.SS7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2j-1\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2j-1\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}m_{j}\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}m_{j}\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq\nu_{2}(b_{j})\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#S7.E84" title="In Lemma 7.30. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">84</span></a>), so its valuation is at least <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\nu_{2}(N!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\nu_{2}(N!)</annotation></semantics></math>: strictly
larger. By the ultrametric inequality the valuation of <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly
<math id="S7.SS7.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\nu_{2}(N!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\nu_{2}(N!)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS7.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N!\,u_{N}\equiv(-1)^{N}\pmod{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N!\,u_{N}\equiv(-1)^{N}\pmod{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.33</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem33.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E87" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(87)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\bigl([Z^{-(2m+2)}]\,\mathsf{F}\bigr)=2-2m+s_{2}(m)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖥</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\bigl([Z^{-(2m+2)}]\,\mathsf{F}\bigr)=2-2m+s_{2}(m).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expand the rational prefactor of (<a href="#S7.E86" title="In Lemma 7.31. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">86</span></a>) in <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex110" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex110.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{-4Z}{(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)}=-4\sum_{k\geq 0}c_{k}\,Z^{-k-2},\qquad c_{k}=[x^{k}]\,\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}(1+x)}=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1\in\mathbb{Z}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{-4Z}{(Z-1)^{2}(Z+1)}=-4\sum_{k\geq 0}c_{k}\,Z^{-k-2},\qquad c_{k}=[x^{k}]\,\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}(1+x)}=\Bigl\lfloor\frac{k}{2}\Bigr\rfloor+1\in\mathbb{Z},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and
<math id="S7.SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Z-1)^{-N}=\sum_{m\geq N}\binom{m-1}{N-1}Z^{-m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Z-1)^{-N}=\sum_{m\geq N}\binom{m-1}{N-1}Z^{-m}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, with integer
coefficients. Hence, for <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex111" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex111.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F}=-4\sum_{N=0}^{n-2}u_{N}\,M_{n,N},\qquad M_{n,N}=\sum_{m=N}^{n-2}c_{\,n-2-m}\binom{m-1}{N-1}\in\mathbb{Z}\quad(N\geq 1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝖥</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>4</mn><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F}=-4\sum_{N=0}^{n-2}u_{N}\,M_{n,N},\qquad M_{n,N}=\sum_{m=N}^{n-2}c_{\,n-2-m}\binom{m-1}{N-1}\in\mathbb{Z}\quad(N\geq 1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n,0}=c_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n,0}=c_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{n,n-2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{n,n-2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=n-2</annotation></semantics></math> contributes
<math id="S7.SS7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-4u_{n-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-4u_{n-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, of valuation exactly <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)</annotation></semantics></math> by
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem32" title="Lemma 7.32. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.32</span></a>. For <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S7.SS7.p5.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">even</em>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex112" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(u_{N}M_{n,N})\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(N!)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}((n-3)!)=-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)+\nu_{2}(n-2)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}((n-2)!)+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(u_{N}M_{n,N})\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}(N!)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}((n-3)!)=-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)+\nu_{2}(n-2)\ \geq\ -\nu_{2}((n-2)!)+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">so every other term has strictly larger valuation. Hence
<math id="S7.SS7.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}([Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F})=2-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖥</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}([Z^{-n}]\mathsf{F})=2-\nu_{2}((n-2)!)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly; with <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2m+2</annotation></semantics></math> this
is <math id="S7.SS7.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-(2m-s_{2}(2m))=2-2m+s_{2}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-(2m-s_{2}(2m))=2-2m+s_{2}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.34</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.E88" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(88)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}=-\,\frac{[Z^{-(2m+2)}]\,\mathsf{F}}{4\,(2m+1)!}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖥</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}=-\,\frac{[Z^{-(2m+2)}]\,\mathsf{F}}{4\,(2m+1)!}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and consequently
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem34.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\,\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(w_{m})=-2\,\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 7.29. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.29</span></a>, <math id="S7.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W(t)=-\varphi(t)/(4t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)=-\varphi(t)/(4t)</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S7.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)=\sum_{n}f_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)=\sum_{n}f_{n}t^{n-1}/(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math> the Borel transform of
<math id="S7.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\mathsf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖿</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝖥</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{f}}_{\Gamma}=\mathsf{F}</annotation></semantics></math>; the coefficient of <math id="S7.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S7.SS7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore <math id="S7.SS7.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-f_{2m+2}/(4(2m+1)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-f_{2m+2}/(4(2m+1)!)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
(<a href="#S7.E88" title="In Theorem 7.34. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">88</span></a>). Then Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem33" title="Theorem 7.33. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.33</span></a> and
<math id="S7.SS7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}((2m+1)!)=\nu_{2}((2m)!)=2m-s_{2}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}((2m+1)!)=\nu_{2}((2m)!)=2m-s_{2}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> give
<math id="S7.SS7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(w_{m})=\bigl(2-2m+s_{2}(m)\bigr)-2-\bigl(2m-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-2\bigl(2m-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(w_{m})=\bigl(2-2m+s_{2}(m)\bigr)-2-\bigl(2m-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-2\bigl(2m-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The passage from the <math id="S7.SS7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to the numbers <math id="S7.SS7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is
a convolution which preserves valuations. It was given
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>; we repeat the argument for completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem35" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7.35</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Put</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex113" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex113.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{d}(t)=\frac{2t}{\sinh 2t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{d}_{k}t^{2k},\qquad\mathsf{e}(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{2t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{e}_{k}t^{2k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{d}(t)=\frac{2t}{\sinh 2t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{d}_{k}t^{2k},\qquad\mathsf{e}(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{2t}=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{e}_{k}t^{2k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{d}(t)\,\mathsf{e}(t)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖽</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{d}(t)\,\mathsf{e}(t)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{d}_{0}=\mathsf{e}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{d}_{0}=\mathsf{e}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathsf{d}_{k})=\nu_{2}(\mathsf{e}_{k})=s_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathsf{d}_{k})=\nu_{2}(\mathsf{e}_{k})=s_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem35.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.SS7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{e}_{k}=2^{2k}/(2k+1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{e}_{k}=2^{2k}/(2k+1)!</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.SS7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text"> gives
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathsf{e}_{k})=2k-\bigl(2k+1-s_{2}(2k+1)\bigr)=s_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathsf{e}_{k})=2k-\bigl(2k+1-s_{2}(2k+1)\bigr)=s_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>, using
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}(2k+1)=s_{2}(k)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}(2k+1)=s_{2}(k)+1</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.SS7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expansion
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x/\sinh x=\sum_{k}(2-2^{2k})\mathcal{B}_{2k}x^{2k}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x/\sinh x=\sum_{k}(2-2^{2k})\mathcal{B}_{2k}x^{2k}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S7.SS7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=2t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=2t</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{d}_{k}=(2-2^{2k})\mathcal{B}_{2k}\,2^{2k}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{d}_{k}=(2-2^{2k})\mathcal{B}_{2k}\,2^{2k}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math id="S7.SS7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(2-2^{2k})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(2-2^{2k})=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS7.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1</annotation></semantics></math> by von Staudt–Clausen, so
<math id="S7.SS7.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathsf{d}_{k})=1-1+2k-(2k-s_{2}(k))=s_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathsf{d}_{k})=1-1+2k-(2k-s_{2}(k))=s_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem36" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.36</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; and for every
<math id="S7.Thmtheorem36.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex114" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\bigr)=-H_{r},\qquad H_{r}=r+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\bigr)=-H_{r},\qquad H_{r}=r+\nu_{2}\bigl((r-1)!\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Even indices.</em><span id="S7.SS7.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text"> By Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem31" title="Lemma 7.31. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.31</span></a> the coefficients of
the odd powers of <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{F}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish, so its Borel
transform <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is an odd function of <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. By
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem29" title="Lemma 7.29. ‣ 7.6. The asymptotic matching: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.29</span></a> the Borel transform of <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S7.SS7.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(t)/(2\sinh 2t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(t)/(2\sinh 2t)</annotation></semantics></math>, an even function, so <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{q}_{n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{q}_{n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for even
<math id="S7.SS7.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS7.p9.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=-\widehat{q}_{2k}/2=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=-\widehat{q}_{2k}/2=0</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#S1.E14" title="In Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S7.SS7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Odd indices.</em><span id="S7.SS7.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text"> Put
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{m}=[t^{2m}]\bigl(2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t\bigr)=2\operatorname{Im}g_{2m+1}/(2m)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{m}=[t^{2m}]\bigl(2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t\bigr)=2\operatorname{Im}g_{2m+1}/(2m)!</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(m)=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)=-4m+2s_{2}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(m)=-2\nu_{2}((2m)!)=-4m+2s_{2}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. By the first part,
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)/t</annotation></semantics></math> is an even series, and by
(<a href="#S1.E12" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>)</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex115" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)=\frac{2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)}{t}\;\mathsf{e}(t),\qquad\frac{2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)}{t}=W(t)\,\mathsf{d}(t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)=\frac{2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)}{t}\;\mathsf{e}(t),\qquad\frac{2\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)}{t}=W(t)\,\mathsf{d}(t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">that is, <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\mathsf{e}_{k}\,\iota_{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝖾</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\mathsf{e}_{k}\,\iota_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\mathsf{d}_{k}\,w_{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{m}=\sum_{k=0}^{m}\mathsf{d}_{k}\,w_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>,
Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem35" title="Lemma 7.35. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.35</span></a> and the subadditivity
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}(m)\leq s_{2}(m-k)+s_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}(m)\leq s_{2}(m-k)+s_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> give</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex116" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex116.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl[s_{2}(k)+\tau(m-k)\bigr]-\tau(m)=s_{2}(k)+4k+2s_{2}(m-k)-2s_{2}(m)\ \geq\ 4k-s_{2}(k)\ &gt;\ 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mn> 4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl[s_{2}(k)+\tau(m-k)\bigr]-\tau(m)=s_{2}(k)+4k+2s_{2}(m-k)-2s_{2}(m)\ \geq\ 4k-s_{2}(k)\ &gt;\ 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(w_{j})=\tau(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(w_{j})=\tau(j)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> by
Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem34" title="Theorem 7.34. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.34</span></a>, every term with <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{m}=\sum_{k}\mathsf{d}_{k}w_{m-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖽</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{m}=\sum_{k}\mathsf{d}_{k}w_{m-k}</annotation></semantics></math> has valuation strictly larger than
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(m)</annotation></semantics></math>, while the term <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.SS7.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence
<math id="S7.SS7.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\iota_{m})=\tau(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\iota_{m})=\tau(m)</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex117" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex117.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2m+1}\bigr)=\tau(m)-1+\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)=-\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)-1=-\bigl(2m+1-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-H_{m+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\bigl(\operatorname{Im}g_{2m+1}\bigr)=\tau(m)-1+\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)=-\nu_{2}\bigl((2m)!\bigr)-1=-\bigl(2m+1-s_{2}(m)\bigr)=-H_{m+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statements on <math id="S7.SS7.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> and on
<math id="S7.SS7.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a>, and the
statement on <math id="S7.SS7.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem34" title="Theorem 7.34. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.34</span></a>. By (<a href="#S1.E11" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex118" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex118.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}\bigl([A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}\bigr)=-(2r-1)-H_{r}=-E_{r}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}\bigl([A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}\bigr)=-(2r-1)-H_{r}=-E_{r}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS7.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.37</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial <math id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}\in\mathbb{Q}[A,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}\in\mathbb{Q}[A,B]</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
(<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) coincides with the constant <math id="S7.Thmtheorem37.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math> of the
phase expansion (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS7.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S7.SS7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS7.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS7.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Both sides are polynomials in <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree at most <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r-1\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r-1\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math> in
each variable: for (<a href="#S1.E7" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) this is
Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3. From half-integers to all 𝛼 ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, and <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{r}(\pi/2)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the phase
recursion as an explicit polynomial, whose degrees are read off. The
two polynomials have been evaluated in exact rational arithmetic on the
<math id="S7.SS7.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16\times 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16\times 16</annotation></semantics></math> product grid <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=i^{2}/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=i^{2}/4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=j^{2}/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=j^{2}/9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i,j\leq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i,j\leq 16</annotation></semantics></math>, on
which they agree for every <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>; since a polynomial of degree at
most <math id="S7.SS7.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> in each variable is determined by its values on such a grid,
they coincide.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>The conjugate-layer factorisation</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The double series (<a href="#S7.E36" title="In Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) admits an expansion of
Burchnall–Chaundy type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. We give a self-contained proof
by the Wilf–Zeilberger method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all parameters <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,a^{\prime},b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,a^{\prime},b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and generic <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, as an identity of formal
double series in <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S8.E89" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(89)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\mu,\nu\geq 0}\frac{(a)_{\mu}(b)_{\mu}(a^{\prime})_{\nu}(b^{\prime})_{\nu}}{(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!}x^{\mu}y^{\nu}={}\sum_{r\geq 0}\frac{(-1)^{r}(a)_{r}(b)_{r}(a^{\prime})_{r}(b^{\prime})_{r}}{r!\,(c)_{2r}\,(c+r-1)_{r}}\,(xy)^{r}\\
\times\Fhyp(a{+}r,b{+}r;c{+}2r;x)\,\Fhyp(a^{\prime}{+}r,b^{\prime}{+}r;c{+}2r;y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>{}_2F_1</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.507em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>{}_2F_1</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\mu,\nu\geq 0}\frac{(a)_{\mu}(b)_{\mu}(a^{\prime})_{\nu}(b^{\prime})_{\nu}}{(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!}x^{\mu}y^{\nu}={}\sum_{r\geq 0}\frac{(-1)^{r}(a)_{r}(b)_{r}(a^{\prime})_{r}(b^{\prime})_{r}}{r!\,(c)_{2r}\,(c+r-1)_{r}}\,(xy)^{r}\\
\times\Fhyp(a{+}r,b{+}r;c{+}2r;x)\,\Fhyp(a^{\prime}{+}r,b^{\prime}{+}r;c{+}2r;y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Comparing coefficients of <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\mu}y^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\mu}y^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> and cancelling
<math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)_{r}(a+r)_{\mu-r}=(a)_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)_{r}(a+r)_{\mu-r}=(a)_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and its three companions (the parameter
dependence cancels termwise), the claim is equivalent to the pure
identity</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.E90"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(90)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{r=0}^{\min(\mu,\nu)}t_{r}=\frac{1}{(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{r=0}^{\min(\mu,\nu)}t_{r}=\frac{1}{(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex119"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex119.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle t_{r}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{r!\,(c)_{2r}(c+r-1)_{r}\,(c+2r)_{\mu-r}(c+2r)_{\nu-r}\,(\mu-r)!\,(\nu-r)!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle t_{r}=\frac{(-1)^{r}}{r!\,(c)_{2r}(c+r-1)_{r}\,(c+2r)_{\mu-r}(c+2r)_{\nu-r}\,(\mu-r)!\,(\nu-r)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Set <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mu,r)=t_{r}\,(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mu,r)=t_{r}\,(c)_{\mu+\nu}\,\mu!\,\nu!</annotation></semantics></math>, extended to real <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(\mu-r)!=1/\Gamma(\mu-r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(\mu-r)!=1/\Gamma(\mu-r+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(\nu-r)!=1/\Gamma(\nu-r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(\nu-r)!=1/\Gamma(\nu-r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>; the
claim is <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(\mu):=\sum_{r}f(\mu,r)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(\mu):=\sum_{r}f(\mu,r)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The certificate</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex120" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex120.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(r)=-\,\frac{r\,(c+\nu+r-1)}{(c+2r-1)(\mu+1-r)},\qquad h(r)=R(r)f(\mu,r)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(r)=-\,\frac{r\,(c+\nu+r-1)}{(c+2r-1)(\mu+1-r)},\qquad h(r)=R(r)f(\mu,r),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">satisfies the rational-function identity
<math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mu+1,r)-f(\mu,r)=h(r+1)-h(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mu+1,r)-f(\mu,r)=h(r+1)-h(r)</annotation></semantics></math> (after division by <math id="S8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mu,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mu,r)</annotation></semantics></math> both sides
are rational functions of <math id="S8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and the identity is checked by clearing
denominators). Both sides are finite
at every integer <math id="S8.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>: the only delicate points are the simple zeros of
<math id="S8.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> against the simple pole of <math id="S8.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S8.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mu+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mu+1</annotation></semantics></math>, where the limits
exist and the identity holds by continuity. Summing over
<math id="S8.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq r\leq K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq r\leq K</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\min(\mu+1,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\min(\mu+1,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>, telescopes to
<math id="S8.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(\mu+1)-\Sigma(\mu)=h(K+1)-h(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(\mu+1)-\Sigma(\mu)=h(K+1)-h(0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <math id="S8.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S8.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>; and
<math id="S8.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(K+1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(K+1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>: if <math id="S8.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\leq\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\leq\mu</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S8.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1=\nu+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1=\nu+1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S8.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\Gamma(\nu-r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\Gamma(\nu-r+1)</annotation></semantics></math>
vanishes and <math id="S8.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is regular unless <math id="S8.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case the double zero
of <math id="S8.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\bigl(\Gamma(\mu-r+1)\Gamma(\nu-r+1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\bigl(\Gamma(\mu-r+1)\Gamma(\nu-r+1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> beats the simple pole;
if <math id="S8.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\geq\mu+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\geq\mu+1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S8.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+1=\mu+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+1=\mu+2</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S8.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\Gamma(\mu-r+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\Gamma(\mu-r+1)</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes and
<math id="S8.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is regular. Hence <math id="S8.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(\mu+1)=\Sigma(\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(\mu+1)=\Sigma(\mu)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Symmetry in
<math id="S8.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> completes the induction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, as formal series in <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}(A)=(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{r}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{r}=\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}\bigl((j+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-A\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}(A)=(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha)_{r}(\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha)_{r}=\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}\bigl((j+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}-A\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S8.E91" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(91)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\sum_{r\geq 0}\frac{(-1)^{r}\,\gamma_{r}(A)\,\gamma_{r}(B)}{2^{r}\,r!\,(1-Z)_{2r}\,(-Z+r)_{r}}\;\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)\;\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}(B;Z)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\alpha,\beta;Z)=\sum_{r\geq 0}\frac{(-1)^{r}\,\gamma_{r}(A)\,\gamma_{r}(B)}{2^{r}\,r!\,(1-Z)_{2r}\,(-Z+r)_{r}}\;\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)\;\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}(B;Z),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)=\Fhyp(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha+r,\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha+r;1-Z+2r;x_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>{}_2F_1</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)=\Fhyp(\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha+r,\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha+r;1-Z+2r;x_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same series at <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}=\bar{x}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}=\bar{x}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each
layer is <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Z^{-3r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Z^{-3r})</annotation></semantics></math>: with <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1/Z</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S8.E92" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(92)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(-1)^{r}\,(x_{0}y_{0})^{r}}{r!\,(1-Z)_{2r}\,(-Z+r)_{r}}=\frac{v^{3r}}{2^{r}\,r!}\,\prod_{j=1}^{2r}\frac{1}{1-jv}\;\prod_{j=r}^{2r-1}\frac{1}{1-jv}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(-1)^{r}\,(x_{0}y_{0})^{r}}{r!\,(1-Z)_{2r}\,(-Z+r)_{r}}=\frac{v^{3r}}{2^{r}\,r!}\,\prod_{j=1}^{2r}\frac{1}{1-jv}\;\prod_{j=r}^{2r-1}\frac{1}{1-jv}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 8.1. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.1</span></a> to (<a href="#S7.E36" title="In Lemma 7.2. ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">36</span></a>) with <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=\tfrac{1}{2}-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=\tfrac{1}{2}+\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}-\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}-\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}+\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}=\tfrac{1}{2}+\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1-Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1-Z</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}y_{0}=\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}y_{0}=\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c+r-1)_{r}=(-Z+r)_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c+r-1)_{r}=(-Z+r)_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Per coefficient of <math id="S8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> every
sum is finite (the monomial <math id="S8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mu,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mu,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> contributes from order <math id="S8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu+\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu+\nu</annotation></semantics></math>
on, the layer <math id="S8.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> from order <math id="S8.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3r</annotation></semantics></math> on), so the rearrangement is
legitimate <math id="S8.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-adically. For (<a href="#S8.E92" title="In Corollary 8.2. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">92</span></a>) write
<math id="S8.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-Z+j)=-Z(1-(1+j)v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-Z+j)=-Z(1-(1+j)v)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-Z+r+j)=-Z(1-(r+j)v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-Z+r+j)=-Z(1-(r+j)v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the Legendre point <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}(0)=(\tfrac{1}{2})_{r}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}(0)=(\tfrac{1}{2})_{r}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tfrac{1}{2})_{r}=(2r)!/(4^{r}r!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tfrac{1}{2})_{r}=(2r)!/(4^{r}r!)</annotation></semantics></math>, so the layer-<math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> prefactor in
(<a href="#S8.E91" title="In Corollary 8.2. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">91</span></a>) carries
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}(0)^{2}\,2^{-r}/r!=\bigl((2r)!\bigr)^{4}/\bigl(2^{\,9r}(r!)^{5}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn> 9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}(0)^{2}\,2^{-r}/r!=\bigl((2r)!\bigr)^{4}/\bigl(2^{\,9r}(r!)^{5}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
against three inverse powers of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> per layer.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.4</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Fhyp(a,1-a;c;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>{}_2F_1</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Fhyp(a,1-a;c;x)</annotation></semantics></math> is an associated Legendre function, so
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{0}(A;Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{0}(A;Z)</annotation></semantics></math> is, up to a Gamma-and-power prefactor independent of
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the Legendre function <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{Z}_{\alpha-1/2}(-i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>Z</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{Z}_{\alpha-1/2}(-i)</annotation></semantics></math> of <em id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">order</em>
<math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha-\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha-\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and its partner is the conjugate function
at <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>The transverse linearisation</h2>

<section id="S9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.1. </span>Diagonal and transverse coordinates</h3>

<div id="S9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Appell function
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\alpha,\beta;Z;x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> of Subsection <a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>, regarded
as a formal series in <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Put <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}=\frac{A+B}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}=\frac{A+B}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}=\frac{A-B}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}=\frac{A-B}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial_{A}=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{+}}+\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{-}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A}=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{+}}+\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{-}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define</p>
<table id="S9.Ex121" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex121.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\alpha}=A-\frac{1}{4},\qquad\mu_{\beta}=B-\frac{1}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\alpha}=A-\frac{1}{4},\qquad\mu_{\beta}=B-\frac{1}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{0}=\log F|_{A_{-}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{0}=\log F|_{A_{-}=0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{1}=\partial_{A_{-}}\log F|_{A_{-}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{1}=\partial_{A_{-}}\log F|_{A_{-}=0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and</p>
<table id="S9.Ex122" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex122.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{0},\quad\mathsf{v}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{0},\quad\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{v},\qquad\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1},\quad\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1},\quad\mathsf{h}=\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{0},\quad\mathsf{v}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{0},\quad\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{v},\qquad\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1},\quad\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1},\quad\mathsf{h}=\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{x}=x\partial_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}=y\partial_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>; the sans-serif
letters distinguish these fields from the midpoint constants <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1+i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1+i</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1-i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1-i</annotation></semantics></math> of Section <a href="#S2" title="2. The half-odd-integer family ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Dividing the Appell equations
(<a href="#S7.E40" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a>)–(<a href="#S7.E41" title="In Branch convention. ‣ 7.1. The Appell 𝐹_3 specialization on the physical curve ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a>) by <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> gives, before
linearisation, the logarithmic Horn system</p>
<table id="S9.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.E93"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(93)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z\mathsf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z\mathsf{u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E93.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{t}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{u}^{2}+\mathsf{u}-\mu_{\alpha}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗎𝗍</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝗎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{t}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{u}^{2}+\mathsf{u}-\mu_{\alpha}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S9.E94"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(94)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z\mathsf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z\mathsf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E94.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{t}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{v}+\mathsf{v}^{2}+\mathsf{v}-\mu_{\beta}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗏𝗍</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝗏</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{t}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{v}+\mathsf{v}^{2}+\mathsf{v}-\mu_{\beta}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">together with <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{y}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed,
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Theta_{x}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}F/F=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{u}^{2}+\mathsf{u}+\tfrac{1}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝗎</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Theta_{x}+\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}F/F=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{u}+\mathsf{u}^{2}+\mathsf{u}+\tfrac{1}{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly in
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>, so these equations are exactly equivalent to the Appell PDEs wherever
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible as a formal series (its constant term is <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>). At the
midpoint, the symmetry <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{L_{n}(A,B)}=L_{n}(B,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{L_{n}(A,B)}=L_{n}(B,A)</annotation></semantics></math> makes
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{+}}\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{+}}\log F</annotation></semantics></math> real and
<math id="S9.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{-}}\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{-}}\log F</annotation></semantics></math> purely imaginary on the diagonal <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; the diagonal part is given by
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Differentiating (<a href="#S9.E93" title="In 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S9.E94" title="In 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">94</span></a>) at <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}=0</annotation></semantics></math> gives the
exact <em id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">linear</em> forced system</span></p>
<table id="S9.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.E95"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(95)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z\mathsf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z\mathsf{p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E95.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{p}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{h}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{p}+2\mathsf{u}\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{p}-1\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗉𝗍</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗎𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{p}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{h}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{p}+2\mathsf{u}\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{p}-1\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S9.E96"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(96)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z\mathsf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z\mathsf{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S9.E96.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{q}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{h}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{q}+2\mathsf{v}\mathsf{q}+\mathsf{q}+1\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗊𝗍</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗏𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏𝗊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{q}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{h}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{q}+2\mathsf{v}\mathsf{q}+\mathsf{q}+1\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with the compatibility <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and forcing
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,-y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,-y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">At fixed <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=A_{+}+A_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=A_{+}+A_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=A_{+}-A_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=A_{+}-A_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence
<math id="S9.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\alpha}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\alpha}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\beta}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\beta}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Also</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex123" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex123.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1}=\mathsf{p},\qquad\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{v}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1}=\mathsf{q},\qquad\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{h}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝗁</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1}=\mathsf{p},\qquad\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{v}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1}=\mathsf{q},\qquad\partial_{A_{-}}\mathsf{t}=\mathsf{h}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Differentiating (<a href="#S9.E93" title="In 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>) term by term therefore gives</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex124" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{p}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{h}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{p}+2\mathsf{u}\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{p}-1\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗉𝗍</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗎𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{p}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{u}\mathsf{h}-x\bigl(\Theta_{x}\mathsf{p}+2\mathsf{u}\mathsf{p}+\mathsf{p}-1\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">because the derivative of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mu_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mu_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> equation is identical
except that <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\beta}=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A_{-}}\mu_{\beta}=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so the derivative of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\mu_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\mu_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
which yields</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex125" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex125.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{q}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{h}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{q}+2\mathsf{v}\mathsf{q}+\mathsf{q}+1\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗊𝗍</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗏𝗁</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏𝗊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathsf{h}+\mathsf{q}\mathsf{t}+\mathsf{v}\mathsf{h}-y\bigl(\Theta_{y}\mathsf{q}+2\mathsf{v}\mathsf{q}+\mathsf{q}+1\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{y}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗎</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗏</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}\mathsf{u}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> follows from commutation of
<math id="S9.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{x},\Theta_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{x},\Theta_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log F</annotation></semantics></math>; differentiating this identity in <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{-}</annotation></semantics></math>
gives <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Every occurrence of the
unknown transverse quantities is linear, while <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the fixed
diagonal background.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the class of pairs</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex126" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex126.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{p},\mathsf{q})\in Z^{-1}\,\mathbb{C}[[x,y]][[Z^{-1}]]^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗊</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{p},\mathsf{q})\in Z^{-1}\,\mathbb{C}[[x,y]][[Z^{-1}]]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfying the compatibility <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗉</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝗊</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta_{y}\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathsf{q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
system (<a href="#S9.E95" title="In Theorem 9.1. ‣ 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">95</span></a>)–(<a href="#S9.E96" title="In Theorem 9.1. ‣ 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">96</span></a>) has at most one solution for the
fixed diagonal background <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>; hence the solution obtained by differentiating
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique formal solution with zero constant term in <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Existence is already supplied by <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}=\Theta_{x}\mathcal{V}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S9.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{q}=\Theta_{y}\mathcal{V}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For uniqueness, let two such solutions be
given and denote their difference by <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{p}^{\circ},\mathsf{q}^{\circ})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{p}^{\circ},\mathsf{q}^{\circ})</annotation></semantics></math>. The forcing
terms cancel. Because <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=1+O(Z^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=1+O(Z^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}=O(Z^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝗍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}=O(Z^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose,
for contradiction, that the difference is nonzero and let <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> be the
least index for which the coefficient of <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> in either <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> or
<math id="S9.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{q}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{q}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero. Multiplication by <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> makes the left-hand
side of the corresponding difference equation have a nonzero term of order
<math id="S9.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-(n-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Every term on its right-hand side has order at least
<math id="S9.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math>: the Euler operators do not change the order in <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, multiplication
by <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> does not change it, and multiplication by <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}</annotation></semantics></math> raises it by
at least one. Thus the coefficient of <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{-(n-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{-(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> on the right is zero, a
contradiction. Hence <math id="S9.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{p}^{\circ}=\mathsf{q}^{\circ}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝗉</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝗊</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{p}^{\circ}=\mathsf{q}^{\circ}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The system (<a href="#S9.E93" title="In 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">93</span></a>)–(<a href="#S9.E94" title="In 9.1. Diagonal and transverse coordinates ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">94</span></a>) is nonlinear; its first
transverse variation is linear, with coefficients given by the diagonal
fields <math id="S9.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝗎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗏</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝗍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{u},\mathsf{v},\mathsf{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At the midpoint and at <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{+}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{+}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, write
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{1}=\sum_{n\geq 1}\mathcal{V}_{1,n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{1}=\sum_{n\geq 1}\mathcal{V}_{1,n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\mathcal{V}_{1,n}\,t^{n}/(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\mathcal{V}_{1,n}\,t^{n}/(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, with <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S1.E12" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>),</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex127" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex127.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{2t^{2}}\,\operatorname{Im}\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(t)=\frac{\sinh 2t}{2t^{2}}\,\operatorname{Im}\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">At <math id="S9.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex128" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex128.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{A}\log F=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{+}}\log F+\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{-}}\log F." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo></msub></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{A}\log F=\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{+}}\log F+\frac{1}{2}\partial_{A_{-}}\log F.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">At the midpoint the first term is real and the second is purely
imaginary. Passing coefficientwise to the Borel transforms gives
<math id="S9.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Im}\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\Phi(t)=\frac{1}{2}\operatorname{Im}\widehat{\mathcal{V}}_{1}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the formula follows from (<a href="#S1.E12" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2. </span>A Green kernel at the Legendre background</h3>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first variation of the Jacobi
polynomial itself can be written by reduction of order. Put
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=a+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=a+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=a-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=a-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{P}_{a}(x)=P^{(a,a)}_{d}(x)/P^{(a,a)}_{d}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{P}_{a}(x)=P^{(a,a)}_{d}(x)/P^{(a,a)}_{d}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S9.Ex129" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex129.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{a}=\partial_{\delta}\left[\frac{P^{(a+\delta,a-\delta)}_{d}}{P^{(a+\delta,a-\delta)}_{d}(1)}\right]_{\delta=0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>δ</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{a}=\partial_{\delta}\left[\frac{P^{(a+\delta,a-\delta)}_{d}}{P^{(a+\delta,a-\delta)}_{d}(1)}\right]_{\delta=0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Only <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta-\alpha=-2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta-\alpha=-2\delta</annotation></semantics></math> varies in the Jacobi equation, so
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{a,d}v_{a}=2\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{a,d}v_{a}=2\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{a,d}y=(1-x^{2})y^{\prime\prime}-2(a+1)xy^{\prime}+d(d+2a+1)y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{a,d}y=(1-x^{2})y^{\prime\prime}-2(a+1)xy^{\prime}+d(d+2a+1)y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{a}(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{a}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Writing
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{a}=\widetilde{P}_{a}h_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{a}=\widetilde{P}_{a}h_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and using <math id="S9.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{a,d}\widetilde{P}_{a}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{a,d}\widetilde{P}_{a}=0</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S9.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl((1-x^{2})^{a+1}\widetilde{P}_{a}^{2}h_{a}^{\prime}\bigr)^{\prime}=2(1-x^{2})^{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl((1-x^{2})^{a+1}\widetilde{P}_{a}^{2}h_{a}^{\prime}\bigr)^{\prime}=2(1-x^{2})^{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S9.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S9.Ex130"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex130.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle v_{a}(x)=-2\int_{x}^{1}G_{a,d}(x,x_{1})\,\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}(x_{1})\,dx_{1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{a}(x)=-2\int_{x}^{1}G_{a,d}(x,x_{1})\,\widetilde{P}_{a}^{\prime}(x_{1})\,dx_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S9.Ex131"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex131.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{a,d}(x,x_{1})=\widetilde{P}_{a}(x)\,(1-x_{1}^{2})^{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}(x_{1})\int_{x_{1}}^{x}\frac{dw}{(1-w^{2})^{a+1}\widetilde{P}_{a}(w)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{a,d}(x,x_{1})=\widetilde{P}_{a}(x)\,(1-x_{1}^{2})^{a}\widetilde{P}_{a}(x_{1})\int_{x_{1}}^{x}\frac{dw}{(1-w^{2})^{a+1}\widetilde{P}_{a}(w)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 9.4</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">At <math id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> the Legendre functions of the first and second
kind and <math id="S9.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{h}_{d}=\sum_{j=1}^{d}1/j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{h}_{d}=\sum_{j=1}^{d}1/j</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S9.Ex132" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex132.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{v_{0}(x)}{P_{d}(x)}=\operatorname{artanh}x-\frac{Q_{d}(x)}{P_{d}(x)}-\mathrm{h}_{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>artanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{v_{0}(x)}{P_{d}(x)}=\operatorname{artanh}x-\frac{Q_{d}(x)}{P_{d}(x)}-\mathrm{h}_{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S9.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{d}Q_{d}^{\prime}-Q_{d}P_{d}^{\prime}=(1-x^{2})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{d}Q_{d}^{\prime}-Q_{d}P_{d}^{\prime}=(1-x^{2})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S9.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"> gives
<math id="S9.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{0}/P_{d})^{\prime}=\frac{P_{d}^{2}-1}{(1-x^{2})P_{d}^{2}}=\frac{1}{1-x^{2}}-\bigl(Q_{d}/P_{d}\bigr)^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{0}/P_{d})^{\prime}=\frac{P_{d}^{2}-1}{(1-x^{2})P_{d}^{2}}=\frac{1}{1-x^{2}}-\bigl(Q_{d}/P_{d}\bigr)^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}/P_{d}=\operatorname{artanh}x-Q_{d}/P_{d}+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>artanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}/P_{d}=\operatorname{artanh}x-Q_{d}/P_{d}+c</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\to 1^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\to 1^{-}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S9.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{d}(x)=\frac{1}{2}\log\frac{2}{1-x}-\mathrm{h}_{d}+o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{d}(x)=\frac{1}{2}\log\frac{2}{1-x}-\mathrm{h}_{d}+o(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S9.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{artanh}x=\frac{1}{2}\log\frac{2}{1-x}+o(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>artanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{artanh}x=\frac{1}{2}\log\frac{2}{1-x}+o(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{d}(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{d}(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>; hence
<math id="S9.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}/P_{d}\to\mathrm{h}_{d}+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}/P_{d}\to\mathrm{h}_{d}+c</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math> forces <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=-\mathrm{h}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=-\mathrm{h}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 9.5</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S9.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 9.4. ‣ 9.2. A Green kernel at the Legendre background ‣ 9. The transverse linearisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9.4</span></a> does not give the numbers <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. With
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=a+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=a+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=a-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=a-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-B=4a\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-B=4a\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, so
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{\delta}(A-B)|_{a=0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>δ</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{\delta}(A-B)|_{a=0}=0</annotation></semantics></math>: the first <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-variation at <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> does not
move <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A-B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A-B</annotation></semantics></math> at all. Since <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{r}=(A-B)\,\widetilde{\kappa}_{r}(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{r}=(A-B)\,\widetilde{\kappa}_{r}(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> by
(<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S9.Ex133" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex133.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}=\widetilde{\kappa}_{r}(0,0)=\tfrac{1}{4}\,\partial_{a}\partial_{\delta}\,\kappa_{r}(a+\delta,a-\delta)\big|_{a=\delta=0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>δ</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[A^{1}B^{0}]\kappa_{r}=\widetilde{\kappa}_{r}(0,0)=\tfrac{1}{4}\,\partial_{a}\partial_{\delta}\,\kappa_{r}(a+\delta,a-\delta)\big|_{a=\delta=0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">a <em id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed</em> derivative. Moreover the expansion must be taken with
<math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}=d+a+\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}=d+a+\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> held fixed, not with the degree <math id="S9.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> held fixed; the two
differ by the variation of the eigenvalue.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The layer expansion of Corollary <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 8.2. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.2</span></a> expresses the series
<math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n}g_{n}Z^{-n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a quotient of two explicit series. In this section
we determine the <math id="S10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-adic valuations of the coefficients of the
numerator and of the denominator, using Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Write <math id="S10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}^{\prime}=\partial_{A}\mathcal{P}_{r}|_{A=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><msub><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}^{\prime}=\partial_{A}\mathcal{P}_{r}|_{A=0}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by
<math id="S10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{r}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{r}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> the coefficient of
<math id="S10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}(A)\gamma_{r}(B)\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}(B;Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}(A)\gamma_{r}(B)\mathcal{P}_{r}(A;Z)\overline{\mathcal{P}}_{r}(B;Z)</annotation></semantics></math>
in (<a href="#S8.E91" title="In Corollary 8.2. ‣ 8. The conjugate-layer factorisation ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">91</span></a>), and set, at the Legendre point <math id="S10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=B=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=B=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S10.E97" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(97)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{r\geq 0}\varrho_{r}\,\bigl[\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr]^{\prime}\,\overline{\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}},\qquad\operatorname{Den}=\sum_{r\geq 0}\varrho_{r}\,\bigl(\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr)\overline{\bigl(\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Num</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Den</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{r\geq 0}\varrho_{r}\,\bigl[\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr]^{\prime}\,\overline{\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}},\qquad\operatorname{Den}=\sum_{r\geq 0}\varrho_{r}\,\bigl(\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr)\overline{\bigl(\gamma_{r}\mathcal{P}_{r}\bigr)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the bar denotes the series at <math id="S10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (coefficientwise
conjugation). Both <math id="S10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> are polynomials in <math id="S10.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
coefficientwise, so the derivative is taken term by term. Then,
coefficientwise in <math id="S10.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1/Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1/Z</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S10.E98" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(98)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}v^{n}=\partial_{A}\log F\big|_{A=B=0}=\frac{\operatorname{Num}}{\operatorname{Den}},\qquad\operatorname{Im}g_{n}=\bigl[v^{n}\bigr]\frac{\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}}{\operatorname{Den}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Num</mi><mi>Den</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow><mi>Den</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 1}g_{n}v^{n}=\partial_{A}\log F\big|_{A=B=0}=\frac{\operatorname{Num}}{\operatorname{Den}},\qquad\operatorname{Im}g_{n}=\bigl[v^{n}\bigr]\frac{\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}}{\operatorname{Den}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p2.3" class="ltx_p">the last equality because <math id="S10.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Den}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Den</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Den}</annotation></semantics></math> has real coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 10.1</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S10.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Den}=F(0,0;Z)=\exp\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Den</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Den}=F(0,0;Z)=\exp\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where, by
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> at <math id="S10.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex134" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex134.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda(v)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\lambda_{n}\,v^{n},\qquad\lambda_{n}=L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}\big|_{\alpha=0}=\frac{4^{n}}{n(n+1)}\Bigl[2\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)\Bigr]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>diag</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda(v)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\lambda_{n}\,v^{n},\qquad\lambda_{n}=L_{n}^{\mathrm{diag}}\big|_{\alpha=0}=\frac{4^{n}}{n(n+1)}\Bigl[2\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{3}{4}\bigr)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}(1)-\mathcal{B}_{n+1}\bigl(\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)\Bigr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We write</p>
<table id="S10.Ex135" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex135.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Den}^{\pm 1}=\exp(\pm\Lambda)=\sum_{n\geq 0}d_{n}^{\pm}v^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Den</mi><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Den}^{\pm 1}=\exp(\pm\Lambda)=\sum_{n\geq 0}d_{n}^{\pm}v^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.2</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex136" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex136.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2k}=\frac{\mathsf{E}_{2k}}{4k},\qquad\lambda_{2k-1}=-\frac{(2^{2k}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2k}}{(2k)(2k-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2k}=\frac{\mathsf{E}_{2k}}{4k},\qquad\lambda_{2k-1}=-\frac{(2^{2k}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2k}}{(2k)(2k-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex137" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex137.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\lambda_{2k})=\nu_{2}(\lambda_{2k-1})=-2-\nu_{2}(k);" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\lambda_{2k})=\nu_{2}(\lambda_{2k-1})=-2-\nu_{2}(k);</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in particular <math id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{1}=-1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=-1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\lambda_{n})\geq-n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\lambda_{n})\geq-n</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S10.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>(iii),</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex138" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex138.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon_{0}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1\right)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{\lambda_{n}}{(n-1)!}t^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon_{0}(t)=\frac{1}{2}\left(\operatorname{sech}t-\tanh\frac{t}{2}-1\right)=\sum_{n\geq 1}\frac{\lambda_{n}}{(n-1)!}t^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Using
<math id="S10.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{sech}t=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{E}_{2k}t^{2k}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sech</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝖤</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{sech}t=\sum_{k\geq 0}\mathsf{E}_{2k}t^{2k}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S10.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh\frac{t}{2}=\sum_{k\geq 1}2(2^{2k}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2k}t^{2k-1}/(2k)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh\frac{t}{2}=\sum_{k\geq 1}2(2^{2k}-1)\mathcal{B}_{2k}t^{2k-1}/(2k)!</annotation></semantics></math> gives the
displayed formulas. Euler numbers are odd, von Staudt–Clausen gives
<math id="S10.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(\mathcal{B}_{2k})=-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S10.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2k}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2k}-1</annotation></semantics></math> is odd; thus both coefficients
have valuation <math id="S10.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2-\nu_{2}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2-\nu_{2}(k)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S10.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> the inequality
<math id="S10.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2-\nu_{2}(k)\geq-2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2-\nu_{2}(k)\geq-2k</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S10.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+\nu_{2}(k)\leq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+\nu_{2}(k)\leq 2k</annotation></semantics></math>; for
<math id="S10.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k-1\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k-1\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> it is <math id="S10.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2+\nu_{2}(k)\leq 2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2+\nu_{2}(k)\leq 2k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Both hold because
<math id="S10.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(k)\leq\log_{2}k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(k)\leq\log_{2}k</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10.3</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex139" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex139.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(d_{n}^{\pm})=-2n-\nu_{2}(n!)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(d_{n}^{\pm})=-2n-\nu_{2}(n!).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently, <math id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(d_{2k}^{\pm})=-\bigl(5k+\nu_{2}(k!)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(d_{2k}^{\pm})=-\bigl(5k+\nu_{2}(k!)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(d_{2k+1}^{\pm})=-\bigl(5k+2+\nu_{2}(k!)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(d_{2k+1}^{\pm})=-\bigl(5k+2+\nu_{2}(k!)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expand the exponential by partitions,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex140" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex140.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{n}^{\pm}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1},\ldots,m_{n}\geq 0\\
\sum_{j=1}^{n}jm_{j}=n\end{subarray}}\prod_{j=1}^{n}\frac{(\pm\lambda_{j})^{m_{j}}}{m_{j}!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{n}^{\pm}=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}m_{1},\ldots,m_{n}\geq 0\\
\sum_{j=1}^{n}jm_{j}=n\end{subarray}}\prod_{j=1}^{n}\frac{(\pm\lambda_{j})^{m_{j}}}{m_{j}!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The partition <math id="S10.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S10.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, contributes
<math id="S10.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pm\lambda_{1})^{n}/n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pm\lambda_{1})^{n}/n!</annotation></semantics></math>, of valuation <math id="S10.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2n-\nu_{2}(n!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2n-\nu_{2}(n!)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider any other partition and put
<math id="S10.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\sum_{j\geq 2}jm_{j}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\sum_{j\geq 2}jm_{j}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S10.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1}=n-M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1}=n-M</annotation></semantics></math>. Lemma <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 10.2. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>
gives</span></p>
<table id="S10.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex141"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex141.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{2}\!\left(\prod_{j}\frac{\lambda_{j}^{m_{j}}}{m_{j}!}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>j</mi></munder></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{2}\!\left(\prod_{j}\frac{\lambda_{j}^{m_{j}}}{m_{j}!}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S10.Ex141.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq-2m_{1}-\sum_{j\geq 2}jm_{j}-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!)=-2n+M-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq-2m_{1}-\sum_{j\geq 2}jm_{j}-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!)=-2n+M-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">The difference with the valuation of the first partition is at least</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex142" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex142.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M+\nu_{2}(n!)-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!)&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M+\nu_{2}(n!)-\sum_{j}\nu_{2}(m_{j}!)&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">because <math id="S10.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j}m_{j}!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j}m_{j}!</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S10.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sum_{j}m_{j})!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sum_{j}m_{j})!</annotation></semantics></math>, which divides <math id="S10.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n!</annotation></semantics></math>, while
<math id="S10.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus exactly one term has minimal valuation, and
<math id="S10.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}(d_{n}^{\pm})=-2n-\nu_{2}(n!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}(d_{n}^{\pm})=-2n-\nu_{2}(n!)</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally
<math id="S10.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}((2k)!)=k+\nu_{2}(k!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}((2k)!)=k+\nu_{2}(k!)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S10.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2}((2k+1)!)=\nu_{2}((2k)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2}((2k+1)!)=\nu_{2}((2k)!)</annotation></semantics></math> give the two other formulas.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.4</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S10.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex143"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex143.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{2}\bigl([v^{2r-1}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\bigr)=-\bigl(5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{2}\bigl([v^{2r-1}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\bigr)=-\bigl(5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.Ex144"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex144.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{2}\bigl([v^{2m}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\bigr)=-\bigl(5m-2+\nu_{2}((m-1)!)\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{2}\bigl([v^{2m}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\bigr)=-\bigl(5m-2+\nu_{2}((m-1)!)\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">By (<a href="#S10.E98" title="In 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">98</span></a>) and Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a>, in which
<math id="S10.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2k}=0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex145" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex145.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\operatorname{Den}\cdot\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Im}g_{n}\,v^{n}=\Bigl(\sum_{n\geq 0}d_{n}^{+}v^{n}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{k\geq 1}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\,v^{2k-1}\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Den</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\operatorname{Den}\cdot\sum_{n\geq 1}\operatorname{Im}g_{n}\,v^{n}=\Bigl(\sum_{n\geq 0}d_{n}^{+}v^{n}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{k\geq 1}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\,v^{2k-1}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">so that</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex146" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex146.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v^{2r-1}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{k=1}^{r}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\;d_{2(r-k)}^{+}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v^{2r-1}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{k=1}^{r}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\;d_{2(r-k)}^{+}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorems <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a> and <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 10.3. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a>, the term <math id="S10.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> has
valuation
<math id="S10.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-H_{k}-5(r-k)-\nu_{2}((r-k)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-H_{k}-5(r-k)-\nu_{2}((r-k)!)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}=k+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}=k+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S10.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>
this is <math id="S10.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-(5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!))</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S10.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> it exceeds that
value by</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex147" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex147.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(k-1)+\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1}&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(k-1)+\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">Hence the term <math id="S10.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> is the only one of minimal valuation, which gives
the first formula. Similarly,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex148" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex148.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[v^{2m}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\;d_{2(m-k)+1}^{+}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[v^{2m}]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}=\sum_{k=1}^{m}\operatorname{Im}g_{2k-1}\;d_{2(m-k)+1}^{+},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">where the term <math id="S10.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> has valuation
<math id="S10.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-H_{k}-5(m-k)-2-\nu_{2}((m-k)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-H_{k}-5(m-k)-2-\nu_{2}((m-k)!)</annotation></semantics></math>; for <math id="S10.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> this is
<math id="S10.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-(5m-2+\nu_{2}((m-1)!))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(5m-2+\nu_{2}((m-1)!))</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S10.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> it exceeds that value by
<math id="S10.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(k-1)+\nu_{2}\binom{m-1}{k-1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(k-1)+\nu_{2}\binom{m-1}{k-1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.5</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq 2r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq 2r-1</annotation></semantics></math> put</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex149" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex149.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r,j}:=2^{\,5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\;\bigl[v^{j}\bigr]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\;\cdot\;\bigl[v^{2r-1-j}\bigr]\operatorname{Den}^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn> 5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0.502em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Den</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r,j}:=2^{\,5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\;\bigl[v^{j}\bigr]\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\;\cdot\;\bigl[v^{2r-1-j}\bigr]\operatorname{Den}^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r,j}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r,j}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S10.Ex150"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex150.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{2}(c_{r,2k-1})=\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1}\qquad(1\leq k\leq r)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{2}(c_{r,2k-1})=\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1}\qquad(1\leq k\leq r),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S10.Ex151"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex151.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu_{2}(c_{r,2k})=1+\nu_{2}\!\left((r-k)\binom{r-1}{k-1}\right)\qquad(1\leq k\leq r-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu_{2}(c_{r,2k})=1+\nu_{2}\!\left((r-k)\binom{r-1}{k-1}\right)\qquad(1\leq k\leq r-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="S10.E99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(99)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.E99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{2r-1}c_{r,j}=2^{\,5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\ \in\ 2^{4(r-1)}\,\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn> 5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mn> 2</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{2r-1}c_{r,j}=2^{\,5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}\ \in\ 2^{4(r-1)}\,\mathbb{Z}_{2}^{\times}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S10.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The two valuation formulas follow from Theorem <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 10.3. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a> and
Proposition <a href="#S10.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 10.4. ‣ 10. The tangent from the layers, and four valuation formulas ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.4</span></a> by addition of exponents: for <math id="S10.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=2k-1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.Ex152" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex152.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-\bigl(5k-4+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)\bigr)-\bigl(5(r-k)+\nu_{2}((r-k)!)\bigr)=\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-\bigl(5k-4+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)\bigr)-\bigl(5(r-k)+\nu_{2}((r-k)!)\bigr)=\nu_{2}\binom{r-1}{k-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">and for <math id="S10.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=2k</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S10.p7.3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-\bigl(5k-2+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)\bigr)-\bigl(5(r-k-1)+2+\nu_{2}((r-k-1)!)\bigr)\\
=1+\nu_{2}\!\left((r-k)\binom{r-1}{k-1}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:0.388em;" width="0.388em"><mi>ν</mi></mpadded><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-\bigl(5k-2+\nu_{2}((k-1)!)\bigr)-\bigl(5(r-k-1)+2+\nu_{2}((r-k-1)!)\bigr)\\
=1+\nu_{2}\!\left((r-k)\binom{r-1}{k-1}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S10.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}=[v^{2r-1}]\bigl(\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\cdot\operatorname{Den}^{-1}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>Den</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}=[v^{2r-1}]\bigl(\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\cdot\operatorname{Den}^{-1}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, the sum of the <math id="S10.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S10.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)}\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose valuation is
<math id="S10.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-H_{r}=4(r-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5r-4+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)-H_{r}=4(r-1)</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem36" title="Theorem 7.36. ‣ 7.7. The arithmetic step: proof of Theorem ‣ 7. Off the diagonal: the transverse variation and the dyadic law ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.36</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S10.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 10.6</span></span><span id="S10.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The individual terms <math id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{r,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{r,j}</annotation></semantics></math> have small valuations (at most of the
order of <math id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}r</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas their sum is divisible by exactly
<math id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{4(r-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{4(r-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The valuation of <math id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}g_{2r-1}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore
cannot be read off from the individual terms of the convolution
<math id="S10.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\cdot\operatorname{Den}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Num</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>Den</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\operatorname{Num}\cdot\operatorname{Den}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11. </span>Application: Jacobi zeros for <math id="S11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this section <math id="S11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta&gt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta&gt;-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math>
has <math id="S11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> real simple zeros in <math id="S11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, ordered as in the Introduction.
The purpose of the section is to illustrate that the phase expansion
(<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) can be evaluated in exact arithmetic, with an
accuracy away from the endpoints limited only by the truncation order.
It is not an optimised algorithm, and no timing comparison with the
specialised Gauss–Jacobi solvers
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> is intended.
For related asymptotic analyses of Jacobi polynomials and their zeros,
see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S1.E5" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) the phase constants vanish for <math id="S11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, so
the Legendre and Gegenbauer cases need none; for <math id="S11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\neq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\neq\beta</annotation></semantics></math> they
are needed. Their denominators are known: by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>,
<math id="S11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{E_{r}}\kappa_{r}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}[\mu_{\alpha},\mu_{\beta}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{E_{r}}\kappa_{r}\in\mathbb{Z}_{2}[\mu_{\alpha},\mu_{\beta}]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{r}=3r-1+\nu_{2}((r-1)!)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the
odd part of <math id="S11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\den\kappa_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>den</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\den\kappa_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> divides
<math id="S11.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{lcm}(1,3,\dots,2r-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{lcm}(1,3,\dots,2r-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The denominators of
<math id="S11.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{1},\dots,\kappa_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{1},\dots,\kappa_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> as polynomials in <math id="S11.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,B)</annotation></semantics></math> are
<math id="S11.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4=2^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4=2^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="96=2^{5}\!\cdot\!3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>96</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">96=2^{5}\!\cdot\!3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7680=2^{9}\!\cdot\!15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7680</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>9</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7680=2^{9}\!\cdot\!15</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S11.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86016=2^{12}\!\cdot\!21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86016</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86016=2^{12}\!\cdot\!21</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Solving (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) by Newton with a per-node least-term gate (the
truncation order is the last index before the correction terms stop
decreasing), then polishing by a safeguarded Newton on <math id="S11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math>
confined to the cell bounded by the midpoints of the neighbouring reference
angles, gives the following maximum errors in <math id="S11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The reference
zeros are independent of the predictor: they are obtained from
<math id="S11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math> itself in <math id="S11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>-digit multiprecision arithmetic,
and each tabulated error is measured against that reference. In the
table, “bare” is the leading approximation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ The ultraspherical background of the Jacobi phase expansion, in closed form" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) (no
correction terms) over all nodes <math id="S11.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>; “gated” is the
gated Newton solution of <math id="S11.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(\theta)=(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(\theta)=(k-\frac{1}{2})\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, over all nodes
or over the bulk; “polished” is the result after the final Newton
step on <math id="S11.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}^{(\alpha,\beta)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S11.p4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S11.p4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><math id="S11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><math id="S11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><math id="S11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S11.p4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">bare</span></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S11.p4.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">gated, all <math id="S11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S11.p4.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">gated, bulk</span></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S11.p4.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">polished</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S11.p4.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S11.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S11.p4.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.2</annotation></semantics></math></th>
